                        <div class="main-content" data-nosnippet>
                            <section data-title="Introduction"><div class="c-article-section" id="Sec1-section"><h2 id="Sec1" class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item"><span class="c-article-section__title-number">1 </span>Introduction</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Sec1-content"><p>Convexity testing for an arbitrary function may be non-trivial in some cases. One widely used test for convexity is to check the function’s Hessian. A continuous, twice-differentiable function is convex if its Hessian is positive semidefinite everywhere in interior of the convex set. For details we refer to the monographs [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 53" title="D.S. Mitrinović, J. Pečarić, A.M. Fink, Classical and New Inequalities in Analysis (Kluwer, Dordrecht, 1993)" href="#ref-CR53" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e626">53</a>] and [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 65" title="A.W. Roberts, D.E. Varberg, Convex Functions (Academic Press, London, 1973)" href="#ref-CR65" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e629">65</a>].</p><p>The aim of the present chapter is to build several convex and concave functions (some of them defined on convex subsets of linear spaces) starting from some elementary results of the theory of convex functions. Direct checking of the convexity of the functions resulted from our constructions by computing the Hessian matrix is in the great majority of the cases a very difficult task. One of the methods used for generating convex functions is based on the composition of a convex function of several variables which is separately monotone with convex and concave functions.</p><p>Let <i>n</i> ≥ 2 be a natural number, <i>I</i> and <i>J</i> be two intervals of the real axis, and <i>ϕ</i> : <i>I</i> → <i>J</i> be a bijective function. We suppose that <i>J</i> = [0, <i>∞</i>) or <i>J</i> = (0, <i>∞</i>). Consider the function <i>S</i>
<sub><i>ϕ</i></sub> : <i>I</i>
<sup><i>n</i></sup> → <i>I</i> </p><div id="Equa" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} S_{\phi }\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) =\phi ^{-1}\left( \phi \left( x_{1}\right) +\phi \left( x_{2}\right) +\ldots+\phi \left( x_{n}\right) \right) ,\ \ \ \left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) \in I^{n} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> We shall call <i>S</i>
<sub><i>ϕ</i></sub> the symmetric Archimedean function generated by the function <i>ϕ</i>. The symmetric Archimedean functions occur in the study of Copula functions which describe the dependence structure between random variables with arbitrary marginal distribution functions. Copula Theory is a chapter of Probability Theory.</p><p>Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(D=\left \{ \left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in I^{n}:\phi \left ( x_{1}\right ) &gt;\phi \left ( x_{2}\right ) +\ldots +\phi \left ( x_{n}\right ) \right \} \)</span> and define <i>A</i>
<sub><i>ϕ</i></sub> : <i>D</i> → <i>I</i> as follows: </p><div id="Equb" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} A_{\phi }\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) =\phi ^{-1}\left( \phi \left( x_{1}\right) -\phi \left( x_{2}\right) -\ldots-\phi \left( x_{n}\right) \right) , \left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right)\,{\in}\,D. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> <i>A</i>
<sub><i>ϕ</i></sub> will be called the asymmetric Archimedean function generated by the function <i>ϕ</i>.</p><p>The structure of the chapter is as follows. The second section contains statements of several well-known results on the composition of convex and quasi-convex functions. These results help us to build convex, quasi-convex, concave, and quasi-concave functions. The third section is dedicated to the convexity study of symmetric Archimedean functions. Several corollaries are given. In the fourth section is considered the asymmetric Archimedean function. A classical example of such a function is the Bellman function </p><div id="Equc" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{p}\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) =\left( x_{1}^{p}-x_{2}^{p}-\ldots-x_{n}^{p}\right) ^{\frac{1}{p}},\ \left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) \in D_{p} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> where </p><div id="Equd" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} D_{p}=\left\{ \left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) \in \mathbb{R}^{n}:x_{i}\geq 0,\ \ i=1,2,\ldots,n,\ \ \ x_{1}^{p}\geq x_{2}^{p}+\ldots+x_{n}^{p}\right\} \ \text{if }p&gt;0 \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
<p>and </p><div id="Eque" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} D_{p}=\left\{ \left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) \in \mathbb{R}^{n}:x_{i}&gt;0,\ \ i=1,2,\ldots,n,\ \ \ x_{1}^{p}&gt;x_{2}^{p}+\ldots+x_{n}^{p}\right\} \ \text{if }p&lt;0 \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
<p>In 1957 Bellman [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 5" title="R. Bellman, On an inequality concerning an indefinite form. Am. Math. Mon. 63, 108–109 (1957)" href="#ref-CR5" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e2203">5</a>] proved that <i>f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is concave if <i>p</i> ∈ [1, <i>∞</i>). Several references on Bellman function can be found in Losonczi and Pales [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 43" title="L. Losonczi, Z. Pales, Inequalities for indefinite forms. J. Math. Anal. Appl. 205, 148–156 (1997)" href="#ref-CR43" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e2220">43</a>]. In the fourth section are studied convexity properties of the asymmetric Archimedean function. The fifth section is dedicated to the study of convexity/concavity of symmetric polynomials. In the sixth section a new proof of Chandler–Davis theorem is given. Starting from symmetric convex functions defined on finite dimensional spaces we build several convex functions of hermitian matrices. The seventh section is dedicated to a generalization of Muirhead’s theorem and to some applications of it. The eighth section is dedicated to the construction of convex functions based on Taylor remainder series.</p></div></div></section><section data-title="Convexity of Composite Functions"><div class="c-article-section" id="Sec2-section"><h2 id="Sec2" class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item"><span class="c-article-section__title-number">2 </span>Convexity of Composite Functions</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Sec2-content"><p>In this section we shall recall several basic results on the convexity and quasi-convexity of composite functions. Let <i>E</i> be a linear space over <span class="mathjax-tex">\( \mathbb {R} \)</span> and <i>D</i> be a convex subset of <i>E</i>.</p>
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar1">Theorem 2.1</h3>
              <p><i>Let I be an interval of the real axis, u </i>:<i> D </i>→<i> I,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(g:I\rightarrow \mathbb {R} ,\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f\left ( x\right ) =g\left ( u\left ( x\right ) \right ) ,\)</span> <i>x </i>∈<i> D</i>. <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i> </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">10</span>

<p><i>If g is increasing and convex, u is convex, then f is convex.</i>
</p>

</li>
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">20</span>

<p><i>If g is increasing and concave, u is concave, then f is concave.</i>
</p>

</li>
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">30</span>

<p><i>If g is decreasing and convex, u is concave, then f is convex.</i>
</p>

</li>
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">40</span>

<p><i>If g is decreasing and concave, u is convex, then f is concave.</i>
</p>

</li>
</ol>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar2">Corollary 2.2</h3>
              <p>
                <i>Let</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(u:D\rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span>
                <i>. Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">10</span>
                    
                      <p>
                        <i>If u is convex on D, then e</i>
                        <sup><i>u</i></sup>
                        <i>is convex.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">20</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If u </i>&gt; 0 <i>on D is concave, then</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\ln \left ( u\right ) \)</span> <i>is concave and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\frac {1}{u}\)</span> <i>is convex.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">30.</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If u </i>&gt; 0 <i>on D is concave and p </i>∈ (0, 1]<i>, then u</i>
<sup><i>p</i></sup> <i>is concave.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">40.</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If u </i>&gt; 0 <i>on D is convex and p </i>∈ [1, <i>∞</i>)<i>, then u</i>
<sup><i>p</i></sup> <i>is convex.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar3">Theorem 2.3</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 1 <i>be a natural number, I</i>
<sub><i>k</i></sub> , <i>k </i>= 1, 2, …, <i>n be intervals of the real axis,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(J=\left \{ 1,2,\ldots ,n\right \} ,\ J_{1}\subset J,\ J_{2}=J_{1}.\)</span> <i>Consider the functions</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(g:I_{1}\times I_{2}\times \ldots \times I_{n}\rightarrow \mathbb {R} ,\ u_{k}:D\rightarrow I_{k},\ k=1,2,\ldots ,n,\ \ f:D\rightarrow \mathbb {R} ,\ \)</span> </p><div id="Equ1" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f\left( x\right) =g\left( u_{1}\left( x\right) ,u_{2}\left( x\right) ,\ldots,u_{n}\left( x\right) \right) ,\ \ \ x\in D. \end{aligned} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (2.1)
                </div></div>

              <p>
                <i>Suppose that the following conditions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">(i)</span>
                    
                      <p><i>g is monotone increasing in the i-th variable for every i </i>∈<i> J</i>
<sub>1</sub>.</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">(ii)</span>
                    
                      <p><i>g is monotone decreasing in the i-th variable for every i </i>∈<i> J</i>
<sub>2</sub>.</p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
              </p>
              
                <ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">10.</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If g is convex, u</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> <i>is convex for every i </i>∈<i> J</i>
<sub>1</sub>, <i>u</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> <i>is concave for every i </i>∈<i> J</i>
<sub>2</sub>, <i>, then f is convex.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">20.</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If g is concave, u</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> <i>is convex for every i </i>∈<i> J</i>
<sub>2</sub>, <i>u</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> <i>is concave for every i </i>∈<i> J</i>
<sub>1</sub>, <i>, then f is concave.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar4">Theorem 2.4</h3>
              <p><i>Let I be an interval of the real axis, u </i>:<i> D </i>→<i> I,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(g:I\rightarrow \mathbb {R} ,\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f\left ( x\right ) =g\left ( u\left ( x\right ) \right ) ,\)</span> <i>x </i>∈<i> D</i>. <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i> </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">10</span>

<p><i>If g is increasing and u is quasi-convex, then f is quasi-convex.</i>
</p>

</li>
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">20</span>

<p><i>If g is increasing and u is quasi-concave, then f is quasi-concave.</i>
</p>

</li>
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">30</span>

<p><i>If g is decreasing and u is quasi-concave, then f is quasi-convex.</i>
</p>

</li>
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">40</span>

<p><i>If g is decreasing and u is quasi-convex, then f is quasi-concave.</i>
</p>

</li>
</ol>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar5">Theorem 2.5</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 1 <i>be a natural number, I</i>
<sub><i>k</i></sub> , <i>k </i>= 1, 2, …, <i>n be intervals of the real axis,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(J=\left \{ 1,2,\ldots ,n\right \} ,\ J_{1}\subset J,\ J_{2}=J_{1}.\)</span><i>Consider the functions</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(g:I_{1}\times I_{2}\times \ldots \times I_{n}\rightarrow \mathbb {R} ,\ u_{k}:D\rightarrow I_{k},\ k=1,2,\ldots ,n,\ \ f:D\rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span> <i>defined as in</i> (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ1">2.1</a>).</p>
              <p>
                <i>Suppose that the following conditions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">(i)</span>
                    
                      <p><i>g is monotone increasing in the i-th variable for every i </i>∈<i> J</i>
<sub>1</sub>.</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">(ii)</span>
                    
                      <p><i>g is monotone decreasing in the i-th variable for every i </i>∈<i> J</i>
<sub>2</sub>.</p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">10.</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If u</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> <i>is quasi-convex for every i </i>∈<i> J</i>
<sub>1</sub>, <i>u</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> <i>is quasi-concave for every i </i>∈<i> J</i>
<sub>2</sub>, <i>, then f is quasi-convex.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">20.</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If u</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> <i>is quasi-convex for every i </i>∈<i> J</i>
<sub>2</sub>, <i>u</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> <i>is quasi-concave for every i </i>∈<i> J</i>
<sub>1</sub>, <i>, then f is quasi-concave.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            </div></div></section><section data-title="A Study of Convexity of Symmetric Archimedean Functions"><div class="c-article-section" id="Sec3-section"><h2 id="Sec3" class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item"><span class="c-article-section__title-number">3 </span>A Study of Convexity of Symmetric Archimedean Functions</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Sec3-content"><p>Symmetric Archimedean functions appear in the theory of Archimedean copulas [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 78" title="L. Tibiletti, Quasi-concavity property of multivariate distribution functions. Ratio Math. 9, 27–36 (1995)" href="#ref-CR78" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e3765">78</a>] and in the theory of Archimedean <i>t</i>-norms [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 35" title="E.P. Klement, R. Mesiar, E. Pap, Generated triangular norms. Kybernetika, 36(3), 363–377 (2000)" href="#ref-CR35" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e3771">35</a>] and [<a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-27407-8">79</a>
]. In probability theory and statistics, a copula is a multivariate probability distribution for which the marginal-probability distribution of each variable is uniform. Copulas are used to describe the dependence between random variables. Archimedean copulas is an important class of copulas—because of the ease with which they can be constructed and the nice properties they possess. In this section we shall investigate the convexity, quasi-convexity, concavity, and quasi-concavity of the symmetric Archimedean functions</p>
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar6">Theorem 3.1</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2 <i>be a natural number, I</i>, <i>J be two intervals of the real axis, and ϕ </i>:<i> I </i>→<i> J be a two times differentiable bijective function. Denote by I</i>
<sub>1</sub> <i>(resp. J</i>
<sub>1</sub><i>) the interior of I (resp. J). Suppose that J </i>= [0, <i>∞</i>) <i>or J </i>= (0, <i>∞</i>) <i>and ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) ≠ 0<i>, ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) ≠ 0 <i>for every t </i>∈<i> I</i>
<sub>1</sub><i>. Consider the functions</i> </p><div id="Equf" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} g_{n}:I^{n}\rightarrow I,\ g_{n}(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})=\phi ^{-1}(\phi (x_{1})+\phi (x_{2})+\ldots +\phi (x_{n})), \\ x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\in I, \\ v:J_{1}\rightarrow J_{1},\ v(t)=\displaystyle\frac{\phi ^{\prime 2}(\phi ^{-1}(t))}{\phi ^{\prime \prime -1}(t))},\ t\in J_{1}. \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">1∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) &gt; 0, <i>ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) &gt; 0 <i>for every t </i>∈<i> I</i>
<sub>1</sub><i>,</i> </p><div id="Equg" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v(t+s)\ge v(t)+v(s),\ t,s\in J_1, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

                      <p><i>then g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> <i>is convex on I</i>
<sup><i>n</i></sup>.</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">2∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) &gt; 0, <i>ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) &lt; 0 <i>for every t </i>∈<i> I</i>
<sub>1</sub><i>,</i> </p><div id="Equh" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v(t+s)\ge v(t)+v(s),\ t,s\in J_1, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

                      <p><i>then g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> <i>is concave on I</i>
<sup><i>n</i></sup>.</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">3∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) &lt; 0, <i>ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) &gt; 0 <i>for every t </i>∈<i> I</i>
<sub>1</sub><i>,</i> </p><div id="Equi" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v(t+s)\le v(t)+v(s),\ t,s\in J_1, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

                      <p><i>then g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> <i>is concave on I</i>
<sup><i>n</i></sup>.</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">4∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) &lt; 0, <i>ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) &lt; 0 <i>for every t </i>∈<i> I</i>
<sub>1</sub><i>,</i> </p><div id="Equj" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v(t+s)\le v(t)+v(s),\ t,s\in J_1, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

                      <p><i>then g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> <i>is convex on I</i>
<sup><i>n</i></sup>.</p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>We shall prove the assertions in the case <i>n</i> = 2. Denote <i>g</i> = <i>g</i>
<sub>2</sub>. Thus </p><div id="Equk" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g(x,y)=\phi ^{-1}(\phi (x)+\phi (y)),\ x,y\in I_1. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Taking partial differentials in the equality </p><div id="Equl" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \phi (g(x,y))=\phi (x)+\phi (y) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> we obtain: </p><div id="Equm" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} \phi ^{\prime }(g(x,y))g^{\prime }_x(x,y)=\phi ^{\prime }(x) \\ \phi ^{\prime }(g(x,y))g^{\prime }_y(x,y)=\phi ^{\prime }(y) \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div><p> hence since <i>ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>)≠0 for every <i>t</i> ∈ <i>I</i>
<sub>1</sub> we have </p><div id="Equn" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g^{\prime }_x(x,y)=\displaystyle\frac{\phi ^{\prime }(x)}{\phi ^{\prime }(g(x,y))},\ g^{\prime }_y(x,y)=\displaystyle\frac{\phi ^{\prime }(y)}{\phi ^{\prime }(g(x,y))}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Further computations give us: </p><div id="Equo" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g^{\prime \prime }_{xx}(x,y)=\displaystyle\frac{\phi ^{\prime 2}(g(x,y))\phi ^{\prime \prime }(x)-\phi ^{\prime 2}(x)\phi ^{\prime \prime }(g(x,y))} { \phi ^{\prime 3}(g(x,y))} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equp" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g^{\prime \prime }_{xx}(x,y)=\displaystyle\frac{\phi ^{\prime \prime }(x)[v(\phi (x)+\phi (y))-v(\phi (x))]} {\phi ^{\prime }(g(x,y))v(\phi (x)+\phi (y))} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equq" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g^{\prime \prime }_{yy}(x,y)=\displaystyle\frac{\phi ^{\prime \prime }(y)[v(\phi (x)+\phi (y))-v(\phi (y))]} {\phi ^{\prime }(g(x,y))v(\phi (x)+\phi (y))} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equr" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g^{\prime \prime }_{xy}(x,y)=-\displaystyle\frac{\phi ^{\prime }(x)\phi ^{\prime }(y)}{\phi ^{\prime }(g(x,y))v(\phi (x)+\phi (y))}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Denote by <i>H</i>(<i>x</i>, <i>y</i>) the determinant of the Hessian matrix of <i>g</i>. Note that </p><div id="Equs" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} H(x,y)=g^{\prime \prime }_{xx}(x,y)g^{\prime \prime }_{yy}(x,y)-[g^{\prime \prime }_{xy}(x,y)]^2 \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equt" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =\displaystyle\frac{\phi ^{\prime \prime }(x)\phi ^{\prime \prime }(y)[v(\phi (x)+\phi (y))-v(\phi (x))-v(\phi (y))]} {\phi ^{\prime 2}(g(x,y))v(\phi (x)+\phi (y))}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> From conditions on <i>ϕ</i> from assertion 1<sup>∘</sup> it follows that </p><div id="Equu" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g^{\prime \prime }_{xx}\ge 0,\ g^{\prime \prime }_{yy}\ge 0,\ H\ge 0 \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> on <span class="mathjax-tex">\(I_1^2\)</span>, hence <i>g</i> is convex. One can easily check that the other assertions hold in the case <i>n</i> = 2.</p>
              <p>We return to the study of the case <i>n</i> ≥ 2. Note that </p><div id="Equv" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_n(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)=g_{n-1}(g_2(x_1,x_2),x_3,x_3,\ldots ,x_n) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> and <i>g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> is separately increasing, that is it is increasing in each variable.</p>
              <p>Suppose that <i>g</i>
<sub>2</sub> is convex. Then from equation </p><div id="Equ2" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_3(x_1,x_2,x_3)=g_2(g_2(x_1,x_2),x_3) {} \end{aligned} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (3.2)
                </div></div><p> it follows that <i>g</i>
<sub>3</sub> is convex. By induction we obtain that <i>g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> is convex for every <i>n</i> ≥ 2.</p>
              <p>Suppose now that <i>g</i>
<sub>2</sub> is concave. Then from Eq. (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ2">3.2</a>) and from Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar3">2.3</a> it follows that <i>g</i>
<sub>3</sub> is concave. By induction we obtain that <i>g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> is concave for every <i>n</i> ≥ 2. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar7">Corollary 3.2</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 1<i>, J</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> = [0, <i>∞</i>) <i>if p </i>∈ (0, <i>∞</i>) <i>and J</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> = (0, <i>∞</i>) <i>if p </i>∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0)<i>. For every</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \mathbb {R}^*=\mathbb {R}- \{0\}\)</span> <i>consider the functions:</i> </p><div id="Equ3" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} f_p:J_p^n\to J_p,\ f_p(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n) =\left(\sum_{i=1}^n x_i^p\right)^{1/p}, {}\\ (x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in J_p^n \end{gathered} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (3.3)
                </div></div>

              <p>
                <i>and</i>
                </p><div id="Equ4" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} g:\mathbb{R}^n\to \mathbb{R},\ g(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)=\ln(e^{x_1}+e^{x_2}+\ldots +e^{x_n}), {} \\ (x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in \mathbb{R}^n.  \end{gathered} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (3.4)
                </div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">1∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If p </i>∈ [1, <i>∞</i>)<i>, then f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> <i>is convex on</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(J_p^n\)</span>.</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">2∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If p </i>∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0) ∪ (0, 1]<i>, then f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> <i>is concave on</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\( J_p^n\)</span>.</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">3∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>g is convex on</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbb {R}^n\)</span>.</p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>For every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \mathbb {R}^*\)</span> consider the function </p><div id="Equw" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \phi _p:J_p\to J_p,\ \phi _p(t)=t^p,\ t\in J_p. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that <i>ϕ</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is bijective, </p><div id="Equx" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} \phi _p^{-1}(t)=t^{1/p},\ \phi _p^{-1}(t)=t^{1/p},\ \phi ^{\prime }_p(t)=pt^{p-1}, \\ \phi ^{\prime \prime }_p(t)=p(p-1)t^{p-2},\ t\in J_p. \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div><p> For every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \mathbb {R}^*-\{1\}\)</span> we have </p><div id="Equy" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v_p(t)=\displaystyle\frac{\phi ^{\prime 2}_p(\phi _p^{-1}(t))}{\phi ^{\prime \prime }_p(\phi _p^{-1}(t))} =\displaystyle\frac{\phi ^{\prime 2}_p(t^{1/p}) }{\phi ^{\prime \prime }_p(t^{1/p})} =\displaystyle\frac{p^2(t^{1/p})^{p-2}}{ p(p-1)(t^{1/p})^{p-2}}=\displaystyle\frac{p}{p-1}t,\ t\in J_p. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that </p><div id="Equz" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v_p(t+s)=v_p(t)+v_p(s),\ s,t\in J_p,\ p\in \mathbb{R}^*-\{1\}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>If <i>p</i> = 1, then <i>f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is convex and concave.</p>
              <p>If <i>p</i> ∈ (1, <i>∞</i>), then <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\phi _{p}^{\prime }(t)&gt;0\)</span>, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\phi _{p}^{\prime \prime }(t)&gt;0\)</span>, <i>t</i> ∈ <i>J</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub>. By assertion 1<sup>∘</sup> of Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar6">3.1</a> it follows that <i>f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is convex.</p>
              <p>If <i>p</i> ∈ (0, 1), then <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\phi _{p}^{\prime }(t)&gt;0\)</span>, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\phi _{p}^{\prime \prime }(t)&lt;0\)</span>, <i>t</i> ∈ <i>J</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub>. By assertion 3<sup>∘</sup> of Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar6">3.1</a> it follows that <i>f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is concave.</p>
              <p>If <i>p</i> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0), then <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\phi ^{\prime }_p(t)&lt;0\)</span>, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\phi ^{\prime \prime }_p(t)&gt;0\)</span>, <i>t</i> ∈ <i>J</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub>. By assertion 2<sup>∘</sup> of Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar6">3.1</a> it follows that <i>f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is concave.</p>
              <p>Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\phi :\mathbb {R}\to (0,\infty )\)</span>, <i>ϕ</i>(<i>t</i>) = <i>e</i>
<sup><i>t</i></sup>, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(t\in \mathbb {R}\)</span>. Note that <i>ϕ</i> is bijective and we have </p><div id="Equaa" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} \phi ^{\prime }(t)&gt;0,\ \phi ^{\prime \prime }(t)&gt;0, \\ v(t)=\displaystyle\frac{\phi ^{\prime 2}(\phi ^{-1}(t))}{\phi ^{\prime \prime -1}(t))}=t,\ t\in \mathbb{R}. \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div><p> By assertion 1<sup>∘</sup> of Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar6">3.1</a> it follows that <i>g</i> is convex on <span class="mathjax-tex">\( \mathbb {R}^n\)</span>. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar8">Corollary 3.3</h3>
              <p><i>Let J</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> = [0, <i>∞</i>) <i>if p </i>∈ (0, <i>∞</i>) <i>and J</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> = (0, <i>∞</i>) <i>if p </i>∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0)<i>. Suppose that E is a linear space and D is a convex subset of E. For every</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \mathbb {R}^*\)</span> <i>consider the functions u</i>
<sub><i>k</i>,<i>p</i></sub> :<i> D </i>→<i> J</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub><i>, k </i>= 1, 2, …, <i>n, and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\( f_p:J_p^n\to \mathbb {R}\)</span><i>,</i> </p><div id="Equab" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_p(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)=(u_{1,p}^p(x)+u_{2,p}^p(x)+\ldots +u_{n,p}^p(x))^{1/p},\ x\in D. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p><i>Let</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(u_i:D\to \mathbb {R}\)</span><i>, i </i>= 1, 2, …, <i>n, and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(g:D\to \mathbb {R},\)</span> </p><div id="Equac" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g(x)=\ln(e^{u_1(x)}+e^{u_2(x)}+\ldots +e^{u_n(x)}),\ x\in D. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">1∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If p </i>∈ [1, <i>∞</i>)<i>, u</i>
<sub><i>i</i>,<i>p</i></sub> <i>are convex for i </i>= 1, 2, …, <i>n, then f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> <i>is convex.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">2∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If p </i>∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0) ∪ (0, 1)<i>, u</i>
<sub><i>i</i>,<i>p</i></sub> <i>are concave for every i </i>= 1, 2, …, <i>n, then f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> <i>is concave.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">3∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If u</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> <i>are convex for i </i>= 1, 2, …, <i>n, then g is convex.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>The validity of the assertions follows at once from the preceding corollary and from Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar3">2.3</a>. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar9">Corollary 3.4</h3>
              <p><i>For every p</i>, <i>p</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> ∈ (0, <i>∞</i>)<i>, i </i>= 1, 2, …, <i>n, consider the functions</i> </p><div id="Equad" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} g_{p}(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})=\left( \sum_{i=1}^{n}|x_{i}|{}^{p_{i}}\right) ^{1/p},\ (x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in \mathbb{R}^{n} \\ h_{p}(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})=\left( \sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{p_{i}}\right) ^{1/p},\ (x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in \lbrack 0,\infty )^{n}. \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div>

              <p><i>For every p</i>, <i>p</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0) <i>consider the function</i> </p><div id="Equae" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} w_{p}(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})=\left( \sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{p_{i}}\right) ^{1/p},\ (x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in (0,\infty )^{n}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>Let</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p_{1},p_{2},\ldots ,p_{n}\in \mathbb {R}^{\ast }\)</span>
                <i>. Denote</i>
                </p><div id="Equaf" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} a=\min_{1\leq i\leq n}(p_{i}),\ b=\max_{1\leq i\leq n}(p_{i}). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">1∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If p</i>
<sub>1</sub>, <i>p</i>
<sub>2</sub>, …, <i>p</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> ∈ [1, <i>∞</i>)<i>, p </i>∈ (0, <i>a</i>]<i>, then g</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> <i>is convex.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">2∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If p</i>
<sub>1</sub>, <i>p</i>
<sub>2</sub>, …, <i>p</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> ∈ (0, 1]<i>, p </i>∈ [<i>b</i>, <i>∞</i>)<i>, then h</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> <i>is concave.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">3∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If p</i>
<sub>1</sub>, <i>p</i>
<sub>2</sub>, …, <i>p</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0)<i>, p </i>∈ (−<i>∞</i>, <i>a</i>]<i>, then w</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> <i>is concave.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <i>f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> be the function defined by (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ3">3.3</a>). Suppose that hypotheses of assertion 1<sup>∘</sup> hold. Note that <i>a</i> ∈ [1, <i>∞</i>). By Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar7">3.2</a> it follows that <i>f</i>
<sub><i>a</i></sub> is convex. For every <i>i</i> = 1, 2, …, <i>n</i> let </p><div id="Equag" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u_{i}:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow \mathbb{R},\ u_{i}(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})=|x_{i}|{}^{p_{i}/a},\ (x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in \mathbb{R}^{n}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Consider the function </p><div id="Equah" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u:\mathbb{R}^{n}\rightarrow \mathbb{R}^{n},\ u(x)=(u_{1}(x),u_{2}(x),\ldots ,u_{n}(x)),\ x\in \mathbb{R}^{n}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that </p><div id="Equai" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{p}(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})=\left( \sum_{i=1}^{n}|x_{i}|{}^{p_{i}}\right) ^{1/p}=\left( \sum_{i=1}^{n}[u_{i}(x)]^{a}\right) ^{1/p}=\left[ f_{a}(u(x)) \right] ^{a/p}, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equaj" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} x=(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in \mathbb{R}^{n}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Since all <i>u</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> are convex and <i>f</i>
<sub><i>a</i></sub> is convex, by Theorem 3.2 it follows that <i>f</i>
<sub><i>a</i></sub> ∘ <i>u</i> is convex. Since <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\displaystyle \frac {a}{p}\geq 1\)</span> it follows that <i>g</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is convex.</p>
              <p>Suppose that hypotheses of assertion 2<sup>∘</sup> hold. Note that <i>b</i> ∈ (0, 1]. By Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar7">3.2</a>, function <i>f</i>
<sub><i>b</i></sub> is concave. For every <i>i</i> = 1, 2, …, <i>n</i> let </p><div id="Equak" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v_i(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)=x_i^{p_i/b},\ (x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in [0,\infty )^n. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Consider the function </p><div id="Equal" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v(x)=(v_1(x),v_2(x),\ldots ,v_n(x)),\ x\in [0,\infty )^n. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that all <i>v</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> are concave and </p><div id="Equam" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} h_p(x)=\left(\sum_{i=1}^n [v_i(x)]^b\right)^{1/p} =[f_b(v(x))]^{b/p},\ x\in [0,\infty )^n. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By Theorem 3.2 the function <i>f</i>
<sub><i>b</i></sub> ∘ <i>v</i> is concave. Since <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\displaystyle \frac {b}{p}\le 1\)</span> it follows that <i>h</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is concave. Suppose that hypotheses of assertion 3<sup>∘</sup> hold. By Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar7">3.2</a> the function <i>f</i>
<sub><i>a</i></sub> is concave. Since <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\displaystyle \frac {p_i}{a}\le 1\)</span> it follows that <i>u</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> are concave on (0, <i>∞</i>)<sup><i>n</i></sup>. Note that </p><div id="Equan" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} w_p(x)=[f_a(u(x))]^{a/p},\ x\in (0,\infty )^n. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By Theorem 3.2 the function <i>f</i>
<sub><i>a</i></sub> ∘ <i>u</i> is concave on (0, <i>∞</i>)<sup><i>n</i></sup>. Since <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\displaystyle \frac {a}{p}\le 1\)</span> it follows that <i>w</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is concave. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar10">Corollary 3.5</h3>
              <p><i>Let p</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> &gt; 0<i>, i </i>= 1, 2, …, <i>n. Then the function</i> </p><div id="Equao" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} f:(0,\infty )^n\to \mathbb{R},\ f(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)=\ln (x_1^{-p_1}+x_2^{-p_2}+\ldots +x_n^{-p_n}), \\ (x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in (0,\infty )^n \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>is convex.</i>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <i>g</i> be the function defined by (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ4">3.4</a>). For every <i>i</i> = 1, 2, …, <i>n</i> consider the function </p><div id="Equap" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u_i(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)=-p_i\ln (x_i),\ (x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in (0,\infty )^n. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that </p><div id="Equaq" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x)=g(u_1(x),u_2(x),\ldots ,u_n(x)),\ x\in (0,\infty )^n \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> and all <i>u</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> are convex.</p>
              <p>Since <i>g</i> is convex it follows from Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar8">3.3</a> that <i>f</i> is convex. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar11">Corollary 3.6</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 1 <i>and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(P:\mathbb {R}^n\to \mathbb {R }\)</span> <i>be a polynomial function with positive coefficients. Then the function</i> </p><div id="Equar" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)=\ln(P(e^{x_1},e^{x_2},\ldots ,e^{x_n})),\ (x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in \mathbb{R}^n\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>is convex.</i>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\alpha _{k}=(\alpha _{k1},\alpha _{k2},\ldots ,\alpha _{kn})\in \mathbb {N}^{n}\)</span>, <i>a</i>
<sub><i>k</i></sub> &gt; 0, <i>k</i> = 1, 2, …, <i>m</i> and </p><div id="Equas" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} P(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})=\sum_{k=1}^{m}a_{k}x_{1}^{\alpha _{k1}}x_{2}^{\alpha _{k2}}\ldots x_{n}^{\alpha _{kn}}.\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div><p> If </p><div id="Equat" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} u_{k}(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})=\alpha _{k1}x_{1}+\alpha _{k2}x_{2}+\ldots +\alpha _{kn}x_{n}+\ln (a_{k}),\\ (x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in \mathbb{R}^{n} \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div><p> then </p><div id="Equau" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})=\ln [P(e^{x_{1}},e^{x_{2}},\ldots ,e^{x_{n}})] \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equav" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =\ln \left[ \sum_{k=1}^{m}a_{k}\exp \left( \sum_{i=1}^{n}\alpha _{ki}x_{i}\right) \right] =\ln \left[ \sum_{k=1}^{m}\exp (u_{k}(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}))\right] . \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> If <i>g</i> is the function defined by (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ4">3.4</a>), then </p><div id="Equaw" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x)=g(u_{1}(x),u_{2}(x),\ldots ,u_{n}(x)),\ x\in \mathbb{R}^{n}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Since <i>g</i> is convex and all <i>u</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> are convex, by Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar3">2.3</a> it follows that <i>f</i> is also convex. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar12">Lemma 3.7</h3>
              <p><i>Let I</i>, <i>J be two intervals of the real axis, ϕ </i>:<i> I </i>→<i> J be a bijective, two times differentiable function. Suppose that ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>)≠0<i>, ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>)≠0 <i>for every t </i>∈<i> I. Consider the functions</i> </p><div id="Equax" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u:I\to \mathbb{R},\ u(t)=\displaystyle\frac{1}{\phi ^{\prime }(t)},\ t\in I \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              
                <div id="Equay" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v(t)=\displaystyle\frac{\phi ^{\prime 2}(\phi ^{-1}(t))}{\phi ^{\prime \prime -1}(t))},\ t\in J. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">1∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If u is convex, then v </i>∘<i> ϕ is increasing.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">2∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If u is concave, then v </i>∘<i> ϕ is decreasing.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Note that </p><div id="Equaz" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u^{\prime }(t)=-\displaystyle\frac{1}{v(\phi (t))},\ t\in I. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> If <i>u</i> is convex, then <i>u</i>
<sup><i>′</i></sup> is increasing, hence <i>v</i> ∘ <i>ϕ</i> is increasing.</p>
              <p>If <i>u</i> is concave, then <i>u</i>
<sup><i>′</i></sup> is decreasing, hence <i>v</i> ∘ <i>ϕ</i> is decreasing. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar13">Theorem 3.8</h3>
              <p><i>Let I</i>, <i>I</i>
<sub>1</sub>, <i>J</i>, <i>J</i>
<sub>1</sub>, <i>ϕ be as in the statement of Theorem</i> <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar6">3.1</a><i>. Suppose that</i> </p><div id="Equba" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \phi ^{\prime }(t)\ne 0,\ \phi ^{\prime \prime }(t)\ne 0,\ t\in I_1. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>Consider the functions</i>
                </p><div id="Equ5" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_n(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)=\phi ^{-1}(\phi (x_1)+\phi (x_2)+\ldots +\phi (x_n)),\ x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n\in I {} \end{aligned} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (3.5)
                </div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equbb" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u:I_1\to \mathbb{R},\ u(t)=\displaystyle\frac{1}{\phi ^{\prime }(t)},\ t\in I_1 \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equbc" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v:J_1\to J_1,\ v(t)=\displaystyle\frac{\phi ^{\prime 2}(\phi ^{-1}(t))}{\phi ^{\prime \prime -1}(t))},\ t\in J_1. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">1∘</span>
                    
                      <p>
                        <i>If u is convex on I</i>
                        <sub>1</sub>
                        <i>, then g</i>
                        <sub>
                          <i>n</i>
                        </sub>
                        <i>is separately convex (i.e. g</i>
                        <sub>
                          <i>n</i>
                        </sub>
                        <i>is convex in each variable).</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">2∘</span>
                    
                      <p>
                        <i>If u is concave on I</i>
                        <sub>1</sub>
                        <i>, then g</i>
                        <sub>
                          <i>n</i>
                        </sub>
                        <i>is separately concave (i.e. g</i>
                        <sub>
                          <i>n</i>
                        </sub>
                        <i>is concave in each variable).</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Note that for <i>i</i> = 1, 2, …, <i>n</i> we have </p><div id="Equbd" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \displaystyle\frac{\partial g_n}{\partial x_i}(x)=\displaystyle\frac{\phi ^{\prime }(x_i)}{\phi ^{\prime }(g_n(x))}, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Eqube" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \displaystyle\frac{\partial g_n}{\partial x_i^2}(x) =\displaystyle\frac{\phi ^{\prime \prime }(x_i)\phi ^{\prime }(g_n(x))-\phi ^{\prime 2}(x_i)\phi ^{\prime \prime }(g_n(x_i))} {\phi ^{\prime 3}(g_n(x))} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equbf" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =\displaystyle\frac{\phi ^{\prime \prime }(g_n(x))\phi ^{\prime \prime }(x_i)[v(\phi (g_n(x)))-v(\phi (x_i))]} {\phi ^{\prime 3}(g_n(x))}, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equbg" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} x=(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in I_1^n. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Suppose that <i>u</i> is convex. By Lemma <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar12">3.7</a> it follows that <i>v</i> ∘ <i>ϕ</i> is increasing.</p>
              <p>If <i>ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′</i></sup> &gt; 0 on <i>I</i>
<sub>1</sub>, then <i>ϕ</i>
<sup>−1</sup> is increasing hence <i>v</i> is increasing. Note that </p><div id="Equ6" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \displaystyle\frac{\partial^2 g_n}{\partial x_i^2}\ge 0\mbox{ on } I_1^n. {} \end{aligned} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (3.6)
                </div></div>

              <p>If <i>ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′</i></sup> &lt; 0 on <i>I</i>
<sub>1</sub>, then <i>ϕ</i>
<sup>−1</sup> is decreasing, hence <i>v</i> is decreasing. Note that (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ6">3.6</a>) holds again. We proved that <i>g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> is separately convex on <span class="mathjax-tex">\(I_1^n\)</span>. Since <i>g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> is continuous it follows that <i>g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> is separately convex on <i>I</i>
<sup><i>n</i></sup>.</p>
              <p>Assertion 2<sup>∘</sup> follows with a similar argument. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar14">Theorem 3.9</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2 <i>be a natural number, I</i>, <i>J be two intervals of the real axis. Suppose that J </i>= [0, <i>∞</i>) <i>or J </i>= (0, <i>∞</i>)<i>. Let ϕ </i>:<i> I </i>→<i> J be a bijective continuous function and g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> :<i> I</i>
<sup><i>n</i></sup> →<i> I be the function defined by</i> (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ5">3.5</a>)<i>. Then the following assertions hold:</i> </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">1∘</span>

<p><i>If ϕ is increasing and convex, then g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> <i>is quasi-convex.</i>
</p>

</li>
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">2∘</span>

<p><i>If ϕ is increasing and concave, then g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> <i>is quasi-concave.</i>
</p>

</li>
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">3∘</span>

<p><i>If ϕ is decreasing and convex, then g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> <i>is quasi-concave.</i>
</p>

</li>
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">4∘</span>

<p><i>If ϕ is decreasing and concave, then g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> <i>is quasi-convex.</i>
</p>

</li>
</ol>

            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let </p><div id="Equbh" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \psi _n(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)=\phi (x_1)+\phi (x_2)+\ldots +\phi (x_n),\ x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n\in I. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that we have <i>g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> = <i>ϕ</i>
<sup>−1</sup> ∘ <i>ψ</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub>.</p>
              <p>In order to prove assertion 1<sup>∘</sup> suppose that <i>ϕ</i> is increasing and convex. Then <i>ψ</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> is convex and <i>ϕ</i>
<sup>−1</sup> is increasing. By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar4">2.4</a> it follows that <i>g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> is quasi-convex.</p>
              <p>The other assertions can be proved by a similar argument. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar15">Theorem 3.10</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2 <i>be a natural number, I</i>, <i>J be two intervals of the real axis, and ϕ </i>:<i> I </i>→<i> J be a bijective differentiable function. Consider the function g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> :<i> I</i>
<sup><i>n</i></sup> →<i> I defined by</i> (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ5">3.5</a>)<i>. Suppose that ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>)≠0 <i>for every t </i>∈<i> I. Then the following assertions hold:</i> </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">1∘</span>

<p><i>If ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′</i></sup> &gt; 0 <i>on I and ϕ is convex, then g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> <i>is Schur-convex.</i>
</p>

</li>
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">2∘</span>

<p><i>If ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′</i></sup> &lt; 0 <i>on I and ϕ is convex, then g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> <i>is Schur-concave.</i>
</p>

</li>
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">3∘</span>

<p><i>If ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′</i></sup> &gt; 0 <i>on I and ϕ is concave, then g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> <i>is Schur-concave.</i>
</p>

</li>
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">4∘</span>

<p><i>If ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′</i></sup> &lt; 0 <i>on I and ϕ is concave, then g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> <i>is Schur-convex.</i>
</p>

</li>
</ol>

            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Note that <i>g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> is symmetric. In order to decide the Schur-convexity or Schur-concavity of <i>g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> we have to study the sign of </p><div id="Equbi" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} A(x)=(x_1-x_2)\left(\displaystyle\frac{\partial g_n}{\partial x_1}(x)- \displaystyle\frac{\partial g_n}{\partial x_2}(x)\right),\ x=(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in I^n. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that </p><div id="Equbj" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} A(x)=\displaystyle\frac{(x_1-x_2)(\phi ^{\prime }(x_1)-\phi ^{\prime }(x_2)) }{\phi ^{\prime }(g_n(x))},\ x=(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in I^n. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> All the assertions from the statement of the theorem follow at once by computing the sign of <i>A</i>(<i>x</i>). □</p>
            </div></div></section><section data-title="A Study of Convexity of Asymmetric Archimedean Functions"><div class="c-article-section" id="Sec4-section"><h2 id="Sec4" class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item"><span class="c-article-section__title-number">4 </span>A Study of Convexity of Asymmetric Archimedean Functions</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Sec4-content">
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar16">Theorem 4.1</h3>
              <p><i>Let I</i>, <i>J be two intervals of the real axis and ϕ </i>:<i> I </i>→<i> J be a differentiable bijective function. Denote by I</i>
<sub>1</sub> <i>(resp. J</i>
<sub>1</sub><i>) the interior of I (resp. J). Suppose that J </i>= [0, <i>∞</i>) <i>or J </i>= (0, <i>∞</i>) <i>and ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) ≠ 0<i>, ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) ≠ 0 <i>for every t </i>∈<i> I</i>
<sub>1</sub><i>. Let v </i>:<i> J</i>
<sub>1</sub> →<i> J</i>
<sub>1</sub><i>,</i> </p><div id="Equ7" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v(t)=\displaystyle\frac{\phi ^{\prime 2}(\phi ^{-1}(t))}{\phi ^{\prime \prime -1}(t))},\ t\in J_{1}{} \end{aligned} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (4.7)
                </div></div>

              
                <div id="Equbk" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} D=\{(x,y)\in I^{2}:\ \phi (x)&gt;\phi (y)\}, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equbl" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g:D\rightarrow I,\ g(x,y)=\phi ^{-1}(\phi (x)-\phi (y)),\ (x,y)\in D. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">1∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) &gt; 0 <i>, ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) &gt; 0 <i>for every t </i>∈<i> I</i>
<sub>1</sub> <i>and</i> </p><div id="Equbm" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v(t+s)\geq v(t)+v(s),\ t,s\in J_{1} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

                      <p>
                        <i>then g is concave on D.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">2∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) &gt; 0, <i>ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) &lt; 0 <i>for every t </i>∈<i> I</i>
<sub>1</sub> <i>and</i> </p><div id="Equbn" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v(t+s)\geq v(t)+v(s),\ t,s\in J_{1} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

                      <p>
                        <i>then g is convex on D.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">3∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) &lt; 0, <i>ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) &gt; 0 <i>for every t </i>∈<i> I</i>
<sub>1</sub> <i>and</i> </p><div id="Equbo" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v(t+s)\leq v(t)+v(s),\ t,s\in J_{1} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

                      <p>
                        <i>then g is convex on D.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">4∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) &lt; 0<i>, ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) &lt; 0 <i>for every t </i>∈<i> I</i>
<sub>1</sub> <i>and</i> </p><div id="Equbp" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v(t+s)\leq v(t)+v(s),\ t,s\in J_{1} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

                      <p>
                        <i>then g is concave on D.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Note that <i>D</i> is a convex subset of <i>I</i>
<sup>2</sup>, </p><div id="Equbq" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \phi (g(x,y))=\phi (x)-\phi (y),\ v(\phi (x))=\displaystyle\frac{\phi ^{\prime 2}(x)}{\phi ^{\prime \prime }(x)} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equbr" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{x}^{\prime }(x,y)=\displaystyle\frac{\phi ^{\prime }(x)}{\phi ^{\prime }(g(x,y))},\ g_{y}^{\prime }(x,y)=-\displaystyle\frac{\phi ^{\prime }(y)}{ \phi ^{\prime }(g(x,y))} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equbs" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{xx}^{\prime \prime }(x,y)=\displaystyle\frac{\phi ^{\prime \prime }(x)(v(\phi (x)-\phi (y))-v(\phi (x)))}{\phi ^{\prime }(g(x,y))v(\phi (x)-\phi (y))} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equbt" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{yy}^{\prime \prime }(x,y)=-\displaystyle\frac{\phi ^{\prime \prime }(y)(v(\phi (y))+v(\phi (x)-\phi (y)))}{\phi ^{\prime }(g(x,y))v(\phi (x)-\phi (y))} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equbu" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{xy}^{\prime \prime }(x,y)=\displaystyle\frac{\phi ^{\prime }(x)\phi ^{\prime }(y)}{\phi ^{\prime }(g(x,y))v(\phi (x)-\phi (y))} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>Let <i>H</i>
<sub><i>g</i></sub> be the determinant of the Hessian matrix of <i>g</i>. Note that </p><div id="Equbv" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} H_{g}(x,y)=g_{xx}^{\prime \prime }(x,y)g_{yy}^{\prime \prime }(x,y)-[g_{xy}^{\prime \prime }(x,y)]^{2} \\ =\frac{\phi ^{\prime \prime }(x)\phi ^{\prime \prime }(y)[v(\phi (x))-v(\phi (x)-\phi (y))][v(\phi (y))+v(\phi (x)-\phi (y))]-\phi ^{\prime 2}(x)\phi ^{\prime 2}(y)}{\phi ^{\prime 2}(g(x,y))v^{2}(\phi (x)-\phi (y))} \\ =\displaystyle\frac{\phi ^{\prime \prime }(x)\phi ^{\prime \prime }(y)[v(\phi (x))-v(\phi (y))-v(\phi (x)-\phi (y))]}{\phi ^{\prime 2}(g(x,y))v(\phi (x)-\phi (y))} \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div>

              <p>A direct check shows that assertions 1<sup>∘</sup>–4<sup>∘</sup> hold. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar17">Theorem 4.2</h3>
              <p><i>Let I</i>, <i>J</i>, <i>I</i>
<sub>1</sub>, <i>J</i>
<sub>1</sub>, <i>ϕ</i>, <i>v be defined as in the statement of Theorem</i> <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar16">4.1</a><i>. For every natural number n </i>≥ 2 <i>let</i> </p><div id="Equbw" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} D=\{(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in I^n\mid \phi (x_1)&gt;\phi (x_2)+\ldots + \phi (x_n)\}\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>

              <p><i>and g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> :<i> D </i>→<i> I,</i> </p><div id="Equbx" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_n(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)=\phi ^{-1}(\phi (x_1)-\phi (x_2) -\ldots -\phi (x_n)),\ (x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in D.\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>If U is a convex subset of D, then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">1∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) &gt; 0, <i>ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) &gt; 0 <i>for t </i>∈<i> I</i>
<sub>1</sub> <i>and</i> </p><div id="Equby" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v(t+s)\geq v(t)+v(s),\ s,t\in J_{1},\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>

                      <p>
                        <i>then D is convex and g</i>
                        <sub>
                          <i>n</i>
                        </sub>
                        <i>is concave on D.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">2∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) &gt; 0, <i>ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) &lt; 0 <i>for t </i>∈<i> I</i>
<sub>1</sub> <i>and</i> </p><div id="Equbz" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v(t+s)\geq v(t)+v(s),\ s,t\in J_{1},\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>

                      <p>
                        <i>then D is convex and g</i>
                        <sub>
                          <i>n</i>
                        </sub>
                        <i>is convex on D.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">3∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) &lt; 0, <i>ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) &gt; 0 <i>for t </i>∈<i> I</i>
<sub>1</sub><i>and</i> </p><div id="Equca" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v(t+s)\leq v(t)+v(s),\ s,t\in J_{1}, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

                      <p>
                        <i>then g</i>
                        <sub>
                          <i>n</i>
                        </sub>
                        <i>is convex on D.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">4∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) &lt; 0, <i>ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′</i></sup>(<i>t</i>) &lt; 0 <i>for t </i>∈<i> I</i>
<sub>1</sub><i>and</i> </p><div id="Equcb" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v(t+s)\leq v(t)+v(s),\ s,t\in J_{1}, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

                      <p>
                        <i>then g</i>
                        <sub>
                          <i>n</i>
                        </sub>
                        <i>is concave on D.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>For every <i>n</i> ≥ 2 let </p><div id="Equcc" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} u_n(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n) =\phi ^{-1}(\phi (x_1)+\phi (x_2)+\ldots +\phi (x_n)), \\ (x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in I^n. \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div><p> Note that </p><div id="Equ8" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_n(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)=g_2(x_1,u_{n-1}(x_2,x_3,\ldots ,x_n)) {} \end{aligned} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (4.8)
                </div></div><p> Suppose that the hypotheses of assertion 1<sup>∘</sup> hold. Note that </p><div id="Equcd" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} D=\{(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in I^n\mid x_1&gt;\phi ^{-1}(\phi (x_2)+\ldots +\phi (x_n))\} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equce" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =\{(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in I^n\mid x_1&gt;u_{n-1}(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar6">3.1</a>, <i>u</i>
<sub><i>n</i>−1</sub> is convex, hence <i>D</i> is convex. Note that <i>g</i>
<sub>2</sub> is concave, is increasing in the first argument and decreasing in the second argument. By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar3">2.3</a>, <i>g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> is concave.</p>
              <p>Suppose now that the hypotheses of assertion 2<sup>∘</sup> hold. Note that </p><div id="Equcf" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} D=\{(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in I^{n}\mid x_{1}&lt;\phi ^{-1}(\phi (x_{2})+\ldots +\phi (x_{n}))\} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equcg" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =\{(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in I^{n}\mid x_{1}&lt;u_{n-1}(x_{2},x_{3},\ldots ,x_{n})\}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar6">3.1</a>, <i>u</i>
<sub><i>n</i>−1</sub> is concave, hence <i>D</i> is convex. By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar16">4.1</a>, <i>g</i>
<sub>2</sub> is convex. By (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ8">4.8</a>) and by Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar3">2.3</a>, it follows that <i>g</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> is convex. Similar arguments apply for the proof of assertions 3<sup>∘</sup> and 4<sup>∘</sup>. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar18">Corollary 4.3</h3>
              <p>
                <i>Let</i>
                </p><div id="Equch" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} &amp; D_{p}=\{(x,y)\in \lbrack 0,\infty )^{2}\mid x&gt;y\}\mathit{\mbox{ if }}p\in (0,\infty )\mathit{\mbox{ and}} \\ &amp; D_{p}=\{(x,y)\in (0,\infty )^{2}\mid x&lt;y\}\mathit{\mbox{ if }}p\in (-\infty ,0). \end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>For</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \mathbb {R}^{\ast }= \mathbb {R} -\left \{ 0\right \} \)</span>
                <i>consider the function</i>
                </p><div id="Equci" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{p}(x,y)=(x^{p}-y^{p})^{\frac{{1}}{{p}}},\ (x,y)\in D_{p}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">1∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If p </i>∈ [1, <i>∞</i>)<i>, then f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> <i>is concave on D</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub>.</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">2∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If p </i>∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0) ∪ (0, 1]<i>, then f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> <i>is convex on D</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub>.</p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Note that for every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \mathbb {R}^{\ast }\)</span> the set <i>D</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is convex. If <i>p</i> = 1, then <i>f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is convex and concave. Suppose that <i>p</i> ∈ (1, <i>∞</i>) and let </p><div id="Equ9" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \phi _{p}(t)=t^{p},\ t\in \lbrack 0,\infty ) \end{aligned} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (4.9)
                </div></div> <div id="Equ10" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v_{p}(t)=\displaystyle\frac{\phi _{p}^{\prime 2}(\phi _{p}^{-1}(t))}{\phi _{p}^{\prime \prime }(\phi _{p}^{-1}(t))},\ t\in (0,\infty ). \end{aligned} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (4.10)
                </div></div>

              <p>Note that </p><div id="Equcj" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \phi _{p}^{\prime }(t)&gt;0,\ \phi _{p}^{\prime \prime }(t)&gt;0,\ v_{p}(t+s)=v_{p}(t)+v_{p}(s),\ t,s\in (0,\infty ). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar16">4.1</a> it follows that <i>f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is concave on <i>D</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub>.</p>
              <p>Suppose that <i>p</i> ∈ (0, 1). Note that </p><div id="Equck" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \phi ^{\prime }_p(t)&gt;0,\ \phi ^{\prime \prime }_p(t)&lt;0,\ t\in (0,\infty ) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> and <i>v</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is additive on (0, <i>∞</i>). By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar16">4.1</a> it follows that <i>f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is convex on <i>D</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub>.</p>
              <p>Suppose that <i>p</i> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0). Note that </p><div id="Equcl" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \phi ^{\prime }_p(t)&lt;0,\ \phi ^{\prime \prime }_p(t)&gt;0,\ t\in (0,\infty ) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> and <i>v</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is additive on (0, <i>∞</i>). By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar16">4.1</a> <i>f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is convex on <i>D</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub>. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar19">Theorem 4.4</h3>
              <p><i>Let J</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> = [0, <i>∞</i>) <i>if p </i>∈ (0, <i>∞</i>) <i>and J</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> = (0, <i>∞</i>) <i>if p </i>∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0)<i>. For every</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \mathbb {R}^*\)</span> <i>consider the set</i> </p><div id="Equcm" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} D_p=\{(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in J_p^n\mid x_1^p&gt;x_2^p+x_3^p+\ldots +x_n^p\}\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>and the function</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f_p:D_p\to \mathbb {R}\)</span>
                <i>,</i>
                </p><div id="Equ11" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_p(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)=(x_1^p-x_2^p-\ldots -x_n^p)^{\frac{1}{p}},\ (x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in D_p. {}\end{aligned} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (4.11)
                </div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">1∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>D</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> <i>is convex for p </i>∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0) ∪ [1, <i>∞</i>).</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">2∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If p </i>∈ [1, <i>∞</i>)<i>, then f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> <i>is concave on D</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub>.</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">3∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If p </i>∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0)<i>, then f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> <i>is convex on D</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub>.</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">4∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If p </i>∈ (0, 1)<i>, then f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> <i>is convex on every convex subset of D</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub>.</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">5∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If p </i>∈ (0, 1)<i>, then D</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> <i>is convex if and only if n </i>= 2.</p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <i>ϕ</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> and <i>v</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> be defined as in (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ9">4.9</a>) and (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ10">4.10</a>). Note that </p><div id="Equcn" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \phi ^{\prime }_p(t)=pt^{p-1},\ \phi ^{\prime \prime }_p(t)=p(p-1)t^{p-2},\ v_p(t)=\displaystyle\frac{t}{p-1},\ t\in J_p. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Consider the function </p><div id="Equco" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \psi _p(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_{n-1}) =(x_1^p+x_2^p+\ldots +x_{n-1}^p)^{\frac{1}{p}} ,\ (x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_{n-1})\in J_p^{n-1}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>If <i>p</i> ∈ [1, <i>∞</i>), then <i>ψ</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is convex. Note that the function </p><div id="Equcp" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u_p(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)=\psi _p(x_2,x_3,\ldots ,x_n)-x_1,\ (x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in D_p \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> is convex and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(D_p=\{x\in J_p^n\mid u_p(x)&lt;0\}\)</span>. Hence <i>D</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is convex.</p>
              <p>If <i>p</i> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0), then <i>ψ</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> and <i>u</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> are concave and </p><div id="Equcq" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} D_p=\{x\in J_p^n\mid u_p(x)&gt;0\}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Consequently <i>D</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is convex.</p>
              <p>Suppose that <i>p</i> ∈ (1, <i>∞</i>). Note that </p><div id="Equcr" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \phi ^{\prime }_p(t)&gt;0,\ \phi ^{\prime \prime }_p(t)&gt;0,\ v_p(t+s)=v_p(t)+v_p(s),\ t,s\in (0,\infty ). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> From Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar17">4.2</a> it follows that <i>f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is concave on <i>D</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub>.</p>
              <p>If <i>p</i> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0), then <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\phi ^{\prime }_p(t)&lt;0\)</span>, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\phi ^{\prime \prime }_p(t)&gt;0\)</span>, <i>t</i> ∈ (0, <i>∞</i>) and <i>v</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is additive. From Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar17">4.2</a> it follows that <i>f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is convex.</p>
              <p>If <i>p</i> ∈ (0, 1), then <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\phi ^{\prime }_p&gt;0\)</span>, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\phi ^{\prime \prime }_p&lt;0\)</span>, <i>v</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is additive on (0, <i>∞</i>). If <i>U</i> is a convex subset of <i>D</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub>, then from Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar17">4.2</a> it follows that <i>f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is convex on <i>U</i>.</p>
              <p>In order to prove assertion 5<sup>∘</sup> let <i>p</i> ∈ (0, 1) and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(a\in \left (1,2^{\frac {1-p}{p}}\right )\)</span>. If <i>n</i> = 2 one can easily see that <i>D</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is convex. If <i>n</i> ≥ 3 let </p><div id="Equcs" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} x=(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}),\ y=(y_{1},y_{2},\ldots ,y_{n}),\ z=(z_{1},z_{2},\ldots ,z_{n})\in \mathbb{R}^{n}, \\ x_{1}=a,\ x_{2}=0,\ x_{3}=1,\ y_{1}=a,\ y_{2}=1,\ y_{3}=0,\ x_{i}=y_{i}=0\ \text{for }i\in \left\{ 4,5,..n\right\} . \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div><p> Note that <i>x</i> ∈ <i>D</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> if and only if <i>y</i> ∈ <i>D</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> which is equivalent with <i>a</i> &gt; 1. Now, observe that <span class="mathjax-tex">\(z=\displaystyle \frac {1}{2}(x+y)\)</span> does not belong to <i>D</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub>. Hence <i>D</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is not convex. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar20">Theorem 4.5</h3>
              <p>
                <i>For every</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \mathbb {R}^{\ast }\)</span>
                <i>let J</i>
                <sub>
                  <i>p</i>
                </sub>
                <i>be defined as in the statement of the preceding Theorem. Let E be a linear space, D be a convex subset of E. For every</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \mathbb {R}^{\ast }\)</span>
                <i>consider</i>
                </p><div id="Equct" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} u_{i,p}:D\rightarrow J_{p},\ i=1,2,\ldots ,n, \\ U_{p}=\{x\in D\mid u_{1,p}^{p}(x)&gt;u_{2,p}^{p}(x)+\ldots +u_{n,p}^{p}(x)\}, \\ g_{p}(x)=(u_{1,p}^{p}(x)-u_{2,p}^{p}(x)-\ldots -u_{n,p}^{p}(x))^{\frac{{1}}{{ p}}},\ x\in U_{p}, \\ h_{p}(x)=\ln [u_{1,p}^{p}(x)-u_{2,p}^{p}(x)-\ldots -u_{n,p}^{p}(x)],\ x\in U_{p}, \\ w_{p}=(u_{1,p}^{p}(x)-u_{2,p}^{p}(x)-\ldots -u_{n,p}^{p}(x))^{a},\ x\in U_{p}. \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">1∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If p </i>∈ [1, <i>∞</i>)<i>, a </i>∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0)<i>, u</i>
<sub>1,<i>p</i></sub> <i>is concave and u</i>
<sub><i>i</i>,<i>p</i></sub> <i>are convex for i </i>∈{2, 3, …, <i>n</i>}<i>, then g</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> <i>and h</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> <i>are concave on U</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> <i>and w</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> <i>is convex.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">2∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If a</i>, <i>p </i>∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0)<i>, ap </i>∈ [1, <i>∞</i>)<i>, u</i>
<sub>1,<i>p</i></sub> <i>is convex and u</i>
<sub><i>i</i>,<i>p</i></sub> <i>are concave for every i </i>∈{2, 3, …, <i>n</i>}<i>, then w</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> <i>is convex on U</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub>.</p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <i>f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> be defined as in (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ11">4.11</a>). Suppose that <i>p</i> ∈ [1, <i>∞</i>) and <i>a</i> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0). Note that </p><div id="Equcu" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_p(x)=f_p(u_1(x),u_2(x),\ldots ,u_n(x)),\ x\in U_p. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar19">4.4</a> it follows that <i>f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is concave. By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar3">2.3</a> it follows that <i>g</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is concave. By Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar2">2.2</a> it follows that <i>h</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is concave and <i>w</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is convex.</p>
              <p>Suppose that the hypotheses of assertion 2<sup>∘</sup> hold. Note that </p><div id="Equcv" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} w_p(x)=[f_p(u_1(x),u_2(x),\ldots ,u_n(x))]^{ap},\ x\in U_p. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar19">4.4</a> it follows that <i>f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is convex. Using Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar3">2.3</a> it follows that <i>g</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is convex. By Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar2">2.2</a> it follows that <i>w</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is convex on <i>U</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub>. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar21">Corollary 4.6</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2, <i>p </i>≥ 1<i>, E be a linear normed space, D be a convex subset of E, and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(v:E^{n}\rightarrow \mathbb {R}\)</span> <i>be a linear function. Consider the set</i> </p><div id="Equcw" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} U_{p}=\left\{ (x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in D^{n}\mid v(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})&gt;\left( \sum_{i=2}^{n}\Vert x_{i}\Vert ^{p}\right) ^{\frac{{1}}{{p}} }\right\} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>and the function</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(g_{p}:U_{p}\rightarrow \mathbb {R}\)</span>
                <i>,</i>
                </p><div id="Equcx" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{p}(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})=\left( [v(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})]^{p}-\sum_{i=2}^{n}\Vert x_{i}\Vert ^{p}\right) ^{\frac{{1}}{{p}}}, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equcy" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} (x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in U_{p}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p><i>Then g</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> <i>is concave on U</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub>.</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let </p><div id="Equcz" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u_1=v,\ u_i(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)=\|x_i\|,\ i=2,3,\ldots ,n,\ (x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in D^n. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Consider the function </p><div id="Equda" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u(x)=(u_1(x),u_2(x),\ldots ,u_n(x)),\ x\in D^n. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that <i>u</i>
<sub>1</sub> is concave and <i>u</i>
<sub>2</sub>, <i>u</i>
<sub>3</sub>, …, <i>u</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> are convex. If <i>f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is defined by (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ11">4.11</a>), then </p><div id="Equdb" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_p(x)=f_p(u(x)),\ x\in U_p. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar20">4.5</a> it follows that <i>g</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is concave on <i>U</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub>. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar22">Corollary 4.7</h3>
              <p>
                <i>For every</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \mathbb {R}^*\)</span>
                <i>let</i>
                </p><div id="Equdc" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} U_p=\left\{(z_1,z_2,\ldots ,z_n)\in \mathbb{C}^n\mid \mathrm{Re} (z_1)&gt;(|z_2|{}^p+|z_3|{}^p+\ldots +|z_n|{}^p)^{\frac{1}{p}}\right\}, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equdd" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_p(z_1,z_2,\ldots ,z_n)=(|\mathrm{Re} (z_1)|{}^p-|z_2|{}^p-|z_3|{}^p -\ldots -|z_n|{}^p)^{\frac{1}{p}}, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equde" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} (z_1,z_2,\ldots ,z_n)\in U_p. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p><i>If p </i>∈ [1, <i>∞</i>)<i>, then g</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> <i>is concave on U</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub>.</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let </p><div id="Equdf" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u_1(z_1,z_2,\ldots ,z_n)=\mathrm{Re}(z_1),\ u_k(z_1,z_2,\ldots ,z_n)=|z_k|, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equdg" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} k\in \{2,3,\ldots ,n\},\ (z_1,z_2,\ldots ,z_n)\in \mathbb{C}^n. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Let <i>f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> be defined by (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ11">4.11</a>). Note that </p><div id="Equdh" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_p(z)=f_p(u_1(z),u_2(z),\ldots ,u_n(z)),\ z\in U_p \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> and <i>u</i>
<sub>1</sub> is concave and <i>u</i>
<sub>2</sub>, <i>u</i>
<sub>3</sub>, …, <i>u</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> are convex. By the preceding Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar21">4.6</a> it follows that <i>g</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is concave on <i>U</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub>. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar23">Corollary 4.8</h3>
              <p><i>For every n </i>≥ 2<i>, p </i>∈ [1, <i>∞</i>)<i>, a </i>&lt; 0 <i>consider</i> </p><div id="Equdi" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} D_{p,n}=\{(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in \lbrack 0,\infty )^{n}\mid x_{1}^{p}&gt;x_{2}^{p}+x_{3}^{p}+\ldots +x_{n}^{p}\} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              
                <div id="Equdj" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} U_{p,n}=\left\{ (x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in \lbrack 0,\infty )^{n}\mid \left( \sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}\right) ^{p}&gt;\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{p}\right\} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equdk" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{1,p,n}(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})=\ln (x_{1}^{p}-x_{2}^{p}-\ldots -x_{n}^{p}),\ (x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in D_{p,n} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equdl" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{2,p,n}(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})=(x_{1}^{p}-x_{2}^{p}-\ldots -x_{n}^{p})^{a},\ (x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in D_{p,n} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equdm" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><img src="//media.springernature.com/lw518/springer-static/image/chp%3A10.1007%2F978-3-030-27407-8_4/MediaObjects/450928_1_En_4_Equdm_HTML.png" class="u-display-block" alt=""></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equdn" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><img src="//media.springernature.com/lw516/springer-static/image/chp%3A10.1007%2F978-3-030-27407-8_4/MediaObjects/450928_1_En_4_Equdn_HTML.png" class="u-display-block" alt=""></div></div>
              
              <p><i>If p </i>∈ [1, <i>∞</i>) <i>then the following assertions hold:</i> </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">1∘</span>

<p><i>D</i>
<sub><i>p</i>,<i>n</i></sub> <i>and U</i>
<sub><i>p</i>,<i>n</i></sub> <i>are convex sets.</i>
</p>

</li>
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">2∘</span>

<p><i>g</i>
<sub>1,<i>p</i>,<i>n</i></sub> <i>and g</i>
<sub>3,<i>p</i>,<i>n</i></sub> <i>are concave.</i>
</p>

</li>
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">3∘</span>

<p><i>g</i>
<sub>3,<i>p</i>,<i>n</i></sub> <i>and g</i>
<sub>4,<i>p</i>,<i>n</i></sub> <i>are convex.</i>
</p>

</li>
</ol>

            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>For every <i>k</i> ≥ 1 let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(h_{k,p}:[0,\infty )^k\to \mathbb {R}\)</span> be defined as follows: </p><div id="Equdo" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} h_{k,p}(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_k)=(x_1^p+x_2^p+\ldots +x_k^p)^{\frac{1}{p}},\ (x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_k)\in [0,\infty )^k. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar7">3.2</a>, <i>h</i>
<sub><i>k</i>,<i>p</i></sub> is convex. Since </p><div id="Equdp" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} D_{p,n}=\{(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in [0,\infty )^n\mid x_1&gt;h_{n-1,p}(x_2,x_3,\ldots ,x_n)\} \\ U_{p,n}=\left\{(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in [0,\infty )^n\mid \sum_{i=1}^n x_i&gt;h_{n,p}(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\right\} \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div><p> it follows that <i>D</i>
<sub><i>p</i>,<i>n</i></sub> and <i>U</i>
<sub><i>p</i>,<i>n</i></sub> are convex sets.</p>
              <p>By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar20">4.5</a> it follows that <i>g</i>
<sub>1,<i>p</i></sub> is concave and <i>g</i>
<sub>2,<i>p</i></sub> is convex. Since </p><div id="Equdq" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{3,p,n}(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n) =g_{1,p}\left(\sum_{i=1}^n x_i,x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n\right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> and </p><div id="Equdr" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{4,p,n}(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n) =g_{2,p,n+1}\left(\sum_{i=1}^n x_i,x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n\right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> it results that <i>g</i>
<sub>3,<i>p</i>,<i>n</i></sub> is concave and <i>g</i>
<sub>4,<i>p</i>,<i>n</i></sub> is convex. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar24">Theorem 4.9</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2, <i>p</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> &gt; 0<i>, i </i>= 1, 2, …, <i>n, p </i>&gt; 0 <i>and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(a=\min \limits _{2\leq i\leq n}(p_{i})\)</span><i>. Consider the set</i> </p><div id="Equds" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} D=\left\{ (x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in \mathbb{R}^{n}\mid x_{1}&gt;\left( \sum_{i=2}^{n}|x_{i}|{}^{p_{i}}\right) ^{\frac{{1}}{{p_{1}}}}\right\} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>and the function</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(g_{p}:D\rightarrow \mathbb {R}\)</span>
                <i>,</i>
                </p><div id="Equdt" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{p}(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})=(|x_{1}|{}^{p_{1}}-|x_{2}|{}^{p_{2}}-\ldots -|x_{n}|{}^{p_{n}})^{\frac{{1}}{{p}}},\ (x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in D. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p><i>If a </i>≥ 1 <i>and p</i>
<sub>1</sub> ≤<i> a </i>≤<i> p, then D is a convex set and g</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> <i>is concave on D.</i>
</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(h:\mathbb {R}^{n-1}\to \mathbb {R}\)</span>, </p><div id="Equdu" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} h(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_{n-1})=\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}|x_i|{}^{p_{i+1}} \right)^{\frac{1}{p_1}},\ (x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_{n-1})\in \mathbb{R}^{n-1}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar9">3.4</a>, <i>h</i> is convex. Since </p><div id="Equdv" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} D=\{(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in \mathbb{R}^n\mid x_1&gt;h(x_2,x_3,\ldots ,x_n)\} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> it follows that <i>D</i> is a convex set.</p>
              <p>Let <i>f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> be defined as in (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ11">4.11</a>). Note that </p><div id="Equdw" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_p(x)=[f_a(u_1(x),u_2(x),\ldots ,u_n(x))]^{\frac{a}{p}},\ x\in D. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar19">4.4</a> it follows that <i>f</i>
<sub><i>a</i></sub> is concave. By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar3">2.3</a>, <i>g</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is concave. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar25">Theorem 4.10</h3>
              <p><i>Let p</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> &lt; 0<i>, i </i>= 1, 2, …, <i>n, p </i>&lt; 0<i>,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\( a=\min \limits _{2\le i\le n}(p_i)\)</span><i>,</i> </p><div id="Equdx" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} D=\left\{(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in (0,\infty )^n\mid x_1&lt;\left(\sum_{i=2}^n x_i^{p_i}\right)^{\frac{1}{p_1}}\right\} \\ g_p(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)=(x_1^{p_1}-x_2^{p_2}-\ldots - x_n^{p_n})^{\frac{1}{p}} ,\ (x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in D. \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div>

              <p><i>If p</i>
<sub>1</sub> ≤<i> a </i>≤<i> p, then D is a convex set and g</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> <i>is convex on D.</i>
</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(h:(0,\infty )^{n-1}\to \mathbb {R}\)</span>, </p><div id="Equdy" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} h(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_{n-1})=\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}x_i^{p_{i+1}} \right)^{\frac{ 1}{p_1}},\ (x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_{n-1})\in (0,\infty )^{n-1}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar9">3.4</a>, <i>h</i> is concave on (0, <i>∞</i>)<sup><i>n</i>−1</sup>. Since </p><div id="Equdz" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} D=\{(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in (0,\infty )^n\mid x_1&lt;h(x_2,x_3,\ldots ,x_n)\} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> it follows that <i>D</i> is a convex set.</p>
              <p>Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(u_k:D\to \mathbb {R}\)</span>, <i>k</i> = 1, 2, …, <i>n</i> be defined as follows: </p><div id="Equea" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u_1(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)=x_1^{p_1/a}, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equeb" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u_k(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)=x_k^{p_k/a},\ k=2,3,\ldots ,n,\ (x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in D. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>Since <i>p</i>
<sub>1</sub>∕<i>a</i> ≥ 1 it follows that <i>u</i>
<sub>1</sub> is convex.</p>
              <p>Since <i>p</i>
<sub><i>k</i></sub>∕<i>a</i> ∈ (0, 1], <i>k</i> = 2, 3, …, <i>n</i> it follows that <i>u</i>
<sub><i>k</i></sub>, <i>k</i> = 2, 3, …, <i>n</i> are concave.</p>
              <p>Let <i>u</i>(<i>x</i>) = (<i>u</i>
<sub>1</sub>(<i>x</i>), <i>u</i>
<sub>2</sub>(<i>x</i>), …, <i>u</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub>(<i>x</i>)), <i>x</i> ∈ <i>D</i>. Denote with <i>f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> the function defined by (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ11">4.11</a>). Note that </p><div id="Equec" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_p(x)=[f_a(u(x))]^{a/p},\ x\in D. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar20">4.5</a>, <i>f</i>
<sub><i>a</i></sub> is convex. By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar3">2.3</a> it follows that <i>g</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is convex on <i>D</i>. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar26">Theorem 4.11</h3>
              <p>
                <i>Let</i>
                </p><div id="Equed" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} D=\{(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in \mathbb{R}^{n}\mid x_{1}&gt;\ln (e^{x_{2}}+e^{x_{3}}+\ldots +e^{x_{n}})\},\ a&lt;0, \\ f(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})=\ln (e^{x_{1}}-e^{x_{2}}-\ldots -e^{x_{n}}),\ (x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in D, \\ g(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})=(e^{x_{1}}-e^{x_{2}}-\ldots -e^{x_{n}})^{a},\ (x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in D. \\ h(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})=\ln (e^{x_{1}+x_{2}+\ldots+x_{n}}-e^{x_{1}}-e^{x_{2}}-\ldots -e^{x_{n}}), (x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\,{\in}\,\left( 0,\infty \right) ^{n} \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p><i>Then D is a convex set, f is concave on D</i>, <i>g is convex on D and h is concave on</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}.\)</span></p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let </p><div id="Equee" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} h(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_{n-1})=\ln(e^{x_1}+e^{x_2}+\ldots +e^{x_{n-1}}),\ (x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in \mathbb{R}^{n-1}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar8">3.3</a> it follows that <i>h</i> is convex. Since </p><div id="Equef" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} D=\{(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in \mathbb{R}^n\mid x_1&gt;h(x_2,x_3,\ldots ,x_n)\} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> it follows that <i>D</i> is a convex set.</p>
              <p>Let <i>ϕ</i>(<i>t</i>) = <i>e</i>
<sup><i>t</i></sup>, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(t\in \mathbb {R}\)</span>. Note that </p><div id="Equeg" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v(t)=\displaystyle\frac{\phi ^{\prime 2}(\phi ^{-1}(t))}{\phi ^{\prime \prime -1}(t))}=t,\ \phi ^{\prime }(t)&gt;0,\ \phi ^{\prime \prime }(t)&gt;0,\ t\in \mathbb{R}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar17">4.2</a> it follows that <i>f</i> is concave on <i>D</i>. Since <span class="mathjax-tex">\(g=\exp (af)\)</span> it follows from Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar2">2.2</a> that <i>g</i> is a convex function. The concavity of <i>h</i> follows from Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar3">2.3</a>. and from the concavity of <i>f</i>. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar27">Theorem 4.12</h3>
              <p><i>Let E be a linear space, D be a convex subset of E and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(u_{k}:D\rightarrow \mathbb {R}\)</span><i>, k </i>= 1, 2, …, <i>n. Suppose that u</i>
<sub>1</sub> <i>is concave and u</i>
<sub>2</sub>, <i>u</i>
<sub>3</sub>, …, <i>u</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> <i>are convex. Let a </i>&lt; 0<i>,</i> </p><div id="Equeh" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} U=\{x\in D\mid u_{1}(x)&gt;\ln (e^{u_{2}(x)}+e^{u_{3}(x)}+\ldots +e^{u_{n}(x)})\} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>and</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f,g:U\rightarrow \mathbb {R}\)</span>
                <i>,</i>
                </p><div id="Equei" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x)=\ln (e^{u_{1}(x)}-e^{u_{2}(x)}-\ldots -e^{u_{n}(x)}),\ x\in U, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equej" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g(x)=(e^{u_{1}(x)}-e^{u_{2}(x)}-\ldots -e^{u_{n}(x)})^{a},\ x\in U. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Then U is a convex set, f is concave and g is convex.</i>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(h(x)=\ln (e^{u_2(x)}+e^{u_3(x)} +\ldots +e^{u_n(x)}),\ x\in D.\)</span> By Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar8">3.3</a> it follows that <i>h</i> is convex on <i>D</i>. Since </p><div id="Equek" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} U=\{x\in D\mid u_1(x)&gt;h(x)\} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> it follows that <i>U</i> is a convex set. By the preceding theorem and by Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar3">2.3</a> it follows that <i>f</i> is concave and <i>g</i> is convex. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar28">Theorem 4.13</h3>
              <p>
                <i>Let</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(P:\mathbb {R}^{n}\rightarrow \mathbb {R}\)</span>
                <i>be a function. Suppose that</i>
                </p><div id="Equel" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} P=P_{1}-P_{2} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>where</i>
                </p><div id="Equem" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} P_{1}(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})=d_{0}x_{1}^{a_{1}}x_{2}^{a_{2}}\ldots x_{n}^{a_{n}} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equen" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} P_{2}(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})=\sum_{r=1}^{m}d_{r}x_{1}^{b_{r1}}x_{2}^{b_{r2}}\ldots x_{n}^{b_{rn}}, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equeo" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} a_{i}\geq 0,\ b_{ri}\geq 0,\ d_{r}&gt;0,\ i\in \{1,2,\ldots ,n\},\ r\in \{0,1,\ldots ,m\}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Denote</i>
                </p><div id="Equep" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} c_{i}=\max_{1\leq r\leq m}(b_{ri}),\ i\in \{1,2,\ldots ,n\}, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equeq" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} D_{1}=\{x\in (0,\infty )^{n}\mid P(x)&gt;0\} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equer" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} D_{2}=\{(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in \mathbb{R}^{n}\mid P(e^{x_{1}},e^{x_{2}},\ldots ,e^{x_{n}})&gt;0\}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p><i>Consider the functions</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(g_{i}:D_{i}\rightarrow \mathbb {R}\)</span><i>, i </i>= 1, 2<i>,</i> </p><div id="Eques" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{1}(x)=\ln [P(x)],\ x\in D_{1} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              
                <div id="Equet" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{2}(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})=\ln [P(e^{x_{1}},e^{x_{2}},\ldots ,e^{x_{n}})],\ (x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in D_{2}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">1∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If a</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> ≥<i> c</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> <i>for every i </i>∈{1, 2, …, <i>n</i>}<i>, then D</i>
<sub>1</sub> <i>is a convex set and g</i>
<sub>1</sub> <i>is concave on D</i>
<sub>1</sub>.</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">2∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>The set D</i>
<sub>2</sub> <i>is convex and g</i>
<sub>2</sub> <i>is concave on D</i>
<sub>2</sub>.</p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Suppose that <i>a</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> ≥ <i>c</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> for every <i>i</i> ∈{1, 2, …, <i>n</i>}.</p>
              <p>For <i>r</i> ∈{1, 2, …, <i>m</i>} let </p><div id="Equeu" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} Q_{r}(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})=x_{1}^{b_{r1}-a_{1}}x_{2}^{b_{r2}-a_{2}}\ldots x_{n}^{b_{rn}-a_{n}}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Consider </p><div id="Equev" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} Q(x)=\sum_{r=1}^{m}\displaystyle\frac{d_{r}}{d_{0}}Q_{r}(x). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that </p><div id="Equew" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} Q(x)=\displaystyle\frac{P_{2}(x)}{P_{1}(x)},\ x\in (0,\infty )^{n} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> and </p><div id="Equex" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} D_{1}=\{x\in (0,\infty )^{n}\mid P(x)&lt;0\}=\{x\in (0,\infty )^{n}\mid Q(x)&lt;1\}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Since <i>b</i>
<sub><i>ri</i></sub> − <i>a</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> ≤ 0 for every <i>i</i> ∈{1, 2, …, <i>n</i>}, <i>r</i> ∈{1, 2, …, <i>m</i>} it follows that all functions <i>Q</i>
<sub><i>r</i></sub> are convex. Hence <i>Q</i> is convex and <i>D</i>
<sub>1</sub> is a convex set. Note that </p><div id="Equey" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{1}(x)&amp; =\ln [P(x)]=\ln [P_{1}(x)(1-Q(x))]=\ln [P_{1}(x)]+\ln [1-Q(x)] \\ &amp; =\ln (d_{0})+\sum_{i=1}^{n}a_{i}\ln (x_{i})+\ln (1-Q(x)), \end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equez" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} x=(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in D_{1}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Since all the terms in the right hand side of the preceding equation are concave it follows that <i>g</i>
<sub>1</sub> is concave on <i>D</i>
<sub>1</sub>. Let </p><div id="Equfa" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} w(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})=a_{1}x_{1}+a_{2}x_{2}+\ldots +a_{n}x_{n}+\ln (d_{0}), \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equfb" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u_{r}(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})=b_{r1}x_{1}+b_{r2}x_{2}+\ldots +b_{rn}x_{n}+\ln (d_{r}),\ r\in \{1,2,\ldots ,m\}, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equfc" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v(\mathbf{x})=\ln \left[ \sum_{r=1}^{m}\exp (u_{r}(\mathbf{x}))\right] -w( \mathbf{x}),\ \mathbf{x}\in D_{2}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that </p><div id="Equfd" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} P(e^{x_{1}},e^{x_{2}},\ldots ,e^{x_{n}})=\exp (u_{1}(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}))-\sum_{r=1}^{m}\exp (u_{r}(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> One can easily see that </p><div id="Equfe" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} D_{2}=\{\mathbf{x}\in \mathbb{R}^{n}\mid P(e^{x_{1}},e^{x_{2}},\ldots ,e^{x_{n}})&gt;0\}=\{\mathbf{x}\in \mathbb{R}^{n}\mid v(\mathbf{x})&lt;0\}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Since <i>v</i> is convex it follows that <i>D</i>
<sub>2</sub> is convex. Note that </p><div id="Equff" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{2}(\mathbf{x})=\ln [\exp (w(\mathbf{x}))-\exp (v(\mathbf{x})+w(\mathbf{x} ))],\ \mathbf{x}\in D_{2}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Since <i>w</i> is concave and <i>v</i> + <i>w</i> is convex, from Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar27">4.12</a> it follows that <i>g</i>
<sub>2</sub> is concave on <i>D</i>
<sub>2</sub>. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar29">Lemma 4.14</h3>
              <p><i>Let p </i>≥ 1<i>,</i> 0 ≤<i> b </i>≤<i> a,</i> </p><div id="Equfg" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x)=(x+a)^p-(x+b)^p,\ x\in [0,\infty ). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>Then f is increasing.</i>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Note that <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f\ ^{\prime }\left ( x\right ) =p\left [ (x+a)^{p-1}-(x+b)^{p-1}\right ] \geq 0\)</span>, <i>x</i> ∈ [0, <i>∞</i>). □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar30">Lemma 4.15</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2<i>, p </i>≥ 1<i>, x</i>
<sub>1</sub>, <i>x</i>
<sub>2</sub>, …, <i>x</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> ≥ 0<i>. If</i> </p><div id="Equfh" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}\geq 2\max (x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>then</i>
                </p><div id="Equ12" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left( \sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}\right) ^{p}\geq 2^{p-1}\left( \sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{p}\right) .{} \end{aligned} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (4.12)
                </div></div>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Without loss of generality we may suppose that </p><div id="Equfi" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} 0\le x_1\le x_2\le\ldots \le x_n\le x_1+x_2+\ldots +x_{n-1}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Let <i>n</i> = 2. Then inequality (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ12">4.12</a>) becomes </p><div id="Equfj" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} (x_1+x_2)^p\ge 2^{p-1}(x_1^p+x_2^p)\mbox{ if } x_1=x_2. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Thus inequality (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ12">4.12</a>) holds in the case <i>n</i> = 2. Suppose now that <i>n</i> ≥ 3. Denote </p><div id="Equfk" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} A=x_1+x_2+\ldots +x_{n-2}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Let <i>f</i> be defined as in the statement of the preceding lemma. Take </p><div id="Equfl" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} x=A+x_{n-1},\ a=x_n,\ b=x_{n-1}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Since <i>A</i> + <i>x</i>
<sub><i>n</i>−1</sub> ≥ <i>x</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub>, from the preceding lemma we have that </p><div id="Equfm" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} (A+x_{n-1}+x_n)^p-(A+2x_{n-1})^p =f(A+x_{n-1})\ge f(x_n) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equ13" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =(2x_n)^p-(x_n+x_{n-1})^p {} \end{aligned} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (4.13)
                </div></div><p> In the following we propose to prove the inequality </p><div id="Equfn" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left(\sum_{i=1}^n x_i\right)^p -2^{p-1}\left(\sum_{i=1}^n x_i^p\right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equ14" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \ge \left[\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n-2}x_i\right)+2x_{n-1}\right]^p -2^{p-1}\left[ \left(\sum_{i=1}^{n-2}x_i^p\right)+2x_{n-1}^p\right] {} \end{aligned} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (4.14)
                </div></div><p> Using inequality (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ13">4.13</a>) we obtain: </p><div id="Equfo" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left(\sum_{i=1}^n x_i\right)^p -2^{p-1}\left(\sum_{i=1}^n x_i^p\right) - \left[\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n-2}x_i\right)+2x_{n-1}\right]^p \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equfp" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} +2^{p-1}\left[\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n-2}x_i^p\right)+2x_{n-1}^p\right] \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equfq" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =(A+x_{n-1}+x_n)^p-(A+2x_{n-1})^p-2^{p-1}(x_n^p-x_{n-1}^p) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equfr" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \ge (2x_n)^p-(x_n+x_{n-1})^p-2^{p-1}(x_n^p-x_{n-1}^p) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equfs" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =2^{p-1}(x_{n-1}^p+x_n^p)-(x_{n-1}+x_n)^p\ge 0. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Thus we have proved inequality (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ14">4.14</a>).</p>
              <p>In the following we propose to prove the inequality </p><div id="Equ15" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left[ \left( \sum_{i=1}^{n-2}x_{i}\right) +2x_{n-1}\right] ^{p}-2^{p-1} \left[ \left( \sum_{i=1}^{n-2}x_{i}^{p}\right) +2x_{n-1}^{p}\right] \geq 0 {} \end{aligned} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (4.15)
                </div></div><p> From the Bernoulli inequality it follows that </p><div id="Equ16" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} (A+2x_{n-1})^{p}\geq (2x_{n-1})^{p}+pA(2x_{n-1})^{p-1}{} \end{aligned} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (4.16)
                </div></div><p> From the above inequality we obtain </p><div id="Equft" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left[ \left( \sum_{i=1}^{n-2}x_{i}\right) +2x_{n-1}\right] ^{p}-2^{p-1} \left[ \left( \sum_{i=1}^{n-2}x_{i}^{p}\right) +2x_{n-1}^{p}\right] \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equfu" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =(A+2x_{n-1})^{p}-(2x_{n-1})^{p}-2^{p-1}\left( \sum_{i=1}^{n-2}x_{i}^{p}\right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equfv" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \geq pA(2x_{n-1})^{p-1}-2^{p-1}\left( \sum_{i=1}^{n-2}x_{i}^{p}\right) =2^{p-1}\left[ \sum_{i=1}^{n-2}x_{i}(px_{n-1}^{p-1}-x_{i}^{p-1})\right] \geq 0. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Thus we have proved inequality (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ15">4.15</a>). Inequality (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ12">4.12</a>) follows at once from inequalities (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ14">4.14</a>) and (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ15">4.15</a>). □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar31">Theorem 4.16</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 3<i>, p </i>∈ [1, <i>∞</i>), <i>a </i>∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0), </p><div id="Equfw" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} D=\left\{ (x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in \lbrack 0,\infty )^{n}\mid \sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}\geq 2\max (x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\right\} , \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              
                <div id="Equfx" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} U=\left\{ (x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in D\mid \left( \sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}\right) ^{p}&gt;2^{p-1}\left( \sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{p}\right) \right\} , \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equfy" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><img src="//media.springernature.com/lw521/springer-static/image/chp%3A10.1007%2F978-3-030-27407-8_4/MediaObjects/450928_1_En_4_Equfy_HTML.png" class="u-display-block" alt=""></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equfz" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><img src="//media.springernature.com/lw518/springer-static/image/chp%3A10.1007%2F978-3-030-27407-8_4/MediaObjects/450928_1_En_4_Equfz_HTML.png" class="u-display-block" alt=""></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equga" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><img src="//media.springernature.com/lw518/springer-static/image/chp%3A10.1007%2F978-3-030-27407-8_4/MediaObjects/450928_1_En_4_Equga_HTML.png" class="u-display-block" alt=""></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">1∘</span>
                    
                      <p>
                        <i>D and U are convex sets.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">2∘</span>
                    
                      <p>
                        <i>f is concave on D.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">2∘</span>
                    
                      <p>
                        <i>g is concave on U.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">4∘</span>
                    
                      <p>
                        <i>h is convex on U.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let </p><div id="Equgb" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} v(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)=2\max(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n),\ (x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in [0,\infty )^n, \\ w(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)=2^{1-\frac{1}{p}}\left(\sum_{i=1}^n x_i^p \right)^{1/p},\ (x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in [0,\infty )^n, \\ u(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)=\sum_{i=1}^n x_i. \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div><p> Note that <i>v</i> − <i>u</i> is a convex function and </p><div id="Equgc" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} D=\{x\in [0,\infty )^n\mid v(x)-u(x)\le 0\}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Hence <i>D</i> is a convex set. Note that <i>w</i> − <i>u</i> is a convex function and </p><div id="Equgd" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} U=\{x\in D\mid w(x)-u(x)&lt;0\}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Hence <i>U</i> is a convex set. By Lemma <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar30">4.15</a> the function <i>f</i> is well defined. Note that <i>u</i> is concave, <i>w</i> is convex, </p><div id="Equge" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x)=[u^p(x)-w^p(x)]^{1/p},\ x\in D. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar18">4.3</a> and Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar3">2.3</a> it follows that <i>f</i> is concave on <i>D</i>. Since </p><div id="Equgf" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g(x)=\ln[f(x)],\ x\in U, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> by Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar2">2.2</a> it follows that <i>g</i> is concave on <i>U</i>. Since </p><div id="Equgg" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} h(x)=\exp(ag(x)),\ x\in U, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> by Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar2">2.2</a> it follows that <i>h</i> is convex on <i>U</i>. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar32">Theorem 4.17</h3>
              <p><i>Let a </i>&lt; 0<i>, E be a linear space, D be a convex subset of E and v</i>, <i>u</i>
<sub>1</sub>, <i>u</i>
<sub>2</sub>, …, <i>u</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> :<i> D </i>→ [0, <i>∞</i>) <i>be such that u</i>
<sub>1</sub>, <i>u</i>
<sub>2</sub>, …, <i>u</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> <i>are concave functions and v is a convex function. Consider the set</i> </p><div id="Equgh" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} U=\{x\in D\mid u_1(x)u_2(x)\ldots u_n(x)&gt;v^n(x)\} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>and the functions</i>
                </p><div id="Equgi" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} f(x)=\sqrt[n]{u_1(x)u_2(x)\ldots u_n(x)-v^n(x)},\ x\in U \\ g(x)=\ln [u_1(x)u_2(x)\ldots u_n(x)-v^n(x)],\ x\in U \\ h(x)=[u_1(x)u_2(x)\ldots u_n(x)-v^n(x)]^a,\ x\in U. \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">1∘</span>
                    
                      <p>
                        <i>U is convex set.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">2∘</span>
                    
                      <p>
                        <i>The functions f and g are concave on U.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">3∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If a </i>&lt; 0<i>, then the function h is convex on U.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Consider the function </p><div id="Equgj" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u(x)=\sqrt[n]{u_1(x)u_2(x)\ldots u_n(x)},\ x\in U. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that <i>u</i> is concave and <i>v</i> − <i>u</i> is convex. Since </p><div id="Equgk" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} U=\{x\in D\mid v(x)-u(x)&lt;0\} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> it follows that <i>U</i> is a convex set. Note that </p><div id="Equgl" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x)=[u^n(x)-v^n(x)]^{1/n},\ x\in U \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equgm" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g(x)=\ln[u^n(x)-v^n(x)],\ x\in U \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equgn" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} h(x)=[u^n(x)-v^n(x)]^a,\ x\in U. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar18">4.3</a> it follows that <i>f</i> is concave. Since </p><div id="Equgo" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g(x)=n\cdot \ln [f(x)] \mbox{ and } h(x)=\exp(ag(x)),\ x\in U \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> it follows that <i>g</i> is concave and <i>h</i> is convex on <i>U</i>. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar33">Corollary 4.18</h3>
              <p><i>Let</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(c_{1},c_{2}\in \mathbb {R}\)</span><i>, a</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub>, <i>b</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> &gt; 0<i>, i </i>= 1, 2, …, <i>n be such that</i> </p><div id="Equgp" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} a_{1}a_{2}\ldots a_{n}&gt;c_{1}^{n},\ b_{1}b_{2}\ldots b_{n}&gt;c_{2}^{n}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>Then the following inequality holds:</i>
                </p><div id="Equgq" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \sqrt[n]{(a_{1}+b_{1})(a_{2}+b_{2})\ldots (a_{n}+b_{n})-(c_{1}+c_{2})^{n}} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equgr" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \geq \sqrt[n]{a_{1}a_{2}\ldots a_{n}-c_{1}^{n}}+\sqrt[n]{b_{1}b_{2}\ldots b_{n}-c_{2}^{n}}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(D=\{(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n},y)\in \mathbb {R} _{+}^{n}\times \mathbb {R}\mid x_{1}x_{2}\ldots x_{n}&gt;y^{n}\}\)</span>.</p>
              <p>Note that <i>D</i> is a convex set. Consider the function </p><div id="Equgs" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n,y)=\sqrt[n]{x_1x_2\ldots x_n-y^n},\ (x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n,y)\in D. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar32">4.17</a> the function <i>f</i> is concave on <i>D</i>. Hence </p><div id="Equgt" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} f\left(\displaystyle\frac{a_1+b_1}{2},\displaystyle\frac{a_2+b_2}{2},\ldots , \displaystyle\frac{a_n+b_n}{2}\right) \\ \ge \displaystyle\frac{1}{2}[f(a_1,a_2,\ldots ,a_n,c_1)+f(b_1,b_2,\ldots ,b_n,c_2)]. \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div><p> The inequality from the statement follows at once from the above inequality. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar34">Corollary 4.19</h3>
              <p><i>Let</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(z_i\in \mathbb {R}\)</span><i>, x</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub>, <i>y</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> ∈ (0, <i>∞</i>)<i>, i </i>= 1, 2.</p>
              <p><i>If</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x_iy_i-z_i^2&gt;0\)</span> <i>for i </i>= 1, 2<i>, then the following inequality holds:</i> </p><div id="Equgu" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \displaystyle\frac{8}{(x_1+x_2)(y_1+y_2)-(z_1+z_2)^2} \le \displaystyle\frac{ 1}{x_1y_1-z_1^2}+\displaystyle\frac{1}{x_2y_2-z_2^2}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let </p><div id="Equgv" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} D=\{(x,y,z)\in \mathbb{R}^3\mid x&gt;0,\ y&gt;0,\ xy-z^2&gt;0\} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> and </p><div id="Equgw" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x,y,z)=\displaystyle\frac{1}{xy-z^2},\ (x,y,z)\in D. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that <i>D</i> is a convex set. By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar32">4.17</a> it follows that <i>f</i> is convex on <i>D</i>. Hence </p><div id="Equgx" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f\left(\displaystyle\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},\displaystyle\frac{y_1+y_2}{2}, \displaystyle\frac{z_1+z_2}{2}\right) \le \displaystyle\frac{1}{2} [f(x_1,y_1,z_1)+f(x_2,y_2,z_2)]. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>The inequality from the statement of the corollary follows at once from the above inequality. □</p>
            <p>The inequality from the statement was proposed at the International Mathematical Olympiad in 1969 ( see [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 4" title="M. Becheanu, International Mathematical Olympiads 1959–2000, Problems, Solutions, Results (Academic Distribution Center, Freeland, 2001)" href="#ref-CR4" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e55162">4</a>] and [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 17" title="D. Djukić, V. Janković, I. Matić, N. Petrović, The IMO Compendium: A Collection of Problems Suggested for the International Mathematical Olympiads: 1959–2004 (Springer, Berlin, 2006)" href="#ref-CR17" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e55165">17</a>]). A different proof is given in [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 3" title="D. Andrica, M.O. Drimbe, On some inequalities involving isotonic functionals. Math. Anal. Numer. Theorie Approx. 17(1), 1–7 (1988)" href="#ref-CR3" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e55168">3</a>].</p>
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar35">Theorem 4.20</h3>
              <p><i>Let E be a linear space, D be a convex subset of E, a </i>&gt; 0<i>, u</i>, <i>v</i>, <i>w </i>:<i> D </i>→ (0, <i>∞</i>)<i>,</i> </p><div id="Equgy" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} U_1=\{x\in D\mid u(x)&gt;v(x)\}, \\ U_2=\{x\in D\mid u(x)v(x)&gt;w^2(x)\}, \\ f_1(x)=\displaystyle\frac{u(x)v(x)}{(u^a(x)+v^a(x))^{1/a}},\ x\in D, \\ f_2(x)=\displaystyle\frac{u(x)v(x)}{(u^a(x)-v^a(x))^{1/a}},\ x\in U_1, \\ f_3(x)=\displaystyle\frac{w(x)\sqrt {u(x)v(x)}}{\sqrt {u(x)v(x)-w^2(x)}},\ x\in U_2, \\ f_4(x)=\displaystyle\frac{u(x)v(x)w^2(x)}{u(x)v(x)-w^2(x)},\ x\in U_2. \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">1∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If u</i>, <i>v are concave, then f</i>
<sub>1</sub> <i>is concave.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">2∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If u is concave and v is convex, then U</i>
<sub>1</sub> <i>is a convex set and f</i>
<sub>2</sub> <i>is convex on U</i>
<sub>1</sub>.</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">3∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If u</i>, <i>v are concave and w is convex, then U</i>
<sub>2</sub> <i>is a convex set and f</i>
<sub>3</sub> <i>and f</i>
<sub>4</sub> <i>are convex on U</i>
<sub>2</sub>.</p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <i>p</i> &lt; 0. Consider the functions </p><div id="Equgz" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_1(x,y)=(x^p+y^p)^{1/p},\ (x,y)\in (0,\infty )^2, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equha" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_2(x,y)=(x^p-y^p)^{1/p},\ (x,y)\in (0,\infty )^2,\ x&lt;y. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar7">3.2</a>, <i>g</i>
<sub>1</sub> is concave. By Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar18">4.3</a>, <i>g</i>
<sub>2</sub> is convex.</p>
              <p>Suppose that <i>u</i>, <i>v</i> are concave and <i>p</i> = −<i>a</i>. Note that </p><div id="Equhb" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_1(x)=g_1(u(x),v(x)),\ x\in (0,\infty )^2. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar3">2.3</a>, <i>f</i>
<sub>1</sub> is concave.</p>
              <p>Suppose that <i>u</i> is concave and <i>v</i> is convex and <i>p</i> = −<i>a</i>. Note that </p><div id="Equhc" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_2(x)=g_2(v(x),u(x)),\ x\in U_1. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar3">2.3</a>, <i>f</i> is convex.</p>
              <p>Suppose <i>u</i>, <i>v</i> are concave, <i>w</i> is convex and <i>p</i> = −<i>a</i>. Note that <span class="mathjax-tex">\( \sqrt {uv}\)</span> is concave, </p><div id="Equhd" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_3(x)=g_2\left(w(x),\sqrt {u(x)v(x)}\right),\ x\in U_2, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equhe" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_4(x)=f_3^2(x),\ x\in U_2. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar3">2.3</a>, <i>f</i>
<sub>3</sub> and <i>f</i>
<sub>4</sub> are convex on <i>U</i>
<sub>2</sub>. □</p>
            </div></div></section><section data-title="Convexity of Symmetric Functions"><div class="c-article-section" id="Sec5-section"><h2 id="Sec5" class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item"><span class="c-article-section__title-number">5 </span>Convexity of Symmetric Functions</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Sec5-content"><p>An <i>n</i>-variable function <i>f</i> is called symmetric if it does not change by any permutation of its variables. A class of symmetric functions that have many applications in various domains is the class of symmetric polynomials. The symmetric <i>n</i>-variable polynomials form a ring that plays an important role in mathematics and mathematical physics [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 68" title="H.J. Schmidt, J. Schnack, Partition functions and symmetric polynomials. Am. J. Phys. 70, 53–57 (2002)" href="#ref-CR68" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e56984">68</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 69" title="H.J. Schmidt, J. Schnack, Symmetric polynomials in physics, in ed. by J.-P. Gazeau et al., GROUP 24, Physical and Mathematical Aspects of Symmetries (Institute of Physics Publishing, Bristol and Philadelphia, 2002), pp. 147–153" href="#ref-CR69" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e56987">69</a>]. Symmetric polynomials are widely used in many fields such as algebra [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 1" title="M. Aguiar, C. Andre, C. Benedetti, N. Bergeron, Z. Chen, P. Diaconis, A. Hendrickson, S. Hsiao, I.M. Isaacs, A. Jedwab, et al., Supercharacters, symmetric functions in noncommuting variables, and related Hopf algebras. Adv. Math. 229(4), 2310–2337 (2012)" href="#ref-CR1" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e56991">1</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 7" title="C. Bertone, The Euler characteristic as a polynomial in the Chern classes. Int. J. Algebra 2, 757–769 (2008)" href="#ref-CR7" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e56994">7</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 14" title="Z. Cvetkovski, Inequalities.Theorems, Techniques and Selected Problems (Springer, Berlin, 2012)" href="#ref-CR14" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e56997">14</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 64" title="T. Puong, Diamonds in Mathematical Inequalities (Hanoi Publishing House, 2007)" href="#ref-CR64" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e57000">64</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 84" title="B.J. Venkatachala, Inequalities. An Approach Through Problems (Springer, Berlin, 2018)" href="#ref-CR84" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e57003">84</a>], linear algebra [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="M.E.A. El-Mikkawy, On a connection between the Pascal, Vandermonde and Stirling matrices-I. Appl. Math. Comput. 145, 23–32 (2003)" href="#ref-CR18" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e57006">18</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="M.E.A. El-Mikkawy, On a connection between the Pascal, Vandermonde and Stirling matrices-II. Appl. Math. Comput. 146, 759–769 (2003)" href="#ref-CR19" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e57006_1">19</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 20" title="M.E.A. El-Mikkawy, Explicit inverse of a generalized Vandermonde matrix. Appl. Math. Comput. 146, 643–651 (2003)" href="#ref-CR20" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e57010">20</a>], algebraic geometry [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 54" title="V.V. Monov, A family of symmetric polynomials of the eigenvalues of a matrix. Linear Algebra Appl. 429, 2199–2208 (2008)" href="#ref-CR54" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e57013">54</a>], representation theory [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 26" title="V. Gorin, G. Panova, Asymptotics of symmetric polynomials with applications to statistical mechanics and representation theory. Ann. Probab. 43(6), 3052–3132 (2015)" href="#ref-CR26" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e57016">26</a>], combinatorics [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 2" title="E.E. Allen, The descent monomials and a basis for the diagonally symmetric polynomials. J. Algebra Combin. 3, 5–16 (1994)" href="#ref-CR2" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e57019">2</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 11" title="W.Y.C. Chen, C. Krattenthaler, A.L.B. Yang, The flagged Cauchy determinant. Graphs Combin. 21, 51–62 (2005)" href="#ref-CR11" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e57022">11</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 25" title="I.M. Gessel, Symmetric functions and P-recursiveness. J. Combin.Theory Ser. A 53, 257–285 (1990)" href="#ref-CR25" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e57025">25</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 77" title="R. Stanley, Some combinatorial properties of Jack symmetric functions. Adv. Math. 77, 76–115 (1989)" href="#ref-CR77" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e57029">77</a>], statistics [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 31" title="D.L. Hydorn, R.J. Muirhead, Polynomial estimation of eigenvalues. Commun. Stat. Theory Meth. 28 , 581–596 (1999)" href="#ref-CR31" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e57032">31</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 46" title="P. Major, The limit behavior of elementary symmetric polynomials of I.I.D. random variables when their order tends to infinity. Ann. Probab. 27, 1980–2010 (1999)" href="#ref-CR46" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e57035">46</a>], mechanics [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 26" title="V. Gorin, G. Panova, Asymptotics of symmetric polynomials with applications to statistical mechanics and representation theory. Ann. Probab. 43(6), 3052–3132 (2015)" href="#ref-CR26" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e57038">26</a>], physics [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 8" title="E.C. Boadi, Symmetric Polynomials, Combinatorics and Mathematical, Master Thesis, University of Ottawa, Canada, 2016. &#xA;www.Physicsmysite.science.uottawa.ca/hsalmasi/report/thesis-evans.pdf&#xA;&#xA;&#xA;" href="#ref-CR8" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e57041">8</a>, <a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 44" title="S.V. Lyudkovskii, Compact relationships between invariants of classical Lie groups and elementary symmetric polynomials. Theory Math. Phys. 89, 1281–1286 (1991)" href="#ref-CR44" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e57044">44</a>], discrete mathematics [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 74" title="T. Sogabe, M.E.A. El-Mikkawy, On a problem related to the Vandermonde determinant. Disc. Appl. Math. 157, 2997–2999 (2009)" href="#ref-CR74" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e57048">74</a>], geometry [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 28" title="S. Helgason, Differential Geometry and Symmetric Spaces, vol. 341 (American Mathematical Society, Providence, 2001)" href="#ref-CR28" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e57051">28</a>], information theory [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 32" title="R. Jozsa, G. Mitchison, Symmetric polynomials in information theory: entropy and subentropy. J. Math. Phys. 56(6), 062201 (2015)" href="#ref-CR32" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e57054">32</a>] and many others. For the basic properties of symmetric polynomials, see [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 45" title="I.G. Macdonald, Symmetric Functions and Hall Polynomials. Oxford Mathematical Monographs, 2nd edn. (The Clarendon Press, Oxford University Press, New York, 1995)" href="#ref-CR45" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e57057">45</a>] and for references on recent studies on symmetric polynomials, we refer to [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 21" title="M.E.A. El-Mikkawy, T. Sogabe, Notes on particular symmetric polynomials with applications. Appl. Math. Comput. 215, 3311–3317 (2010)" href="#ref-CR21" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e57060">21</a>].</p><p>In this section we shall study the convexity and concavity of symmetric functions which are defined as multivariate monomials or ratios or powers of elementary symmetric polynomials. We prove some “power” generalizations of Marcus–Lopes inequality [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 48" title="M. Marcus, L. Lopes, Symmetric functions and Hermitian matrices. Can. J. Math. 9, 305–312 (1957)" href="#ref-CR48" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e57066">48</a>] concerning elementary symmetric polynomials. Our results extend some recent results obtained by Sra [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 76" title="S. Sra, New concavity and convexity results for symmetric polynomials and their ratios. Linear Multilinear Algebra 1–9 (2018). &#xA;http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/03081087.2018.1527891&#xA;&#xA;&#xA;" href="#ref-CR76" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e57069">76</a>] and Lachaume [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 36" title="X. Lachaume, On the concavity of a sum of elementary symmetric polynomials. ArXiv e-prints, arXiv:1712.10327 (2017)" href="#ref-CR36" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e57072">36</a>].</p><p>In the second part of this section we study the convexity and concavity of the functions of the type <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f\left ( x\right ) =\left [ \sigma _{r}\left ( x\right ) \right ] ^{a}\ \left [ \sigma _{s}\left ( x\right ) \right ] ^{b}\)</span>, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(g\left ( x\right ) =\left [ \sigma _{k}\left ( x^{p}\right ) \right ] ^{a}\)</span>, and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(h\left ( x\right ) =\ln \left ( \sigma _{k}\left ( x^{p}\right ) \right )\)</span>, where <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}\)</span>.</p><p>For <i>n</i> ≥ 2 and for every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(k\in \left \{ 1,2,\ldots ,n\right \} ,\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}\)</span> we denote by <span class="mathjax-tex">\( \sigma _{k,n}\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \)</span> the <i>k</i> −th elementary symmetric polynomial. </p><div id="Equhf" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \sigma _{k,n}\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) =\underset{1\leq i_{1}&lt;i_{2}&lt;\ldots&lt;i_{k}\leq n}{\sum }x_{i_{1}}x_{i_{2}}\ldots x_{i_{k}} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> We set <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\sigma _{0,n}\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) =1\)</span>. In the case no confusion may arise we shall write <i>σ</i>
<sub><i>k</i></sub> instead <i>σ</i>
<sub><i>k</i>,<i>n</i></sub>.</p><p>If <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \mathbb {R} ,\ \mathbf {x}=\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}\)</span>, we shall denote by <b>x</b>
<sup><i>p</i></sup> the vector <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( x_{1}^{p},x_{2}^{p},\ldots ,x_{n}^{p}\right ) .\)</span>
</p><p>A well-known result concerning the concavity of <i>σ</i>
<sub><i>k</i></sub> is the Marcus–Lopes inequality [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 48" title="M. Marcus, L. Lopes, Symmetric functions and Hermitian matrices. Can. J. Math. 9, 305–312 (1957)" href="#ref-CR48" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e58179">48</a>] </p><div id="Equhg" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \frac{\sigma _{k}\left( \mathbf{x}+\mathbf{y}\right) }{\sigma _{k-1}\left( \mathbf{x}+\mathbf{y}\right) }\geq \frac{\sigma _{k}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) }{\sigma _{k-1}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) }+\frac{\sigma _{k}\left( \mathbf{y} \right) }{\sigma _{k-1}\left( \mathbf{y}\right) },\ \ \ 1\leq k\leq n,\ \ \ \ \mathbf{x,y}\in \left( 0,\infty \right) ^{n} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> The above inequality can be used to prove the following inequalities </p><div id="Equhh" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left[ \sigma _{k}\left( \mathbf{x+y}\right) \right] ^{\frac{1}{k}}\geq \left[ \sigma _{k}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) \right] ^{\frac{1}{k}}+\left[ \sigma _{k}\left( \mathbf{y}\right) \right] ^{\frac{1}{k}},\ \ \ 1\leq k\leq n,\ \ \ \ \mathbf{x,y}\in \left( 0,\infty \right) ^{n} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> and </p><div id="Equhi" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left[ \frac{\sigma _{k}\left( \mathbf{x+y}\right) }{\sigma _{k-r}\left( \mathbf{x+y}\right) }\right] ^{\frac{1}{r}}\geq \left[ \frac{\sigma _{k}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) }{\sigma _{k-r}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) }\right] ^{\frac{1}{r}}+\left[ \frac{\sigma _{k}\left( \mathbf{y}\right) }{\sigma _{k-r}\left( \mathbf{y}\right) }\right] ^{\frac{1}{r}},\mathbf{x}\in \left( 0,\infty \right) ^{n},1\leq r\leq k\leq n. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> The above inequalities can be reformulated as follows:</p>
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar36">Theorem 5.1 ([<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 48" title="M. Marcus, L. Lopes, Symmetric functions and Hermitian matrices. Can. J. Math. 9, 305–312 (1957)" href="#ref-CR48" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e59048">48</a>])</h3>
              <p><i>If</i> 1 ≤<i> r </i>≤<i> k </i>≤<i> n, then the functions</i> </p><div id="Equhj" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} f\left( \mathbf{x}\right) =\frac{\sigma _{k}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) }{ \sigma _{k-1}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) },\ \mathbf{x}\in \left( 0,\infty \right) ^{n},\\ g\left( \mathbf{x}\right) =\left[ \sigma _{k}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) \right] ^{\frac{1}{k}}, \mathbf{x}\in \left( 0,\infty \right) ^{n},\\ h\left( x\right) =\left[ \frac{\sigma _{k}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) }{\sigma _{k-r}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) }\right] ^{\frac{1}{r}}, \mathbf{x} \in \left( 0,\infty \right) ^{n} \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>are concave.</i>
              </p>
            <p>The above theorem may be used to prove concavity and convexity of several symmetric functions constructed with the help of elementary symmetric polynomials. Recent results concerning the concavity of such functions can be found in Sra [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 76" title="S. Sra, New concavity and convexity results for symmetric polynomials and their ratios. Linear Multilinear Algebra 1–9 (2018). &#xA;http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/03081087.2018.1527891&#xA;&#xA;&#xA;" href="#ref-CR76" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e59445">76</a>] and Lachaume [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 36" title="X. Lachaume, On the concavity of a sum of elementary symmetric polynomials. ArXiv e-prints, arXiv:1712.10327 (2017)" href="#ref-CR36" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e59448">36</a>]. The next three theorems present some important contributions from the above two mentioned papers.</p>
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar37">Theorem 5.2 (Sra [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 76" title="S. Sra, New concavity and convexity results for symmetric polynomials and their ratios. Linear Multilinear Algebra 1–9 (2018). &#xA;http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/03081087.2018.1527891&#xA;&#xA;&#xA;" href="#ref-CR76" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e59456">76</a>])</h3>
              <p><i>If</i> 1 ≤<i> r </i>≤<i> k </i>≤<i> n and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \left [ 0,1\right ],\)</span> <i>then the functions</i> </p><div id="Equhk" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} f\left( \mathbf{x}\right) =\left[ \frac{\sigma _{k}\left( \mathbf{x} ^{p}\right) }{\sigma _{k-1}\left( {\mathbf{x}}^{p}\right) }\right] ^{\frac{1}{p }},\ \mathbf{x}\in \left( 0,\infty \right) ^{n}\\ g\left( \mathbf{x}\right) =\left[ \frac{\sigma _{k}\left( \mathbf{x} ^{p}\right) }{\sigma _{k-r}\left( {\mathbf{x}}^{p}\right) }\right] ^{\frac{1}{ rp}},\mathbf{x}\in \left( 0,\infty \right) ^{n}\\ h\left( \mathbf{x}\right) =\left[ \sigma _{k}\left( {\mathbf{x}}^{p}\right) \right] ^{\frac{1}{kp}},\ \ \ \mathbf{x}\in \left( 0,\infty \right) ^{n} \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>are concave.</i>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar38">Theorem 5.3 (Lachaume [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 36" title="X. Lachaume, On the concavity of a sum of elementary symmetric polynomials. ArXiv e-prints, arXiv:1712.10327 (2017)" href="#ref-CR36" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e59977">36</a>])</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2<i>, a</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> ≥ 0, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(i\in \left \{ 0,1,2\right \} ,\)</span> </p><div id="Equhl" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f\left( \mathbf{x}\right) =\sqrt{a_{0}+a_{1}\sigma _{1}\left( \mathbf{x} \right) +a_{2}\sigma _{2}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) },\mathit{\ \ \ \  }\mathbf{x }\in \left( 0,\infty \right) ^{n}\mathit{\  } \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>If</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(na_{1}^{2}-2\left ( n-1\right ) a_{0}a_{2}\geq 0\)</span>
                <i>, then f is concave.</i>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar39">Theorem 5.4 (Lachaume [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 36" title="X. Lachaume, On the concavity of a sum of elementary symmetric polynomials. ArXiv e-prints, arXiv:1712.10327 (2017)" href="#ref-CR36" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e60298">36</a>])</h3>
              <p><i>Let</i> 2 ≤<i> k </i>≤<i> n</i>, <i>a </i>≥ 0, <i>b </i>≥ 0, </p><div id="Equhm" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f\left( \mathbf{x}\right) =\left( a\sigma _{k-1}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) +b\sigma _{k}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) \right) ^{\frac{1}{k}},\ \ \ \ \ \mathbf{x}\in \left( 0,\infty \right) ^{n} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>Then f is concave.</i>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar40">Theorem 5.5</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2<i>, r</i>, <i>s </i>∈{1, 2, …, <i>n</i>}<i>, r </i>&lt;<i> s,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(a\in \mathbb {R}\)</span><i>,</i> </p><div id="Equhn" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} f(x)=\left[ \displaystyle\frac{\sigma _{s}(x)}{\sigma _{r}(x)}\right] ^{a},\ x\in (0,\infty )^{n} \\ g(x)=\ln [\sigma _{s}(x)]-\ln [\sigma _{r}(x)],\ x\in (0,\infty )^{n}. \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">1∘</span>
                    
                      <p>
                        <i>If</i>
                        <span class="mathjax-tex">\(a\in \left [0,\displaystyle \frac {1}{s-r}\right ]\)</span>
                        <i>, then f is concave.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">2∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If a </i>∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0]<i>, then f is convex.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">3∘</span>
                    
                      <p>
                        <i>g is concave.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let </p><div id="Equho" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} h(x)=\left[\displaystyle\frac{\sigma _s(x)}{\sigma _r(x)}\right]^{\frac{1}{s-r }},\ x\in (0,\infty )^n. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> For every <i>t</i> ∈{<i>r</i> + 1, <i>r</i> + 2, …, <i>s</i>} let </p><div id="Equhp" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_t(x)=\displaystyle\frac{\sigma _t(x)}{\sigma _{t-1}(x)},\ x\in (0,\infty )^n. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By the Marcus–Lopes theorem (Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar36">5.1</a>) all the functions <i>g</i>
<sub><i>t</i></sub> are concave. Hence <span class="mathjax-tex">\(h=(g_{r+1}g_{r+2}\ldots g_s)^{\frac {1}{s-r}}\)</span> is concave. Suppose <span class="mathjax-tex">\(a\in \left [0,\displaystyle \frac {1}{s-r}\right ]\)</span>. Since <i>h</i> is concave, <i>f</i> = <i>h</i>
<sup><i>a</i>(<i>s</i>−<i>r</i>)</sup> and <i>a</i>(<i>s</i> − <i>r</i>) ∈ [0, 1] it follows that <i>f</i> is concave. Since </p><div id="Equhq" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g(x)=(s-r)\ln[h(x)],\ x\in (0,\infty )^n \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> it follows that <i>g</i> is concave. Suppose <i>a</i> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0]. Then <i>ag</i> is convex. Since <i>f</i> = <i>e</i>
<sup><i>ag</i></sup> it follows that <i>f</i> is convex. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar41">Theorem 5.6</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2<i>, r</i>, <i>s </i>∈{1, 2, …, <i>n</i>}<i>, r </i>&lt;<i> s ,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(a,b\in \mathbb {R}\)</span><i>,</i> </p><div id="Equhr" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x)=[\sigma _r(x)]^a [\sigma _s(x)]^b,\ x\in (0,\infty )^n. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p><i>Then f is convex if and only if b </i>≤ 0 <i>and a </i>+<i> b </i>≤ 0.</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Suppose <i>b</i> ≤ 0 and <i>a</i> + <i>b</i> ≤ 0. Then </p><div id="Equhs" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \ln [f(x)]=a\ln [\sigma _r(x)]+b\ln[\sigma _s(x)] \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equht" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =b(\ln [\sigma _s(x)]-\ln [\sigma _r(x)])+(a+b)\ln [\sigma _r(x)],\ x\in (0,\infty )^n. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By the preceding theorem <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\ln (f)\)</span> is convex, hence <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f=e^{\ln (f)}\)</span> is convex.</p>
              <p>Suppose now that <i>f</i> is convex. Let <i>x</i>
<sub><i>s</i>+1</sub> = <i>x</i>
<sub><i>s</i>+2</sub> = … = <i>x</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> = 0 in the equation that defined <i>f</i>. Then </p><div id="Equhu" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_s)=f(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_s,0,0,\ldots ,0) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equhv" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =[\sigma _r(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_s,0,0,\ldots ,0)]^a (x_1x_2\ldots x_s)^b. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Let <i>x</i>
<sub>1</sub> = <i>x</i>, <i>x</i>
<sub>2</sub> = <i>y</i>, <i>x</i>
<sub>3</sub> = <i>x</i>
<sub>4</sub> = … = <i>x</i>
<sub><i>s</i></sub> = 1 in the above equation. Then </p><div id="Equhw" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g(x,y,1,1,\ldots ,1)=[c(xy+\alpha x+\alpha y+\beta )]^a(xy)^b,\ (x,y)\in (0,\infty )^2 \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> for some <i>c</i> &gt; 0, <i>α</i> &gt; 0, <i>β</i> &gt; 0.</p>
              <p>Since <i>f</i> is convex it follows that the function </p><div id="Equhx" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} h(x,y)=(xy+\alpha x+\alpha y+\beta )^a (xy)^b,\ (x,y)\in (0,\infty )^2 \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> is convex. Since <i>h</i> is symmetric it follows that <i>h</i> is Schur convex.</p>
              <p>Let </p><div id="Equhy" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u(x,y)=axy(y+\alpha )+by(xy+\alpha x+\alpha y+\beta ),\ (x,y)\in (0,\infty )^2. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that </p><div id="Equhz" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} h^{\prime }_x(x,y)=(xy+\alpha x+\alpha y+\beta )^{a-1}(xy)^{b-1}u(x,y) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equia" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} h^{\prime }_y(x,y)=(xy+\alpha x+\alpha y+\beta )^{a-1}(xy)^{b-1}u(y,x) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equib" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u(x,y)-u(y,x)=(y-x)[(a+b)xy+b(\alpha x+\alpha y+\beta )] \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equic" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} h_x(x,y)-h_y(x,y)=(xy+\alpha x+\alpha y+\beta )^{a-1} (xy)^{b-1}(u(x,y)-u(y,x)). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Since <i>h</i> is Schur convex it follows that </p><div id="Equid" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} (x-y)(h^{\prime }_x(x,y)-h^{\prime }_y(x,y))\ge 0 \mbox{ for every } (x,y)\in (0,\infty )^2 \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> hence </p><div id="Equie" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} (a+b)xy+\alpha b(x+y)+b\beta \ge 0 \mbox{ for every } (x,y)\in (0,\infty )^2. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> If we put <i>x</i> = <i>y</i> in the preceding inequality we obtain </p><div id="Equif" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} (a+b)x^2+2\alpha bx+b\beta \le 0 \mbox{ for every } x\in (0,\infty ) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> hence <i>b</i> ≤ 0 and <i>a</i> + <i>b</i> ≤ 0. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar42">Theorem 5.7</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2<i>, r</i>, <i>s </i>∈{1, 2, …, <i>n</i>}<i>, r </i>&lt;<i> s ,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(a,b\in \mathbb {R}\)</span><i>,</i> </p><div id="Equig" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x)=[\sigma _r(x)]^a [\sigma _s(x)]^b,\ x\in (0,\infty )^n. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p><i>Then f is concave if and only if a </i>+<i> b </i>∈ [0, 1]<i>, b </i>∈ [0, 1] <i>and ar </i>+<i> bs </i>∈ [0, 1].</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Suppose that <i>f</i> is concave. Let <i>x</i>
<sub><i>s</i>+1</sub> = <i>x</i>
<sub><i>s</i>+2</sub> = … = <i>x</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> = 0 in the equation that defines <i>f</i>. Then </p><div id="Equih" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{s})=f(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{s},0,0,\ldots ,0) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equii" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =[\sigma _{r}(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{s},0,0,\ldots ,0)]^{a}(x_{1}x_{2}\ldots x_{s})^{b} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> is concave. Let <i>x</i>
<sub>1</sub> = <i>t</i>, <i>x</i>
<sub>2</sub> = <i>x</i>
<sub>3</sub> = … = <i>x</i>
<sub><i>s</i></sub> = 1 in the preceding equation. We obtain </p><div id="Equij" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g(t,1,1,\ldots ,1)=\left[ C_{n-1}^{r-1}\left( t+\displaystyle\frac{n-r}{r} \right) \right] ^{a}t^{b}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Denote <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\alpha =\displaystyle \frac {n-r}{r}\)</span> and consider the function </p><div id="Equik" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} h(t)=(t+\alpha )^{a}t^{b},\ t\in (0,\infty ). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Since <i>g</i> is concave it follows that <i>h</i> is concave. Note that </p><div id="Equil" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} h^{\prime \prime a-2}t^{b}+2ab(t+\alpha )^{a-1}t^{b-1}+b(b-1)(t+\alpha )^{a}t^{b-2} \\ =(t+\alpha )^{a-2}t^{b-2}[a(a-1)t^{2}+2abt(t+\alpha )+b(b-1)(t+\alpha )^{2}]. \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div><p> Since <i>h</i>
<sup><i>′′</i></sup>≤ 0 it follows that </p><div id="Equim" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} (a+b)(a+b-1)t^{2}+2\alpha b(a+b-1)t+(b^{2}-b)\alpha ^{2}\leq 0,\ t\in (0,\infty ). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> From the above inequality it follows that <i>b</i> ∈ [0, 1], <i>a</i> + <i>b</i> ∈ [0, 1].</p>
              <p>If in the equation that defines <i>f</i> we put <i>x</i>
<sub>1</sub> = <i>x</i>
<sub>2</sub> = … = <i>x</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> = <i>t</i> we obtain </p><div id="Equin" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} w(t)=f(t,t,\ldots ,t)=ct^{ar+bs},\ t\in (0,\infty ) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> is concave. Hence <i>ar</i> + <i>bs</i> ∈ [0, 1].</p>
              <p>Suppose now that <i>a</i> + <i>b</i> ∈ [0, 1], <i>b</i> ∈ [0, 1] and <i>ar</i> + <i>bs</i> ∈ [0, 1].</p>
              <p>Consider the functions </p><div id="Equio" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u(x)=[\sigma _r(x)]^{\frac{1}{r}},\ v(x)=\left[\displaystyle\frac{\sigma _s(x) }{\sigma _r(x)}\right]^{\frac{1}{s-r}},\ x\in (0,\infty )^n. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that </p><div id="Equip" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x)=[u(x)]^{(a+b)r}\cdot [v(x)]^{b(s-r)},\ x\in (0,\infty )^n. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar36">5.1</a> (Marcus–Lopes theorem), <i>u</i> and <i>v</i> are concave. Since </p><div id="Equiq" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} (a+b)r\ge 0,\ b(s-r)\ge 0\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div><p> and </p><div id="Equir" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} (a+b)r+b(s-r)=ar+bs\in [0,1]\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div><p> it follows that <i>f</i> is concave. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar43">Corollary 5.8</h3>
              <p><i>Let r</i>, <i>s </i>∈{1, 2, …, <i>n</i>}<i>, n </i>≥ 2<i>,</i> </p><div id="Equis" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x)=\displaystyle\frac{\sigma _r(x)}{\sigma _s(x)},\ x\in (0,\infty )^n. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">1∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>f is convex if and only if s </i>∈{<i>r</i>, <i>r </i>+ 1, …, <i>n</i>}.</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">2∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>f is concave if and only if s </i>∈{<i>r </i>− 1, <i>r</i>}.</p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar44">Theorem 5.9</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2<i>, k </i>∈{1, 2, …, <i>n</i>}<i>, p </i>≥<i> n </i>+ 1<i>,</i> </p><div id="Equit" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x)=\displaystyle\frac{\sigma _1(x^p)}{\sigma _k(x)},\ x\in (0,\infty )^n. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>Then f is convex.</i>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Consider the functions </p><div id="Equiu" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} g(x,y)=x^{p}y^{-k},\ (x,y)\in (0,\infty )^{2}, \\ u(x)=[\sigma _{1}(x^{p})]^{\frac{{1}}{{p}}},\ v(x)=[\sigma _{k}(x)]^{\frac{{1 }}{{k}}},\ x\in (0,\infty )^{n}. \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div><p> Note that <i>u</i>, <i>g</i> are convex, <i>v</i> is concave and </p><div id="Equiv" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x)=g(u(x),v(x)),\ x\in (0,\infty )^{n}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar3">2.3</a> it follows that <i>f</i> is convex. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar45">Theorem 5.10</h3>
              <p><i>Let</i> 2 ≤<i> k </i>≤<i> n, a</i>, <i>b </i>≥ 0<i>, p </i>∈ [0, 1]<i>,</i> </p><div id="Equiw" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x)=\sqrt[k]{a[\sigma _{k-1}(x)]^p+b[\sigma _k(x)]^p},\ x\in (0,\infty )^n. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>Then f is concave.</i>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let </p><div id="Equix" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{1}(x)=[\sigma _{1}(x)]^{p},\ g_{k}(x)=a+b\left[ \displaystyle\frac{\sigma _{k}(x)}{\sigma _{k-1}(x)}\right] ^{p},\ x\in (0,\infty )^{n}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> For every <i>r</i> ∈{2, 3, …, <i>k</i> − 1} let </p><div id="Equiy" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{r}(x)=\left[ \displaystyle\frac{\sigma _{r}(x)}{\sigma _{r-1}(x)}\right] ^{p},\ x\in (0,\infty )^{n}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar36">5.1</a> all <i>g</i>
<sub><i>r</i></sub> are concave. Since </p><div id="Equiz" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f=[g_{1}g_{2}\ldots g_{k}]^{\frac{{1}}{{k}}} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> it follows that <i>f</i> is concave. One can easily see that the above theorem is a generalization of Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar39">5.4</a>. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar46">Theorem 5.11</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2<i>, k </i>∈{1, 2, …, <i>n</i>}<i>,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \mathbb {R}\)</span><i>,</i> </p><div id="Equja" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x)=\ln[\sigma _k(x^p)],\ x\in (0,\infty )^n. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">1∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>f is convex if and only if p </i>∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0].</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">2∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If k </i>=<i> n, then f is concave if and only if p </i>∈ [0, <i>∞</i>).</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">3∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>k </i>≤<i> n </i>− 1<i>, then f is concave if and only if p </i>∈ [0, 1] .</p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Suppose <i>f</i> is convex. Then </p><div id="Equjb" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g(t)=f(t,t,\ldots ,t)=\ln(\alpha t^{kp})=kp\ln (\alpha t),\ t\in (0,\infty ) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> is convex. Hence <i>p</i> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0].</p>
              <p>Suppose now that <i>p</i> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0]. If <i>K</i> ⊂{1, 2, …, <i>n</i>} let </p><div id="Equjc" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u_{K}(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})=p\sum_{i\in K}\ln (x_{i}),\ (x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in (0,\infty )^{n}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that all functions <i>u</i>
<sub><i>k</i></sub> are convex. From Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar7">3.2</a> it follows that </p><div id="Equjd" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x)=\ln [\sigma _{k}(x^{p})]=\ln \left( \sum_{|K|=k}\exp (u_{K}(x))\right) ,\ x\in (0,\infty )^{n} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> is convex. Thus assertion 1<sup>∘</sup> is proved.</p>
              <p>If <i>k</i> = <i>n</i>, then </p><div id="Equje" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x)=\ln (x_1x_2\ldots x_n)^p=p\sum_{i=1}^n \ln (x_i). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Thus <i>f</i> is concave if and only if <i>p</i> ∈ [0, <i>∞</i>).</p>
              <p>Suppose now that <i>k</i> ≤ <i>n</i> − 1 and <i>f</i> is concave. Then there exist <i>α</i>, <i>β</i> &gt; 0 such that </p><div id="Equjf" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} h(t)=f(t,1,1,\ldots ,1)=\ln (\alpha t^p+\beta ),\ t\in (0,\infty ) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> is concave. Note that </p><div id="Equjg" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} h^{\prime }(t)=\displaystyle\frac{\alpha pt^{p-1}}{\alpha t^p+\beta },\ h^{\prime \prime p-2}\displaystyle\frac{(p-1)\beta -\alpha t^p}{(\alpha t^p+\beta )^2},\ t\in (0,\infty ). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> One can easily see that <i>h</i>
<sup><i>′′</i></sup>≤ 0 if and only if <i>p</i> ∈ [0, 1] . We proved that <i>f</i> is concave implies <i>p</i> ∈ [0, 1].</p>
              <p>Suppose now that <i>p</i> ∈ [0, 1]. By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar36">5.1</a> the function </p><div id="Equjh" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v(x)=[\sigma _k(x)]^{\frac{1}{k}},\ x\in (0,\infty ) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> is concave. Since </p><div id="Equji" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)=k\ln[v(x_1^p,x_2^p,\ldots ,x_n^p)],\ (x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_n)\in (0,\infty )^n \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> from Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar3">2.3</a> it follows that <i>f</i> is concave. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar47">Theorem 5.12</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2<i>,</i> </p><div id="Equjj" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} A=\{(a,p)\in \mathbb{R}^{2}\mid (ap-1)(p-1)\geq 0\mathit{\mbox{ and }}ap(ap-1)\geq 0\}, \\ B=\{(a,p)\in \mathbb{R}^{2}\mid ap\in \lbrack 0,1]\mathit{\mbox{ and }}p\in (-\infty ,1]\}. \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>For every</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\((a,p)\in \mathbb {R}^{2}\)</span>
                <i>consider the functions</i>
                </p><div id="Equjk" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} s_{p}(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})=x_{1}^{p}+x_{2}^{p}+\ldots +x_{n}^{p},\ (x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in (0,\infty )^{n}, \\ f(x)=[s_{p}(x)]^{a},\ x\in (0,\infty )^{n}. \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">1∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>f is convex if and only if</i> (<i>a</i>, <i>p</i>) ∈<i> A.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">2∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>f is concave if and only if</i> (<i>a</i>, <i>p</i>) ∈<i> B.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>The proof of the theorem will be made in two steps: the case <i>n</i> = 2 and the case <i>n</i> ≥ 2.</p>
              <p>Suppose that <i>n</i> = 2. Let <i>u</i>(<i>x</i>, <i>y</i>) = <i>x</i>
<sup><i>p</i></sup> + <i>y</i>
<sup><i>p</i></sup>, (<i>x</i>, <i>y</i>) ∈ (0, <i>∞</i>)<sup>2</sup>. Then <i>f</i> = <i>u</i>
<sup><i>a</i></sup>. Note that </p><div id="Equjl" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} f_{x}^{\prime }=au^{a-1}u_{x}^{\prime },\ f_{y}^{\prime }=au^{a-1}u_{y}^{\prime },\ f_{xx}^{\prime \prime }=a(a-1)u^{a-2}u_{x}^{\prime 2}+au^{a-1}u_{xx}^{\prime \prime } \\ f_{xy}^{\prime \prime }=a(a-1)u^{a-2}u_{x}^{\prime }u_{y}^{\prime } \\ f_{yy}^{\prime \prime }=a(a-1)u^{a-2}u_{y}^{\prime 2}+au^{a-1}u_{yy}^{\prime \prime }. \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div><p> Denote by <i>H</i> the determinant of the Hessian matrix of <i>f</i>. Then </p><div id="Equjm" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} H=f_{xx}^{\prime \prime }f_{yy}^{\prime \prime }-(f_{xy}^{\prime \prime })^{2} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equjn" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =a^{2}u^{2a-4}[(a-1)u_{x}^{\prime 2}+uu_{xx}^{\prime \prime }][(a-1)u_{y}^{\prime 2}+uu_{yy}^{\prime \prime }]-a^{2}u^{2a-4}(a-1)^{2}u_{x}^{\prime 2}u_{y}^{\prime 2} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equjo" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =a^{2}u^{2a-3}[(a-1)(u_{x}^{\prime 2}u_{yy}^{\prime \prime }+u_{y}^{\prime 2}u_{xx}^{\prime \prime })+uu_{xx}^{\prime \prime }u_{yy}^{\prime \prime }]. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Denote </p><div id="Equjp" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} H_{1}=\displaystyle\frac{H}{a^{2}u^{2a-3}}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Then </p><div id="Equjq" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} H_{1}(x,y)=(a-1)[p^{2}x^{2p-2}p(p-1)y^{p-2}+p^{2}y^{2p-2}p(p-1)x^{p-2}] \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equjr" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} +(x^{p}+y^{p})p^{2}(p-1)^{2}x^{p-2}y^{p-2}=p^{2}x^{p-2}y^{p-2}(x^{p}+y^{p})(ap-1)(p-1) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equjs" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{xx}^{\prime \prime }(x,y)=a(x^{p}+y^{p})^{a-2}[(a-1)p^{2}x^{2p-2}+(x^{p}+y^{p})p(p-1)x^{p-2}] \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equjt" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =apx^{p-2}(x^{p}+y^{p})^{a-2}[(ap-1)x^{p}+(p-1)y^{p}]. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f_{xx}^{\prime \prime }\geq 0\)</span> on (0, <i>∞</i>)<sup>2</sup> if and only if (<i>a</i>, <i>p</i>) ∈ <i>A</i>. This also implies that <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f_{yy}^{\prime \prime }\geq 0\)</span> and <i>H</i> ≥ 0. Hence assertion 1<sup>∘</sup> is proved.</p>
              <p>Note that <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f_{xx}^{\prime \prime }\leq 0\)</span> on (0, <i>∞</i>)<sup>2</sup> if and only if (<i>a</i>, <i>p</i>) ∈ <i>B</i>. This also implies that <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f_{yy}^{\prime \prime }\leq 0\)</span> and <i>H</i> ≥ 0. Hence assertion 2<sup>∘</sup> is proved.</p>
              <p>In the following we shall study the case <i>n</i> ≥ 2. Let </p><div id="Equju" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v(x,y)=(x^p+y^p)^a,\ (x,y)\in (0,\infty )^2. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that <i>v</i>(<i>x</i>, <i>y</i>) equals to </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">(a)</span>

<p>the limit of <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>, <i>y</i>, <i>x</i>
<sub>3</sub>, <i>x</i>
<sub>3</sub>, …, <i>x</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub>) when <i>p</i> &gt; 0 and <i>x</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> → 0 for <i>i</i> ∈{3, 4, …, <i>n</i>};</p>

</li>
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">(b)</span>

<p>the limit of <i>f</i>(<i>x</i>, <i>y</i>, <i>x</i>
<sub>3</sub>, <i>x</i>
<sub>4</sub>, …, <i>x</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub>) when <i>p</i> &lt; 0 and <i>x</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> → +<i>∞</i> for <i>i</i> ∈{3, 4, …, <i>n</i>}.</p>

</li>
</ol>

              <p>From the above remark it follows that if <i>f</i> is convex (resp. concave) then <i>v</i> is convex (resp. concave).</p>
              <p>Hence if <i>f</i> is convex (resp. concave) then (<i>a</i>, <i>p</i>) ∈ <i>A</i> (resp. (<i>a</i>, <i>p</i>) ∈ <i>B</i> ).</p>
              <p>Suppose now that (<i>a</i>, <i>p</i>) ∈ <i>A</i>. This is equivalent with the fact that (<i>a</i>, <i>p</i>) satisfies one of the following two conditions: </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">(i)</span>

<p><i>ap</i> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0] and <i>p</i> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 1];</p>

</li>
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">(ii)</span>

<p><i>ap</i> ∈ [1, <i>∞</i>) and <i>p</i> ∈ [1, <i>∞</i>).</p>

</li>
</ol>

              <p>If (i) holds then <i>g</i> = (<i>s</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub>)<sup>1∕<i>p</i></sup> is concave hence <span class="mathjax-tex">\(h=\ln (g)\)</span> is concave. Since <i>aph</i> is convex and <i>f</i> = <i>e</i>
<sup><i>aph</i></sup> it follows that <i>f</i> is convex.</p>
              <p>If (ii) holds then <i>g</i> is convex hence <i>f</i> = <i>g</i>
<sup><i>ap</i></sup> is convex. Thus assertion 1<sup>∘</sup> is proved.</p>
              <p>Suppose now that (<i>a</i>, <i>p</i>) ∈ <i>B</i>. It follows that <i>g</i> is concave hence <i>f</i> = <i>g</i>
<sup><i>ap</i></sup> is concave. Thus assertion 2<sup>∘</sup> is proved. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar48">Lemma 5.13</h3>
              <p><i>Let</i> 2 ≤<i> k </i>≤<i> n, ap </i>&gt; 0<i>,</i> </p><div id="Equjv" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x)=[\sigma _k(x^p)]^a,\ x\in (0,\infty )^n. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>Then f is not convex.</i>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>If <i>p</i> &gt; 0 let <i>x</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> → 0, <i>i</i> ∈{<i>k</i> + 1, <i>k</i> + 2, …, <i>n</i>} in the equation that defines <i>f</i>. It follows that </p><div id="Equjw" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} f(x_1,x_n,\ldots ,x_k,0,0,\ldots ,0) =[\sigma _k(x_1^p,x_2^p,\ldots ,x_k^p,0,0,\ldots ,0)]^a \\ =(x_1x_2\ldots x_k)^{ap},\ (x_1,x_2,\ldots ,x_k)\in (0,\infty )^k. \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div><p> Since <i>k</i> ≥ 2, the multivariate monomial from the right-hand side of the above equation is not convex. Hence <i>f</i> is not convex.</p>
              <p>If <i>p</i> &lt; 0 let <i>x</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> → +<i>∞</i>, <i>i</i> ∈{<i>k</i> + 1, <i>k</i> + 2, …, <i>n</i>} in the equation that defines <i>f</i>. We obtain that </p><div id="Equjx" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} f(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{k},+\infty ,+\infty ,\ldots ,+\infty )=(x_{1}x_{2}\ldots x_{k})^{ap}, \\ (x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{k})\in (0,\infty )^{k}. \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div><p> Since the multivariate monomial from the right-hand side of the above equation is not convex it follows that <i>f</i> is not convex. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar49">Lemma 5.14</h3>
              <p><i>Let</i> 1 ≤<i> k </i>≤<i> n </i>− 1<i>,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(a,p\in \mathbb {R}\)</span><i>, ap</i>≠0<i>,</i> </p><div id="Equjy" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x)=[\sigma _k(x^p)]^a,\ x\in (0,\infty )^n. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p><i>If</i> (<i>ap </i>− 1)(<i>p </i>− 1) &lt; 0<i>, then f is neither convex nor concave.</i>
</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Note that there exist <i>α</i>, <i>β</i> &gt; 0 such that </p><div id="Equjz" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g(t)=f(t,1,1,\ldots ,1)=(\alpha t^{p}+\beta )^{a},\ t\in (0,\infty ). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> The first two derivatives of <i>g</i> are: </p><div id="Equka" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g^{\prime p}+\beta )^{a-1}, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equkb" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g^{\prime \prime p}+\beta )^{a-2}t^{p-2}[\alpha (ap-1)t^{p}+\beta (p-1)],\ t\in (0,\infty ). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> One can easily see that (<i>ap</i> − 1)(<i>p</i> − 1) &lt; 0 implies that <i>g</i>
<sup><i>′′</i></sup> has no constant sign. Consequently <i>f</i> is neither convex nor concave. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar50">Theorem 5.15</h3>
              <p><i>Let</i> 2 ≤<i> k </i>≤<i> n </i>− 1<i>,</i> </p><div id="Equkc" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} A_1=\{(a,p)\in \mathbb{R}^2\mid a\in (-\infty ,0) \mathit{\mbox{ and }} p\in [0,1]\}, \\ A_2=\{(a,p)\in \mathbb{R}^2\mid a\in [0,\infty ) \mathit{\mbox{ and }} p\in (-\infty ,0]\},\ A=A_1\cup A_2, \\ f(x)=[\sigma _k(x^p)]^a,\ x\in (0,\infty )^n. \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div>

              <p><i>Then f is convex if and only if</i> (<i>a</i>, <i>p</i>) ∈<i> A.</i>
</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Suppose (<i>a</i>, <i>p</i>) ∈ <i>A</i>. Let </p><div id="Equkd" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g(x)=\ln [\sigma _{k}(x^{p})],\ x\in (0,\infty )^{n}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> If (<i>a</i>, <i>p</i>) ∈ <i>A</i>
<sub>1</sub> then by Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar46">5.11</a>, <i>g</i> is concave. Hence <i>ag</i> and <i>f</i> = <i>e</i>
<sup><i>ag</i></sup> are convex.</p>
              <p>If (<i>a</i>, <i>p</i>) ∈ <i>A</i>
<sub>2</sub> then by Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar46">5.11</a>, <i>g</i> is convex. Hence <i>ag</i> and <i>f</i> = <i>e</i>
<sup><i>ag</i></sup> are convex.</p>
              <p>We proved that (<i>a</i>, <i>p</i>) ∈ <i>A</i> implies <i>f</i> is convex. Suppose now that <i>f</i> is convex. Then there exists <i>a</i> &gt; 0 such that </p><div id="Equke" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} h(t)=f(t,t,\ldots ,t)=\alpha t^{akp},\ t\in (0,\infty ). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Since <i>f</i> is convex it follows that <i>akp</i> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0] ∪ [1, <i>∞</i>). By Lemma <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar49">5.14</a> it follows that <i>ap</i> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0].</p>
              <p>We shall study two cases: <i>a</i> ≤ 0 and <i>a</i> ≥ 0.</p>
              <p>If <i>a</i> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0], then <i>p</i> ∈ [0, <i>∞</i>). By Lemma <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar49">5.14</a> it follows that <i>p</i> ≤ 1. Hence <i>p</i> ∈ [0, 1] and (<i>a</i>, <i>p</i>) ∈ <i>A</i>
<sub>1</sub>.</p>
              <p>If <i>a</i> ∈ [0, <i>∞</i>), then <i>p</i> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0], hence (<i>a</i>, <i>p</i>) ∈ <i>A</i>
<sub>2</sub>. Because <i>f</i> is convex it follows (<i>a</i>, <i>p</i>) ∈ <i>A</i>. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar51">Definition 5.16</h3>
              <p>Let <i>A</i> be a subset of <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbb {R}^{\ast }=\mathbb { R}\setminus \{0\}\)</span>. A function <i>u</i> : [0, <i>∞</i>)<sup><i>n</i></sup> → [0, <i>∞</i>) will be called <i>A</i>-<i>concave</i> if for every <i>p</i> ∈ <i>A</i> the function </p><div id="Equkf" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u_{p}(x)=[u(x^{p})]^{\frac{1}{p}},\ x\in (0,\infty )^{n} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> is concave.</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar52">Lemma 5.17</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2<i>, A </i>= [−1, 0) ∪ (0, <i>∞</i>) <i>and</i> </p><div id="Equkg" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x)=\displaystyle\frac{\sigma _n(x)}{\sigma _{n-1}(x)},\ x\in (0,\infty )^n. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>Then f is A-concave.</i>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>From Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar7">3.2</a> it follows that the function </p><div id="Equkh" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{p}(x)=[\sigma _{1}(x^{p})]^{\frac{1}{p}},\ x\in (0,\infty )^{n} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> is concave for every <i>p</i> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0) ∪ (0, 1]. Let <i>q</i> = −<i>p</i>. Then </p><div id="Equki" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{p}(x)=[\sigma _{1}(x^{-q})]^{-\frac{1}{q}}=\left[ \displaystyle\frac{ \sigma _{n}(x^{q})}{\sigma _{n-1}(x^{q})}\right] ^{\frac{1}{q}}=[f(x^{q})]^{ \frac{1}{q}},\ x\in (0,\infty )^{n}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Since <i>p</i> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0) ∪ (0, 1] is equivalent with <i>q</i> ∈ <i>A</i> it follows that <i>f</i> is <i>A</i>-concave. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar53">Lemma 5.18</h3>
              <p><i>Let B </i>= (−<i>∞</i>, 0) ∪ (0, 1] <i>and u</i>, <i>v </i>: (0, <i>∞</i>)<sup><i>n</i></sup> → (0, <i>∞</i>) <i>be two B-concave functions. Then the function</i> </p><div id="Equkj" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} w=\displaystyle\frac{uv}{u+v} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>is B-concave.</i>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Note that: </p><div id="Equkk" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} w_p(x)=[w(x^p)]^{\frac{1}{p}} =\left[\displaystyle\frac{u(x^p)v(x^p)}{ u(x^p)+v(x^p)}\right]^{\frac{1}{p}} \\ =\displaystyle\frac{[u(x^p)]^{\frac{1}{p}}[v(x^p)]^{\frac{1}{p}}} {\{[u(x^p)^{\frac{1}{p}}]^p+[v(x^p)^{\frac{1}{p}}]^p\}^{\frac{1}{p}}} =\displaystyle\frac{ u_p(x)v_p(x)}{\{[u_p(x)]^p+[v_p(x)]^p\}^{\frac{1}{p}}}. \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div><p> If <i>p</i> ∈ <i>B</i> then <i>u</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> and <i>v</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> are concave. By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar35">4.20</a> it follows that <i>w</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is concave for every <i>p</i> ∈ <i>B</i>. Hence <i>w</i> is B-concave. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar54">Lemma 5.19</h3>
              <p>
                <i>Let</i>
                </p><div id="Equkl" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} B=(-\infty ,0)\cup (0,1],\ u_{1},u_{2},\ldots ,u_{m}:(0,\infty )^{n}\rightarrow (0,\infty ). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p><i>If u</i>
<sub>1</sub>, <i>u</i>
<sub>2</sub>, …, <i>u</i>
<sub><i>m</i></sub> <i>are B-concave, then u </i>=<i> u</i>
<sub>1</sub> +<i> u</i>
<sub>2</sub> + … +<i> u</i>
<sub><i>m</i></sub> <i>is B-concave.</i>
</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>For every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \mathbb {R}^{\ast }\)</span> let </p><div id="Equkm" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v_{i,p}(x)=[u_{i}(x^{p})]^{\frac{1}{p}},\ x\in (0,\infty )^{n},\ i=1,2,\ldots ,m. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that </p><div id="Equkn" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} w_{p}(x)=[u(x^{p})]^{\frac{1}{p}}=\left( \sum_{i=1}^{m}[v_{i,p}(x)]^{p}\right) ^{\frac{1}{p}},\ x\in (0,\infty )^{n}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Since <i>v</i>
<sub><i>i</i>,<i>p</i></sub> are concave for every <i>i</i> ∈{1, 2, …, <i>m</i>} and <i>p</i> ∈ <i>B</i>, from Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar7">3.2</a> it follows that <i>w</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is concave for every <i>p</i> ∈ <i>B</i>. Consequently <i>u</i> is <i>B</i>-concave. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar55">Lemma 5.20</h3>
              <p><i>Let</i> 2 ≤<i> k </i>≤<i> n and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\phi :(0,\infty )^{2}\rightarrow \mathbb {R}\)</span><i>,</i> </p><div id="Equko" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \phi (x,y)=\displaystyle\frac{xy}{x+y},\ (x,y)\in (0,\infty )^{2}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p><i>If</i> <b>x</b> = (<i>x</i>
<sub>1</sub>, <i>x</i>
<sub>2</sub>, …, <i>x</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub>) ∈ (0, <i>∞</i>)<sup><i>n</i></sup><i>, i </i>∈{1, 2, …, <i>n</i>} <i>we denote</i> </p><div id="Equkp" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} {\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{\prime }=(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,\widehat{x}_{i},\ldots ,x_{n})\in (0,\infty )^{n-1}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>Here the notation</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\widehat {x}_{i}\)</span>
                <i>means that x</i>
                <sub>
                  <i>i</i>
                </sub>
                <i>is missing from the components of the vector.</i>
              </p>
              <p>
                <i>Then the following equality holds:</i>
                </p><div id="Equkq" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \displaystyle\frac{\sigma _{k,n}(\mathbf{x})}{\sigma _{k-1,n}(\mathbf{x})}= \displaystyle\frac{1}{k}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\phi \left( x_{i},\displaystyle\frac{ \sigma _{k-1,n-1}({\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{\prime })}{\sigma _{k-2,n-1}(\mathbf{x} _{i}^{\prime })}\right) ,\ \mathbf{x}=(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})\in (0,\infty )^{n}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>One can easily see that for every <i>i</i> ∈{1, 2, …, <i>n</i>} we have </p><div id="Equkr" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \sigma _{k,n}(\mathbf{x})=x_{i}\sigma _{k-1,n-1}({\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{\prime })+\sigma _{k,n-1}({\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{\prime }) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> and </p><div id="Equks" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \sum_{i=1}^{n}\sigma _{k,n-1}({\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{\prime })=(n-k)\sigma _{k,n}( \mathbf{x}). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that </p><div id="Equkt" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \displaystyle\frac{\sigma _{k,n}(\mathbf{x})}{\sigma _{k-1,n}(\mathbf{x})}= \displaystyle\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\displaystyle\frac{\sigma _{k,n}( \mathbf{x})}{\sigma _{k-1,n}(\mathbf{x})}=\displaystyle\frac{1}{n} \sum_{i=1}^{n}\displaystyle\frac{x_{i}\sigma _{k-1,n-1}(\mathbf{x} _{i}^{\prime })+\sigma _{k,n-1}({\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{\prime })}{\sigma _{k-1,n}( \mathbf{x})} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equku" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =\displaystyle\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\displaystyle\frac{x_{i}\sigma _{k-1,n-1}({\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{\prime })}{\sigma _{k-1,n}(\mathbf{x})}+ \displaystyle\frac{1}{n\sigma _{k-1,n}(\mathbf{x})}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sigma _{k,n-1}({\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{\prime }) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equkv" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =\displaystyle\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\displaystyle\frac{x_{i}\sigma _{k-1,n-1}(x_{i}^{\prime })}{\sigma _{k-1,n}(x)}+\displaystyle\frac{ (n-k)\sigma _{k,n}(x)}{n\sigma _{k-1,n}(x)}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> From the above sequence of equalities we obtain </p><div id="Equkw" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \displaystyle\frac{\sigma _{k,n}(\mathbf{x})}{\sigma _{k-1,n}(\mathbf{x})}- \displaystyle\frac{(n-k)\sigma _{k,n}(\mathbf{x})}{n\sigma _{k-1,n}(\mathbf{x })}=\displaystyle\frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\displaystyle\frac{x_{i}\sigma _{k-1,n-1}({\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{\prime })}{\sigma _{k-1,n}(\mathbf{x})} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> hence </p><div id="Equkx" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \displaystyle\frac{\sigma _{k,n}(\mathbf{x})}{\sigma _{k-1,n}(\mathbf{x})}= \displaystyle\frac{1}{k}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\displaystyle\frac{x_{i}\sigma _{k-1,n-1}({\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{\prime })}{x_{i}\sigma _{k-2,n-1}(\mathbf{x} _{i}^{\prime })+\sigma _{k-1,n-1}({\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{\prime })} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equky" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =\displaystyle\frac{1}{k}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\phi \left( x_{i},\displaystyle\frac{ \sigma _{k-1,n-1}({\mathbf{x}}_{i}^{\prime })}{\sigma _{k-2,n-1}(\mathbf{x} _{i}^{\prime })}\right) . \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar56">Theorem 5.21</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2<i>,</i> 1 ≤<i> k </i>≤<i> n </i>− 1<i>, C </i>= [−1, 0) ∪ (0, 1]<i>,</i> </p><div id="Equkz" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{k,n}(\mathbf{x})=\displaystyle\frac{\sigma _{k,n}(\mathbf{x})}{\sigma _{k-1,n}(\mathbf{x})},\ \mathbf{x}\in (0,\infty )^{n}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p><i>Then f</i>
<sub><i>k</i>,<i>n</i></sub> <i>is C-concave. We supposed that σ</i>
<sub>0,<i>n</i></sub>(<b>x</b>) ≡ 1.</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>We shall prove that if <i>k</i> ≥ 1 then <i>f</i>
<sub><i>k</i>,<i>n</i></sub> is <i>C</i> -concave for every <i>n</i> ≥ <i>k</i>. If <i>k</i> = 1 then <i>f</i>
<sub><i>k</i>,<i>n</i></sub>(<b>x</b>) = <i>σ</i>
<sub>1,<i>n</i></sub>(<b>x</b>), <b>x</b> ∈ (0, <i>∞</i>)<sup><i>n</i></sup>. From Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar7">3.2</a> it follows that </p><div id="Equla" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} w_{p}(\mathbf{x})=[f_{k,n}({\mathbf{x}}^{p})]^{\frac{1}{p}}=[\sigma _{1}( {\mathbf{x}}^{p})]^{\frac{1}{p}},\ \mathbf{x}\in (0,\infty )^{n} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> is concave for every <i>p</i> ∈ <i>C</i>. This implies that <i>f</i>
<sub><i>k</i>,<i>p</i></sub> is <i>C</i>-concave.</p>
              <p>Suppose now that <i>f</i>
<sub><i>k</i>−1,<i>n</i></sub> is <i>C</i>-concave. By Lemma <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar55">5.20</a> it follows that </p><div id="Equlb" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{k,n+1}(\mathbf{x})=\displaystyle\frac{1}{k}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\phi (x_{i},f_{k-1,n}(\mathbf{x})). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By Lemma <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar53">5.18</a> all the functions </p><div id="Equlc" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \psi _{i}(\mathbf{x})=\phi (x_{i},f_{k-1,n}(\mathbf{x})),\ \mathbf{x}\in (0,\infty )^{n} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> are <i>C</i>-concave. By Lemma <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar54">5.19</a> it follows that <i>f</i>
<sub><i>k</i>+1,<i>n</i></sub> is <i>C</i>-concave. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar57">Theorem 5.22</h3>
              <p><i>let</i> 2 ≤<i> k </i>≤<i> n, p </i>∈ [−1, 0) ∪ (0, 1]<i>,</i> </p><div id="Equld" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(\mathbf{x})=[\sigma _{k}({\mathbf{x}}^{p})]^{1/kp},\ \mathbf{x}\in (0,\infty )^{n}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>Then f is concave.</i>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <i>σ</i>
<sub>0</sub>(<b>x</b>) ≡ 1. For every <i>r</i> ∈{1, 2, …, <i>n</i>} let </p><div id="Equle" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{r}(\mathbf{x})=\left[ \displaystyle\frac{\sigma _{r}({\mathbf{x}}^{p})}{ \sigma _{r-1}({\mathbf{x}}^{p})}\right] ^{\frac{1}{p}},\ \mathbf{x}\in (0,\infty )^{n}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By the preceding theorem, <i>g</i>
<sub><i>r</i></sub> is concave for every <i>r</i> ∈{1, 2, …, <i>n</i>}. Since </p><div id="Equlf" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f=(g_{1}g_{2}\ldots g_{k})^{\frac{1}{k}} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> it follows that <i>f</i> is concave. □</p>
            <p>In the following we shall recall two definitions.</p>
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar58">Definition 5.23</h3>
              <p>Let <i>E</i> be a linear space over <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbb {R}\)</span>. A subset <i>C</i> of <i>E</i> is called a <i>convex cone</i> if the following conditions are verified: </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">(i)</span>

<p>If <i>x</i> ∈ <i>C</i> then <i>tx</i> ∈ <i>C</i> for every <i>t</i> ∈ [0, <i>∞</i>).</p>

</li>
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">(ii)</span>

<p>If <i>x</i>, <i>y</i> ∈ <i>C</i> then <i>x</i> + <i>y</i> ∈ <i>C</i>.</p>

</li>
</ol>

            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar59">Definition 5.24</h3>
              <p>Let <i>E</i> be a linear space over <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbb {R}\)</span>, <i>C</i> be a convex cone in <i>E</i> and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f:C\to \mathbb {R}\)</span>. The function <i>f</i> is called <i>positive homogeneous</i> of degree <span class="mathjax-tex">\(a\in \mathbb {R}\)</span> if </p><div id="Equlg" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(tx)=t^af(x) \mbox{ for every } t\in (0,\infty ) \mbox{ and } x\in C. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar60">Theorem 5.25</h3>
              <p><i>Let E be a real linear space, C be a convex cone in E and f </i>:<i> C </i>→ [0, <i>∞</i>) <i>be a positive homogeneous function of degree one. Then the following assertions hold:</i> </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">1∘</span>

<p><i>If f is quasi-convex, then f is convex.</i>
</p>

</li>
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">2∘</span>

<p><i>If f is quasi-concave, then f is concave.</i>
</p>

</li>
</ol>

            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <i>x</i>, <i>y</i> ∈ <i>C</i> and (<i>a</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub>)<sub><i>n</i>≥1</sub>, (<i>b</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub>)<sub><i>n</i>≥1</sub> be two sequences of positive numbers such that </p><div id="Equlh" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \lim\limits_{n\to \infty }a_n=f(x) \mbox{ and } \lim\limits_{n\to \infty }b_n=f(y). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Suppose <i>f</i> is quasi-convex. Then </p><div id="Equli" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x+y)=(a_n+b_n)f\left(\displaystyle\frac{x+y}{a_n+b_n}\right) =f\left( \displaystyle\frac{a_n}{a_n+b_n}\cdot \displaystyle\frac{x}{a_n} + \displaystyle\frac{b_n}{a_n+b_n}\cdot \displaystyle\frac{y}{b_n}\right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equlj" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \le (a_n+b_n)\max\left(f\left(\displaystyle\frac{x}{a_n}\right), f\left( \displaystyle\frac{y}{b_n}\right)\right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equlk" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =(a_n+b_n)\max\left(\displaystyle\frac{f(x)}{a_n},\displaystyle\frac{f(y)}{ b_n}\right),\ n\ge 1. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> If we let <i>n</i> →<i>∞</i> we obtain </p><div id="Equll" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x+y)\le f(x)+f(y). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Since <i>f</i> is positive homogeneous of degree one it follows that <i>f</i> is convex.</p>
              <p>Suppose now that <i>f</i> is quasi-concave. Then </p><div id="Equlm" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} f(x+y)=(a_{n}+b_{n})f\left( \displaystyle\frac{a_{n}}{a_{n}+b_{n}}\cdot \displaystyle\frac{x}{a_{n}}+\displaystyle\frac{b_{n}}{a_{n}+b_{n}}\cdot \displaystyle\frac{y}{b_{n}}\right) \\ \geq (a_{n}+b_{n})\min \left( \displaystyle\frac{f(x)}{a_{n}},\displaystyle \frac{f(y)}{b_{n}}\right) . \end{gathered} $$
</span></div></div><p> If we let <i>n</i> →<i>∞</i> we obtain </p><div id="Equln" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x+y)\geq f(x)+f(y).\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div><p> Since <i>f</i> is positive homogeneous of degree one it follows that <i>f</i> is concave. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar61">Corollary 5.26</h3>
              <p><i>Let E be a real linear space, let C be a convex cone in E,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(a\in \mathbb {R}^*=\mathbb {R}\setminus \{0\}\)</span> <i>and let</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\( u_i:C\to \mathbb {R}_+\)</span><i>, i </i>= 1, 2, …, <i>n, be positive homogeneous functions of degree a. Consider the function:</i> </p><div id="Equlo" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(x)=\left(\sum_{i=1}^n u_i(x)\right)^{\frac{1}{a}},\ x\in C.\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">1∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If all u</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> <i>are convex and a </i>∈ [1, <i>∞</i>)<i>, then f is convex.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">2∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If all u</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> <i>are concave and a </i>∈ (0, 1]<i>, then f is concave.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">3∘</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If all u</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> <i>are convex and a </i>∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0)<i>, then f is concave.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Note that <i>f</i> is positive homogeneous of degree one. Let </p><div id="Equlp" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \phi (t)=t^a,\ t\in (0,\infty ),\ u=\sum_{i=1}^n u_i. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Suppose that all <i>u</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> are convex and <i>a</i> ∈ [1, <i>∞</i>). Then <i>u</i> is convex, hence <i>u</i> is quasi-convex. Since <i>ϕ</i> is increasing and <i>f</i> = <i>ϕ</i>(<i>u</i>) it follows that <i>f</i> is quasi-convex. By the preceding theorem it follows that <i>f</i> is convex.</p>
              <p>Suppose now that all <i>u</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> are concave and <i>a</i> ∈ (0, 1]. Then <i>u</i> is concave, hence <i>u</i> is quasi-concave. Since <i>ϕ</i> is increasing and <i>f</i> = <i>ϕ</i>(<i>u</i>) it follows that <i>f</i> is quasi-concave. By the preceding theorem <i>f</i> is concave.</p>
              <p>Suppose now that all <i>u</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> are convex and <i>a</i> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0). Then <i>u</i> is convex, hence quasi-convex. Since <i>ϕ</i> is decreasing it follows that <i>f</i> = <i>ϕ</i>(<i>u</i>) is quasi-concave. By the preceding theorem <i>f</i> is concave. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Remark</h3>
              <p>If in the preceding corollary we take <span class="mathjax-tex">\(E=\mathbb {R}^{n}\)</span>, <i>C</i> = [0, <i>∞</i>)<sup><i>n</i></sup>, </p><div id="Equlq" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u_{i}=(x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n})=x_{i}^{p},\ (x_{1},\ldots ,x_{n})\in C, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> then the validity of assertions 1<sup>∘</sup> and 2<sup>∘</sup> of Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar7">3.2</a> follows.</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar62">Theorem 5.27</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2<i>,</i> 1 ≤<i> k </i>≤<i> n,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(a,p\in \mathbb {R}\)</span><i>,</i> </p><div id="Equlr" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f(\mathbf{x})=[\sigma _{k}({\mathbf{x}}^{p})]^{a},\ \mathbf{x}\in (0,\infty )^{n}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p><i>Then f is concave if and only if</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(ap\in \left [ 0,\displaystyle \frac {1}{k} \right ] \)</span> <i>and p </i>∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 1].</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Suppose that <i>f</i> is concave. Then </p><div id="Equls" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g(t)=f(t,t,\ldots ,t)=ct^{akp},\ t\in (0,\infty ) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> is concave. Hence <i>akp</i> ∈ [0, 1]. By Lemma <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar49">5.14</a> it follows that <i>p</i> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 1].</p>
              <p>Suppose now that <span class="mathjax-tex">\(ap\in \left [ 0,\displaystyle \frac {1}{k}\right ] \)</span> and <i>p</i> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 1]. Let </p><div id="Eqult" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} h(\mathbf{x})=[\sigma _{k}({\mathbf{x}}^{p})]^{\frac{1}{kp}},\ \mathbf{x}\in (0,\infty )^{n},\ \phi (t)=t^{\frac{1}{kp}},\ t\in (0,\infty ). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>If <i>p</i> ∈ [0, 1] then by Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar57">5.22</a>, <i>h</i> is concave, hence <i>f</i> = <i>h</i>
<sup><i>akp</i></sup> is concave.</p>
              <p>If <i>p</i> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0] then <i>u</i>(<b>x</b>) = <i>σ</i>
<sub><i>k</i></sub>(<b>x</b>
<sup><i>p</i></sup>), <b>x</b> ∈ (0, <i>∞</i>)<sup><i>n</i></sup> is convex, hence quasi-convex. Since <i>ϕ</i> is decreasing it follows that <i>h</i> = <i>ϕ</i>(<i>u</i>) is quasi-concave. Note that <i>h</i> is positive homogeneous of degree one. From Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar60">5.25</a> it follows that <i>h</i> is concave. Since <i>akp</i> ∈ [0, 1] it follows that <i>f</i> = <i>h</i>
<sup><i>akp</i></sup> is concave. □</p>
            </div></div></section><section data-title="A Generalization of Davis Theorem and Applications"><div class="c-article-section" id="Sec6-section"><h2 id="Sec6" class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item"><span class="c-article-section__title-number">6 </span>A Generalization of Davis Theorem and Applications</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Sec6-content"><p>In this section we shall give a generalization of Davis theorem and we shall make various applications of this theorem. Let <i>n</i> ≥ 2.</p><p>Recall that a square matrix with complex entries <span class="mathjax-tex">\(A=\left ( a_{ij}\right ) \)</span> is called Hermitian if <i>A</i> = <i>A</i>
<sup>∗</sup>. Here by <i>A</i>
<sup>∗</sup> we denoted the adjoint of <i>A</i>. We have <span class="mathjax-tex">\(A^{\ast }=\overset {\_}{A}^{T}=\left ( \overset {\_}{a} _{ji}\right ) .\)</span>
</p><p>All eigenvalues of a Hermitian matrix are real. A Hermitian matrix is called positive definite if all its eigenvalues are positive. A square matrix <i>Q</i> is called unitary if <i>QQ</i>
<sup>∗</sup> = <i>Q</i>
<sup>∗</sup><i>Q</i> = <i>I</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub>. If <i>Q</i> is a real matrix, then <i>Q</i> is unitary if and only if <i>Q</i> is an orthogonal matrix, that is <i>QQ</i>
<sup><i>T</i></sup> = <i>Q</i>
<sup><i>T</i></sup><i>Q</i> = <i>I</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub>.</p><p>Denote by <span class="mathjax-tex">\(H_{n}\left ( \mathbb {C} \right ) \)</span> the set of <i>n</i> × <i>n</i> hermitian matrices, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(P_{n}\left ( \mathbb {C} \right ) \)</span> the set of <i>n</i> × <i>n</i> Hermitian matrices with positive eigenvalues, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(U_{n}\left ( \mathbb {C} \right ) \)</span> the set of <i>n</i> × <i>n</i> unitary matrices, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(S_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ) \)</span> the set of <i>n</i> × <i>n</i> real symmetric matrices, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(P_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ) \)</span> the set of <i>n</i> × <i>n</i> positive definite matrices and by <span class="mathjax-tex">\( O_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ) \)</span> the set of <i>n</i> × <i>n</i> orthogonal matrices. In the following we shall give several definitions.</p><p>A subset <i>K</i> of <span class="mathjax-tex">\(H_{n}\left ( \mathbb {C} \right ) \)</span> is called unitary invariant if for every <i>A</i> ∈ <i>K</i> we have <i>UAU</i>
<sup>∗</sup>∈ <i>K</i> for every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(U\in U_{n}\left ( \mathbb {C} \right ) .\)</span>
</p><p>A subset <i>K</i> of <span class="mathjax-tex">\(S_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ) \)</span> is called orthogonally invariant if for every <i>A</i> ∈ <i>K</i> we have <i>UAU</i>
<sup><i>T</i></sup> ∈ <i>K</i> for every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(U\in O_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ) .\)</span>
</p><p>Let <i>K</i> be a unitary invariant subset of <span class="mathjax-tex">\(H_{n}\left ( \mathbb {C} \right ) .\)</span> A function <span class="mathjax-tex">\(F:K\rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span> is called a unitary invariant if <span class="mathjax-tex">\(F\left ( UAU^{\ast }\right ) =F\left ( A\right ) \)</span> for every <i>A</i> ∈ <i>K</i> and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(U\in U_{n}\left ( \mathbb {C} \right ) .\)</span>
</p><p>Let <i>K</i> be an orthogonally invariant subset of <span class="mathjax-tex">\(S_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ) .\)</span> A function <span class="mathjax-tex">\(F:K\rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span> is called a unitary invariant if <span class="mathjax-tex">\(F\left ( UAU^{T}\right ) =F\left ( A\right ) \)</span> for every <i>A</i> ∈ <i>K</i> and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(U\in O_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ) .\)</span>
</p><p>Unitary invariant and orthogonally invariant functions are called also spectral functions because they are functions of eigenvalues. They depend only on the spectrum of the operator <i>A</i>. All the results we present in this paper have parallel versions both for unitarily invariant functions on the space of Hermitian matrices and for orthogonally invariant functions defined on the space of real symmetric matrices. The proofs are essentially identical.</p><p>A spectral function is symmetric since it is invariant to permutation matrices (which are a special case of unitary matrices). There are many papers studying spectral functions (see, for example, Borwein and Lewis [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 9" title="J.M. Borwein, A.S. Lewis, Convex Analysis and Nonlinear Optimization: Theory and Examples (Springer, Berlin, 2010)" href="#ref-CR9" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e85821">9</a>], Borwein and Vanderwerff [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 10" title="J.M. Borwein, J.D. Vanderwerff, Convex Functions: Constructions, Characterizations and Counterexamples (Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, 2010)" href="#ref-CR10" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e85824">10</a>], Lewis [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="A.S. Lewis, Convex analysis on the Hermitian matrices. SIAM J. Optim. 6, 164–177 (1996)" href="#ref-CR38" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e85827">38</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" title="A.S. Lewis, Derivatives of spectral functions. Math. Oper. Res. 6, 576–588 (1996)" href="#ref-CR39" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e85827_1">39</a>,<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 40" title="A.S. Lewis, The mathematics of eigenvalue optimization. Math. Programm. 97(1–2), 155–176 (2003)" href="#ref-CR40" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e85830">40</a>], Lewis and Overton[<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 41" title="A.S. Lewis, M.L. Overton, Eigenvalue optimization. Acta Numer. 5 , 149–190 (1996)" href="#ref-CR41" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e85833">41</a>], Lewis and Sendov [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 42" title="A.S. Lewis, H. Sendov, Twice differentiable spectral functions. SIAM J. Matrix Anal. Appl. 23(2), 368–386 (2001)" href="#ref-CR42" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e85837">42</a>], Meyer [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 51" title="C.D. Meyer, Matrix analysis and Applied Linear Algebra (SIAM, Philadelphia, 2000)" href="#ref-CR51" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e85840">51</a>], Niculescu and Persson [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 58" title="C.P. Niculescu, L.-E. Persson, Convex Functions and Their Applications: A Contemporary Approach (Springer, Berlin, 2018)" href="#ref-CR58" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e85843">58</a>], and Seeger [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 72" title="A. Seeger, Convex analysis of spectrally defined matrix functions. SIAM J. Optim. 7, 679–696 (1997)" href="#ref-CR72" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e85846">72</a>]).</p><p>Spectrally defined functions arise in various areas of applied mathematics: optimality criteria in experimental design theory (Pazman [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 59" title="A. Pazman, Foundations of optimum experimental design, in Mathematics and its Applications. East European Series (D. Reidel, Boston, 1986)" href="#ref-CR59" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e85853">59</a>], Hoang and Seeger [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 29" title="T. Hoang, A. Seeger, On conjugate functions, subgradients, and directional derivatives of a class of optimality criteria in experimental design. Statistics 22, 349–368 (1991)" href="#ref-CR29" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e85856">29</a>]), barrier functions in matrix optimization (Nesterov and Nemirovskii [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 57" title="Y.E. Nesterov, A.S. Nemirovskii, Optimization over positive semidefinite matrices: Mathematical Background and User’s Manual (USSR Academy Science Center Economics and Mathematical Institute, Moscow, 1990)" href="#ref-CR57" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e85859">57</a>], Lewis [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 38" title="A.S. Lewis, Convex analysis on the Hermitian matrices. SIAM J. Optim. 6, 164–177 (1996)" href="#ref-CR38" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e85862">38</a>]), matrix updates in quasi-Newton methods (Fletcher [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 22" title="R. Fletcher, A new variational result for quasi-Newton formulae. SIAM J. Optim. 1, 18–21 (1991)" href="#ref-CR22" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e85865">22</a>], Wolkowicz [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 83" title="H. Wolkowicz, Measures for symmetric rank-one updates. Math. Oper. Res. 19, 815–830 (1994)" href="#ref-CR83" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e85869">83</a>]), potential energy densities for isotropic elastic materials (Section 2.3 of Curnier et al. [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 13" title="A. Curnier, Q.C. He, P. Zysset, Conewise linear elastic materials. J. Elasticity 37, 1–38 (1995)" href="#ref-CR13" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e85872">13</a>]), etc.</p><p>Spectral functions may be encountered also in quantum mechanics (Kemble [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 33" title="E.C. Kemble, The Fundamental Principles of Quantum Mechanics (Dover, New York, 1958)" href="#ref-CR33" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e85878">33</a>] and Schiff [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 67" title="L.I. Schiff, Quantum Mechanics (McGraw-Hill, New York, 1955)" href="#ref-CR67" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e85881">67</a>]), nonlinear elasticity (Lehmich et al. [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 37" title="S. Lehmich, P. Neff, J. Lankeit, On the convexity of the function C f(det C) on positive-definite matrices. Math. Mech. Solids 19(4), 369–375 (2014)" href="#ref-CR37" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e85884">37</a>], Spector [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 75" title="S.J. Spector, A note on the convexity of C →h(C). J. Elast. 118(2), 251–256 (2015)" href="#ref-CR75" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e85887">75</a>], Šilhavý [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 73" title="M. Silhavy, The convexity of C →h(C). Tech. Mech. 35(1), 60–61 (2015)" href="#ref-CR73" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e85890">73</a>], and Gao et al. [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 23" title="D. Gao, P. Neff, I. Roventa, C. Thiel, On the convexity of nonlinear elastic energies in the right Cauchy-Green tensor. J. Elast. 127, 303–308 (2017)" href="#ref-CR23" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e85894">23</a>]). Nowadays they are an inseparable part of optimization (Lewis and Overton [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 41" title="A.S. Lewis, M.L. Overton, Eigenvalue optimization. Acta Numer. 5 , 149–190 (1996)" href="#ref-CR41" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e85897">41</a>]) and matrix analysis (Horn and Johnson [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 30" title="R.A. Horn, C.R. Johnson, Topics in Matrix Analysis (Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, 1994)" href="#ref-CR30" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e85900">30</a>]).</p><p>Spectral functions like log det(<i>A</i>), <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\lambda _{\max }\left ( A\right ) \ \)</span> the largest eigenvalue of the matrix argument <i>A</i>, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\lambda _{\min }\left ( A\right ) \ \)</span>the smallest eigenvalue of the matrix argument <i>A</i>, tr<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( A^{p}\right ) \)</span> the trace of power <i>p</i> of matrix <i>A</i> or the constraint that <i>A</i> must be positive definite, etc. appear in semidefinite programming (Vandenberghe et al. [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 82" title="L. Vandenberghe, S. Boyd, S.P. Wu, Determinant maximization with linear matrix inequality constraints. SIAM J. Matrix Anal. Appl. 19(2), 499–533 (1998)" href="#ref-CR82" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e86032">82</a>]),</p><p>Remarkably, many properties of the function <i>f</i> defined by <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f\left ( x\right ) =F\left ( \text{diag}\left ( x\right ) \right ) \)</span> are inherited by the spectral function <i>F</i>. For example, this holds for differentiability and convexity (Lewis [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 38" title="A.S. Lewis, Convex analysis on the Hermitian matrices. SIAM J. Optim. 6, 164–177 (1996)" href="#ref-CR38" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e86088">38</a>]), various types of generalized differentiability (Lewis [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 39" title="A.S. Lewis, Derivatives of spectral functions. Math. Oper. Res. 6, 576–588 (1996)" href="#ref-CR39" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e86091">39</a>]), analyticity (Tsing et al. [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 81" title="N.K. Tsing, M.K.H. Fan, E.I. Verriest, On analyticity of functions involving eigenvalues. Linear Algebra Appl. 207, 159–180 (1994)" href="#ref-CR81" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e86095">81</a>]), various second-order properties (Torki [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 80" title="M. Torki, First- and second-order epi-differentiability in eigenvalue optimization. J. Math. Anal. Appl. 234, 391–416 (1999)" href="#ref-CR80" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e86098">80</a>]), etc.</p><p>For every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(A\in H_{n}\left ( \mathbb {C} \right ) \)</span> let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\lambda _{1}\left ( A\right ) \geq \lambda _{2}\left ( A\right ) \geq \ldots \geq \lambda _{n}\left ( A\right ) \)</span> be the eigenvalues of <i>A</i> arranged in descending order. Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\varLambda :H_{n}\left ( \mathbb {C} \right ) \rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span> be the function defined by </p><div id="Equlu" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \varLambda \left( A\right) =\left( \lambda _{1}\left( A\right) ,\lambda _{2}\left( A\right) ,\ldots,\lambda _{n}\left( A\right) \right) \ \ \   A\in H_{n}\left( \mathbb{C} \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
<p>A subset <i>C</i> of <span class="mathjax-tex">\( \mathbb {R} ^{n}\)</span> is called permutation invariant if <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( x_{\sigma \left ( 1\right ) },x_{\sigma \left ( 2\right ) },\ldots ,x_{\sigma \left ( n\right ) }\right ) \in C\)</span> for every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in C\)</span> and for every permutation <i>σ</i> ∈ <i>S</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub>.A function <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f:C\rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span> is called symmetric if <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f\left ( x_{\sigma \left ( 1\right ) },x_{\sigma \left ( 2\right ) },\ldots ,x_{\sigma \left ( n\right ) }\right ) =f\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \)</span> for every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in C\)</span> and for every permutation <i>σ</i> ∈ <i>S</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub>.</p><p>Let <i>K</i> be a unitary invariant subset of <span class="mathjax-tex">\(H_{n}\left ( \mathbb {C} \right ) \)</span> and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(F:K\rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span> be a spectral function. Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(C=\left \{ x\in \mathbb {R} ^{n}:\text{diag}\left ( x\right ) \in K\right \} .\)</span> Then <i>C</i> is a permutation invariant subset of <span class="mathjax-tex">\( \mathbb {R} ^{n}\)</span> and the function <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f\left ( x\right ) =F\left ( \text{diag}\left ( x\right ) \right ) \)</span> <i>x</i> ∈ <i>C</i> is permutation invariant. Spectral decomposition theorem shows that </p><div id="Equlv" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} F\left( A\right) =f\left( \varLambda \left( A\right) \right) =f\left( \lambda _{1}\left( A\right) ,\lambda _{2}\left( A\right) ,\ldots,\lambda _{n}\left( A\right) \right) ,\ \ \ \ \  A\in K \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Here we denoted by diag(<i>x</i>) the diagonal matrix having the entries of vector <i>x</i> on the diagonal.</p><p>In the paper of Davis [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 15" title="C. Davis, All convex invariant functions of Hermitian matrices. Arch. Math. 8(4), 276–278 (1957)" href="#ref-CR15" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e87290">15</a>] was proved the following result:</p>
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar63">Theorem 6.1</h3>
              <p><i>Let K be a unitary invariant subset of</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\( H_{n}\left ( \mathbb {C} \right ) ,\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(F:K\rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span> <i>be a spectral function,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(C=\left \{ x\in \mathbb {R} ^{n}:\mathit{\text{diag}}\left ( x\right ) \in K\right \} \)</span> <i>and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f\left ( x\right ) =F\left ( \mathit{\text{diag}}\left ( x\right ) \right ) \)</span> <i>x </i>∈<i> C</i>.</p>
              <p>
                <i>Then F is convex if and only if its associated symmetric function f is convex.</i>
              </p>
            <p>In the monograph of Niculescu and Persson [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 58" title="C.P. Niculescu, L.-E. Persson, Convex Functions and Their Applications: A Contemporary Approach (Springer, Berlin, 2018)" href="#ref-CR58" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e87493">58</a>] the following remark was made: The Davis’s result remains valid in the framework of log-convex functions, log-concave functions, concave functions, quasi-convex functions, quasi-concave functions. The authors provide a proof of Davis theorem that is for the case the function implied is convex. Unfortunately the case when the function is quasi-convex is not studied.</p><p>In the following we shall state and prove a generalization of Davis theorem. We shall recall some definitions from Hardy–Littlewood–Polya theory of majorization. If <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {x}=\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in \mathbb {R} ^{n}\)</span>, we shall denote by <i>x</i>
<sub>[1]</sub> ≥ <i>x</i>
<sub>[2]</sub> ≥… ≥ <i>x</i>
<sub>[<i>n</i>]</sub> the components of <i>x</i> in descending order. Thus <i>x</i>
<sub>[<i>k</i>]</sub> is the <i>k</i> −th largest component of <i>x</i>. If <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {x,y}\in \mathbb {R} ^{n}\)</span> we say that <b>x</b> is <i>majorized</i> by <b>y</b> if </p><div id="Equlw" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \overset{k}{\underset{i=1}{\sum }}x_{[i]}\leq \overset{k}{\underset{i=1}{ \sum }}y_{[i]}\ \ \  \text{for }k=1,2,\ldots,n \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> and </p><div id="Equlx" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \overset{n}{\underset{i=1}{\sum }}x_{[i]}=\overset{n}{\underset{i=1}{\sum } }y_{[i]}\ \   \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> If <b>x</b> is majorized by <b>y</b> we shall write <b>x</b> ≺<sub><i>HLP</i></sub><b>y</b>. The basic result relating majorization and convexity is the Hardy–Littlewood–Polya inequality of majorization:</p>
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar64">Theorem 6.2</h3>
              <p><i>If</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {x,y}\in \mathbb {R} ^{n}\)</span> <i>and</i> <b>x</b> ≺<sub><i>HLP</i></sub><b>y</b> <i>then</i> </p><div id="Equly" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \overset{n}{\underset{i=1}{\sum }}f\left( x_{i}\right) \leq \overset{n}{ \underset{i=1}{\sum }}f\left( y_{i}\right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p><i>for every continuous convex function whose domain includes the components of</i> <b>x</b> <i>and</i> <b>y</b><i>. Conversely if the above inequality holds for every continuous convex function whose domain includes the components of x and y then</i> <b>x</b> ≺<sub><i>HLP</i></sub><b>y</b>.</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar65">Theorem 6.3</h3>
              <p>
                <i>Let</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x,y\in \mathbb {R} ^{n}.\)</span>
                <i>Then the following assertions are equivalent:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">(a)</span>
                    
                      <p><b>x</b> ≺<sub><i>HLP</i></sub><b>y</b>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">(b)</span>
                    
                      <p><b>x</b> = <b>Py</b> <i>for a suitable doubly stochastic matrix</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\( \mathbf {P}\in M_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ) .\)</span>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            <p>For a proof of the following theorem, see the monograph of Niculescu and Persson [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 58" title="C.P. Niculescu, L.-E. Persson, Convex Functions and Their Applications: A Contemporary Approach (Springer, Berlin, 2018)" href="#ref-CR58" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e88335">58</a>].</p>
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar66">Theorem 6.4</h3>
              <p><i>Let C be a permutation invariant subset of</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\( \mathbb {R} ^{n}\)</span> <i>and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f:C\rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span> <i>be a symmetric quasi-convex function. Then f is Schur convex, that is x</i>, <i>y </i>∈<i> C and x </i>≺<sub><i>HLP</i></sub><i>y implies</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f\left ( x\right ) \leq f\left ( y\right ) .\)</span></p>
            <p>Schur [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 71" title="I. Schur, Uber eine Klasse von Mittelbildungen mit Anwendungdie Determinanten. Theorie Sitzungsber. Berlin. Math. Gesellschaft 22, 9–29 (1923)" href="#ref-CR71" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e88461">71</a>] has proved that the diagonal elements <i>a</i>
<sub>11</sub>, <i>a</i>
<sub>22</sub>, …, <i>a</i>
<sub><i>nn</i></sub> of a Hermitian <i>n</i> × <i>n</i> matrix <span class="mathjax-tex">\(A=\left ( a_{ij}\right ) \)</span> are majorized by the eigenvalues <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\lambda _{1}\left ( A\right ) ,\lambda _{2}\left ( A\right ) ,\ldots ,\lambda _{n}\left ( A\right ) \)</span> that is </p><div id="Equlz" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left( a_{11},a_{22},\ldots,a_{nn}\right) \prec _{HLP}\left( \lambda _{1}\left( A\right) ,\lambda _{2}\left( A\right) ,\ldots,\lambda _{n}\left( A\right) \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
<p>The following theorem is a slightly modified version of Schur’s theorem that will be used for the proof of a generalization of Davis theorem.</p>
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar67">Theorem 6.5</h3>
              <p><i>Let</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(u_{j}\in \mathbb {C} ^{n},\)</span> <i>j </i>= 1, 2, …, <i>n be an orthonormal basis in</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\( \mathbb {C} ^{n},\)</span> <i>that is</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(u_{j}^{T}\bar {u}_{k}=\delta _{jk}\)</span> <i>for</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(j,k\in \in \left \{ 1,2,\ldots ,n\right \} .\)</span> <i>If</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(A\in H_{n}\left ( \mathbb {C} \right ) \)</span> <i>then</i> </p><div id="Equma" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left( u_{1}^{T}A\bar{u}_{1},u_{2}^{T}A\bar{u}_{2},\ldots,u_{n}^{T}A\bar{u} _{n}\right) \prec _{HLP}\left( \lambda _{1}\left( A\right) ,\lambda _{2}\left( A\right) ,\ldots,\lambda _{n}\left( A\right) \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>Let E be a linear space over</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\( \mathbb {R} \)</span>
                <i>and C be a convex subset of E. For every</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( p,q\right ) \in \left [ 0,1\right ] ^{2}\)</span>
                <i>consider the set</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathscr {A}\left ( p,q,C\right ) \)</span>
                <i>of quasi-convex functions</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f:C\rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span>
                <i>with the following property</i>
                </p><div id="Equmb" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} &amp;f\left( \left( 1-p\right) x+py\right) \leq \left( 1-q\right) \left[ \left( 1-p\right) f\left( x\right) +pf\left( y\right) \right] +q\max \left( f\left( x\right) ,f\left( y\right) \right) \\ &amp;\mathit{\text{for every }}x,y\in C \end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p><i>If M is a subset of</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left [ 0,1\right ] ^{2}\)</span> <i>denote by</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathscr {B}\left ( M,C\right ) \)</span> <i>the set of quasi-convex functions</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f:C\rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span> <i>with the property that f </i>∈ <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathscr {A}\left ( p,q,C\right ) \)</span> <i>for every</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( p,q\right ) \in M.\)</span>
</p>
              <p>
                <i>Note that</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">10.</span>
                    
                      <p>
                        <i>If</i>
                        <span class="mathjax-tex">\(M=\left [ 0,1\right ] \times \left \{ 0\right \} \)</span>
                        <i>, then</i>
                        <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathscr {B} \left ( M,C\right ) \)</span>
                        <i>is the set of convex functions on C.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">20.</span>
                    
                      <p>
                        <i>If</i>
                        <span class="mathjax-tex">\(M=\left [ 0,1\right ] \times \left \{ 1\right \} \)</span>
                        <i>, then</i>
                        <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathscr {B} \left ( M,C\right ) \)</span>
                        <i>is the set of quasi-convex functions on C.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">30.</span>
                    
                      <p>
                        <i>If</i>
                        <span class="mathjax-tex">\(M=\left \{ \left ( \frac {1}{2},0\right ) \right \} \)</span>
                        <i>, then</i>
                        <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathscr { B}\left ( M,C\right ) \)</span>
                        <i>is the set of mid-convex functions on C</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
              <p>
                <i>The following theorem is a generalization of Davis theorem. It includes only the nontrivial implication from the original theorem.</i>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar68">Theorem 6.6</h3>
              <p>
                <i>Let C be a permutation invariant convex subset of</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\( \mathbb {R} ^{n}\)</span>
                <i>,</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f:C\rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span>
                <i>be a symmetric quasi-convex function and M be a subset of</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left [ 0,1 \right ] ^{2}.\)</span>
                <i>Consider the set</i>
              </p>
              <p>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(K=\left \{ A\in H_{n}\left (\mathbb {C}\right ):\Lambda \left ( A\right ) \in C\right \},\)</span>
                <i>and the function</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\( \widetilde {f}:K\rightarrow \mathbb {R}\)</span>
                <i>,</i>
                </p><div id="Equmc" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \widetilde{f}\left( A\right) =f\left( \lambda _{1}\left( A\right) ,\lambda _{2}\left( A\right) ,\ldots,\lambda _{n}\left( A\right) \right) \ \ \ \ \ \ A\in K \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>If</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f\in \mathscr {B}\left ( M,C\right ) \)</span>
                <i>, then</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\widetilde {f}\in \mathscr {B} \left ( M,K\right ) .\)</span>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( p,q\right ) \in M\)</span>, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f\in \mathscr {A}\left ( p,q,C\right )\)</span>, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(A,B\in K,D=\left ( 1-p\right ) A+pB\)</span>. Denote by <span class="mathjax-tex">\(u_{j}\in \mathbb {C}^{n},\)</span> <i>j</i> = 1, 2, …, <i>n</i> the eigenvectors of the eigenvalues of the matrix <i>D</i>, that is <i>Du</i>
<sub><i>j</i></sub> = . <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\lambda _{j}\left ( D\right ) u_{j},j=1,2,\ldots ,n.\)</span> By applying Theorems <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar66">6.4</a>. and <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar67">6.5</a> we obtain: </p><div id="Equmd" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \widetilde{f}\left( D\right) =f\left( \lambda _{1}\left( D\right) ,\lambda _{2}\left( D\right) ,\ldots,\lambda _{n}\left( D\right) \right) =f\left( u_{1}^{T}D\bar{u}_{1},u_{2}^{T}D\bar{u}_{2},\ldots,u_{n}^{T}D\bar{u}_{n}\right) =\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equme" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =&amp;f\left( \left( 1-p\right) u_{1}^{T}A\bar{u}_{1}+pu_{1}^{T}B\bar{u} _{1},\left( 1-p\right) u_{2}^{T}A\bar{u}_{2}+pu_{2}^{T}B\bar{u} _{2},\ldots,\left( 1-p\right) u_{n}^{T}A\bar{u}_{n}\right. \\ &amp; \left.+pu_{n}^{T}B\bar{u} _{n}\right) \leq\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equmf" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \leq \left( 1-q\right) &amp;\left[ \left( 1-p\right) f\left( u_{1}^{T}A\bar{u} _{1},u_{2}^{T}A\bar{u}_{2},\ldots,u_{n}^{T}A\bar{u}_{n}\right)\right. \\ &amp; \left.+pf\left( u_{1}^{T}B\bar{u}_{1},u_{2}^{T}B\bar{u}_{2},\ldots,u_{n}^{T}B\bar{u}_{n}\right) \right] + \end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equmg" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} {+}q\max \left( f\left( u_{1}^{T}A\bar{u}_{1},u_{2}^{T}A\bar{u} _{2},\ldots,u_{n}^{T}A\bar{u}_{n}\right) ,\ f\left( u_{1}^{T}B\bar{u} _{1},u_{2}^{T}B\bar{u}_{2},\ldots,u_{n}^{T}B\bar{u}_{n}\right) \right) \leq\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equmh" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \leq &amp;\left( 1-q\right) \left[ \left( 1-p\right) f\left( \lambda _{1}\left( A\right) ,\lambda _{2}\left( A\right) ,\ldots,\lambda _{n}\left( A\right) \right) \right.\\ &amp; \left. +pf\left( \lambda _{1}\left( B\right) ,\lambda _{2}\left( B\right) ,\ldots,\lambda _{n}\left( B\right) \right) \right] + \end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equmi" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} +q\max \left( \left( f\left( \lambda _{1}\left( A\right) ,\lambda _{2}\left( A\right) ,\ldots,\lambda _{n}\left( A\right) \right) ,\ f\left( \lambda _{1}\left( B\right) ,\lambda _{2}\left( B\right) ,\ldots,\lambda _{n}\left( B\right) \right) \right) \right) = \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equmj" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =\left( 1-q\right) \left[ \left( 1-p\right) \widetilde{f}\left( A\right) +p \widetilde{f}\left( B\right) \right] +q\max \left( \widetilde{f}\left( A\right) ,\ \widetilde{f}\left( B\right) \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>Thus <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\widetilde {f}\in \mathscr {A}\left ( p,q,C\right )\)</span>. Consequently the conclusion of the theorem follows. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Examples</h3>
              
                <ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">1.</span>
                    
                      <p>If <span class="mathjax-tex">\(C= \mathbb {R} ^{n}\)</span> and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f_{1}\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) =x_{1}+x_{2}+\ldots +x_{n},\ \left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in C\)</span> then <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\widetilde {f}_{1}\left ( A\right ) =\lambda _{1}\left ( A\right ) +\lambda _{2}\left ( A\right ) +\ldots +\lambda _{n}\left ( A\right ) \)</span>=Tr<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( A\right ) ,\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(A\in S_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ) \)</span>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">2.</span>
                    
                      <p>If <span class="mathjax-tex">\(C= \mathbb {R} ^{n}\)</span> and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f_{2}\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) =x_{1}x_{2}\ldots x_{n},\ \left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in C\)</span> then <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\widetilde {f}_{2}\left ( A\right ) =\lambda _{1}\left ( A\right ) \lambda _{2}\left ( A\right ) \ldots \lambda _{n}\left ( A\right ) \)</span>=det<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( A\right ) ,\ A\in S_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ) \)</span>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">3.</span>
                    
                      <p>If <span class="mathjax-tex">\(C=\left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}\)</span> and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f_{3}\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) =\frac {1}{x_{1}}+\frac {1}{x_{2}}+\ldots .+\frac {1}{ x_{n}},\ \ \left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in C\ \)</span>then <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\widetilde {f} _{3}\left ( A\right ) =\frac {1}{\lambda _{1}\left ( A\right ) }+\frac {1}{\lambda _{2}\left ( A\right ) }+\ldots +\frac {1}{\lambda _{n}\left ( A\right ) }=\lambda _{1}\left ( A^{-1}\right ) +\lambda _{2}\left ( A^{-1}\right ) +\ldots +\lambda _{n}\left ( A^{-1}\right ) \)</span>=Tr<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( A^{-1}\right ) ,\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(A\in S_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ) \)</span>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">4.</span>
                    
                      <p>If 1 ≤ <i>k</i> ≤ <i>n</i> and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(C= \mathbb {R} ^{n}\)</span> and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f_{4}\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) =\sigma _{k}\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) =\)</span> the <i>k</i> −th elementary symmetric polynomial then <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\widetilde {f}_{4}\left ( A\right ) =\widetilde {\sigma }_{k}\left ( A\right ) =\)</span>the sum of principal minors of <i>A</i> of order <i>k</i>.</p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
              <p>If <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x=\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \)</span> and <span class="mathjax-tex">\( y=\left ( y_{1},y_{2},\ldots ,y_{n}\right ) \)</span> are two vectors from <span class="mathjax-tex">\( \mathbb {R} ^{n}\)</span> we shall write <i>x</i> ≤ <i>y</i> if <i>x</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> ≤ <i>y</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> for every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(i\in \left \{ 1,2,\ldots ,n\right \} .\)</span> Let <i>C</i> be a subset of <span class="mathjax-tex">\( \mathbb {R} ^{n}\)</span> A function <span class="mathjax-tex">\(h:C\rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span> is called <i>monotonic increasing</i> if <i>x</i>, <i>y</i> ∈ <i>C</i> and <i>x</i> ≤ <i>y</i> implies <span class="mathjax-tex">\(h\left ( x\right ) \leq h\left ( y\right ) .\)</span> Note that <i>h</i> is monotone increasing if and only if it is separately monotone increasing.</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar69">Theorem 6.7</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2<i>,</i> 1 ≤<i> k </i>≤<i> n, C be a convex permutation invariant subset of</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\( \mathbb {R} ^{n}\)</span><i>,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(P_{k}: \mathbb {R} ^{n}\rightarrow \mathbb {R} ^{k}\)</span><i>,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(P_{k}\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) =\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{k}\right ) ,\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in \mathbb {R} ^{n},\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(C_{k}=P_{k}\left ( \mathbb {R} ^{n}\right ) .\)</span>
</p>
              <p><i>If</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x=\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in \mathbb {R} ^{n}\)</span><i>, we denote by x</i>
<sub>[1]</sub> ≥<i> x</i>
<sub>[2]</sub> ≥… ≥<i> x</i>
<sub>[<i>n</i>]</sub> <i>the components of x in descending order. Consider the functions</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(h_{i}:C_{k}\rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span><i>, i </i>= 1, 2 <i>and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f,g:C\rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span> <i>defined as follows:</i> </p><div id="Equ17" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) =h_{1}\left( x_{[1]},x_{[2]},\ldots,x_{[k]}\right) ,\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) \in C \end{aligned} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (6.17)
                </div></div>

              
                <div id="Equ18" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) =h_{2}\left( x_{[n]},x_{[n-1]},\ldots,x_{[n-k+1]}\right) ,\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \ \left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) \in C \end{aligned} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (6.18)
                </div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Suppose that the following conditions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">10.</span>
                    
                      <p>
                        <i>h</i>
                        <sub>1</sub>
                        <i>and h</i>
                        <sub>2</sub>
                        <i>are symmetric and monotone increasing.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">20.</span>
                    
                      <p>
                        <i>h</i>
                        <sub>1</sub>
                        <i>is convex and h</i>
                        <sub>2</sub>
                        <i>is concave.</i>
                      </p>
                      <p>
                        <i>Then f is convex and g is concave.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>For every permutation <i>σ</i> ∈ <i>S</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> and <i>i</i> = 1, 2 let </p><div id="Equmk" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u_{i,\sigma }\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) =h_{i}\left( x_{\sigma \left( 1\right) },x_{\sigma \left( 2\right) },\ldots,x_{\sigma \left( k\right) }\right) ,\ \ \ \ \left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) \in C. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>Note that for every permutation <i>σ</i> ∈ <i>S</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> , <i>u</i>
<sub>1,<i>σ</i></sub> is convex and <i>u</i>
<sub>2,<i>σ</i></sub> is concave. One can easily see that </p><div id="Equml" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f\left( x\right) =\underset{\sigma \in S_{n}}{\sup }\left[ u_{1,\sigma }\left( x\right) \right] ,\ \ \ \ \ \ x\in C, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equmm" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g\left( x\right) =\underset{\sigma \in S_{n}}{\inf }\left[ u_{2,\sigma }\left( x\right) \right] ,\ \ \ \ \ \ x\in C, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> and <i>f</i> and <i>g</i> are symmetric. Since the maximum of convex functions is a convex function it follows that <i>f</i> is convex. Since the minimum of concave functions is a concave function it follows that <i>g</i> is concave. Note that (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ17">6.17</a>) and (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ18">6.18</a>) hold. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar70">Corollary 6.8</h3>
              <p><i>For every matrix A let</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\lambda _{1}\left ( A\right ) \geq \lambda _{2}\left ( A\right ) \geq \ldots \geq \lambda _{n}\left ( A\right ) \)</span> <i>be the eigenvalues of A arranged in descending order. For every</i> 1 ≤<i> k </i>≤<i> n consider the functions:</i> </p><div id="Equmn" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{1,k}\left( A\right) =\underset{r=1}{\overset{k}{\varSigma }}\lambda _{r}\left( A\right) ,\ \ \ \ \ A\in H_{n}\left( \mathbb{C} \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              
                <div id="Equmo" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{2,k}\left( A\right) =\underset{r=0}{\overset{k-1}{\varSigma }}\lambda _{n-r}\left( A\right) ,\ \ \ \ \ A\in H_{n}\left( \mathbb{C} \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Eqump" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{3}\left( A\right) =\lambda _{1}\left( A\right) ,\ \ \ \ \ A\in H_{n}\left( \mathbb{C} \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equmq" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{4}\left( A\right) =\lambda _{n}\left( A\right) ,\ \ \ \ \ A\in H_{n}\left( \mathbb{C} \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equmr" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{5,k}\left( A\right) =\sqrt[k]{\lambda _{n}\left( A\right) \lambda _{n-1}\left( A\right) \ldots\lambda _{n-k+1}\left( A\right) },\ \ \ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left( \mathbb{C} \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equms" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{6}\left( A\right) =\sqrt[n]{\det \left( A\right) },\ \ \ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left( \mathbb{C} \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p><i>Then f</i>
<sub>1,<i>k</i></sub><i>, f</i>
<sub>3</sub> <i>are convex and f</i>
<sub>2,<i>k</i></sub><i>, f</i>
<sub>4</sub>, <i>f</i>
<sub>5,<i>k</i></sub> <i>and f</i>
<sub>6</sub> <i>are concave.</i>
</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(h_{k}\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{k}\right ) =x_{1}+x_{2}+\ldots +x_{k},\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in \mathbb {R} ^{k}.\)</span> Note that <i>h</i>
<sub><i>k</i></sub> is convex and concave. By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar69">6.7</a>. it follows that </p><div id="Equmt" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{1,k}\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) =h_{k}\left( x_{[1]},x_{[2]},\ldots,x_{[k]}\right) ,\ \ \ \ \ \left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) \in \mathbb{R} ^{n} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> is convex and </p><div id="Equmu" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{2,k}\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) =h_{k}\left( x_{[n]},x_{[n-1]},\ldots,x_{[n-k+1]}\right) ,\ \ \ \ \ \left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) \in \mathbb{R} ^{n} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> is concave. Note that </p><div id="Equmv" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{1,k}\left( A\right) =\widetilde{g}_{1,k}\left( A\right) ,\ \ \ \ \ A\in H_{n}\left( \mathbb{C} \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equmw" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{2,k}\left( A\right) =\widetilde{g}_{2,k}\left( A\right) ,\ \ \ \ \ A\in H_{n}\left( \mathbb{C} \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By the Davis theorem it follows that <i>f</i>
<sub>1,<i>k</i></sub> is convex and <i>f</i>
<sub>2,<i>k</i></sub> is concave. Since <i>f</i>
<sub>3</sub> = <i>f</i>
<sub>1,1</sub> and <i>f</i>
<sub>4</sub> = <i>f</i>
<sub>2,1</sub> it follows that <i>f</i>
<sub>3</sub> is convex and <i>f</i>
<sub>4</sub> is concave. Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(u_{k}\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) =\sqrt [ k]{x_{1}x_{2}\ldots x_{k}},\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}.\)</span> By applying the preceding theorem and the Davis theorem we obtain that <i>f</i>
<sub>5</sub> is concave. Note that <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f_{6}=\widetilde {u} _{n}.\)</span> By Davis theorem it follows that <i>f</i>
<sub>6</sub> is concave. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar71">Lemma 6.9</h3>
              <p><i>Let E be a linear normed space, D be a convex open subset of E, and f be a two times differentiable function. For every a </i>∈<i> D and h </i>∈<i> E let</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\varepsilon \left ( a,h\right ) &gt;0,\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(J\left ( a,h\right ) =\left \{ t\geq 0:a+th\in D\right \} =[0,\varepsilon \left ( a,h\right ) ),\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(u_{a,h}\left ( t\right ) =f\left ( a+th\right ) \)</span><i>,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(t\in J\left ( a,h\right ) .\)</span> <i>Then f is convex if and only if</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(u_{a,h}^{\prime \prime }\left ( 0\right ) \geq 0\)</span> <i>for every a </i>∈<i> D and h </i>∈<i> E</i>.</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>The conclusion of the lemma follows at once from the equality <span class="mathjax-tex">\( u_{a,h}^{\prime \prime }\left ( 0\right ) =f\ ^{\prime \prime }\left ( a\right ) \left ( h,h\right ) ,\)</span> <i>a</i> ∈ <i>D</i> and <i>h</i> ∈ <i>E</i>. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar72">Lemma 6.10</h3>
              <p><i>Let B, C be two n </i>×<i> n positive semidefinite matrices,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(A\in P_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ) \)</span> <i>and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(w\left ( t\right ) =\)</span><i>Tr</i>
<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( C\left ( A^{-1}B+tI_{n}\right ) ^{2}A^{-1}\right ) ,\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(t\in \mathbb {R} .\)</span> <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i> </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">10.</span>

<p>
<span class="mathjax-tex">\(w\left ( t\right ) \geq 0\)</span> <i>for every</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(t\in \mathbb {R} .\)</span>
</p>

</li>
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">20.</span>

<p>
<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left [ \mathit{\text{Tr}}\left ( CA^{-1}BA^{-1}\right ) \right ] ^{2}\leq \)</span><i>Tr</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( CA^{-1}\right ) \)</span><i>Tr</i>
<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( C\left ( A^{-1}B\right ) ^{2}A^{-1}\right ) \)</span>
</p>

</li>
</ol>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(Q\left ( t\right ) =B+tA\)</span>, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(t\in \mathbb {R} .\)</span> There exists a positive semidefinite matrix <i>E</i> such that <i>C</i> = <i>EE</i> and exists a <span class="mathjax-tex">\(D\in P_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ) \)</span> such that <i>A</i>
<sup>−1</sup> = <i>DD</i>. Denote <span class="mathjax-tex">\(S\left ( t\right ) =EDDQ\left ( t\right ) D,\ \ t\in \mathbb {R} .\)</span> Then </p><div id="Equmx" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} w\left( t\right) =\text{Tr}\left( C\left[ A^{-1}\left( B+tA\right) \right] ^{2}A^{-1}\right) =\text{Tr}\left( CA^{-1}Q\left( t\right) A^{-1}Q\left( t\right) A^{-1}\right) = \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equmy" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =\text{Tr}\left( EEDDQ\left( t\right) DDQ\left( t\right) DD\right) =\text{Tr} \left( ES\left( t\right) DQ\left( t\right) DD\right) = \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equmz" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =\text{Tr}\left( S\left( t\right) DQ\left( t\right) DDE\right) =\text{Tr} \left( S\left( t\right) S\left( t\right) ^{T}\right) \geq 0 \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Thus assertion 1<sup>0</sup> is proved. Note that </p><div id="Equna" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} w\left( t\right) =t^{2}\text{Tr}\left( CA^{-1}\right) +2t\cdot \text{Tr} \left( CA^{-1}BA^{-1}\right) +\text{Tr}\left( C\left( A^{-1}B\right) ^{2}A^{-1}\right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Since the quadratic polynomial in <i>t</i> has constant sign it follows that its discriminant is negative. Hence the inequality from assertion 2<sup>0</sup> holds. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar73">Theorem 6.11</h3>
              <p>
                <i>Let</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(C\in P_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ) .\)</span>
                <i>Then the function</i>
                </p><div id="Equnb" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f\left( A\right) =\frac{1}{\mathit{\text{Tr}}\left( CA^{-1}\right) },\ \ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left( \mathbb{R} \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>is concave.</i>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(B\in H_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ) ,\ A\in \)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(P_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ) .\)</span> Then there exists <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\varepsilon =\varepsilon \left ( A,B\right ) &gt;0\)</span> such that <i>A</i> + <i>tB</i> is invertible for every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(t\in \left ( -\epsilon ,\epsilon \right ) \)</span>. Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(v\left ( t\right ) =\left ( A+tB\right ) ^{-1}\ \)</span>and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(u\left ( t\right ) =\)</span>Tr<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( Cv\left ( t\right ) \right ) ,\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(t\in \left ( -\epsilon ,\epsilon \right ) .\)</span>Note that <span class="mathjax-tex">\(u\left ( t\right ) &gt;0\ \)</span>for every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(t\in \left ( -\epsilon ,\epsilon \right ) \)</span> and </p><div id="Equnc" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left( \frac{1}{u}\right) ^{\prime \prime }=\frac{2u\ u^{\prime }-u\ u^{\prime \prime }}{u^{3}}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Hence the function <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\frac {1}{u}\)</span> is concave if and only if <i>u</i> <i>u</i>
<sup><i>′′</i></sup>− 2<i>u</i> <i>u</i>
<sup><i>′</i></sup>≥ 0</p>
              <p>Note that <span class="mathjax-tex">\(v\left ( 0\right ) =A^{-1}.\)</span> If we differentiate with respect to <i>t</i> the identity <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( A+tB\right ) v\left ( t\right ) =I_{n}\)</span> we obtain <span class="mathjax-tex">\( Bv\left ( t\right ) +\left ( A+tB\right ) v^{\prime }\left ( t\right ) =0,\)</span> hence <span class="mathjax-tex">\( v^{\prime }\left ( t\right ) =-v\left ( t\right ) Bv\left ( t\right ) .\)</span>If we differentiate again the last identity we obtain </p><div id="Eqund" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v^{\prime \prime }\left( t\right) =-v^{\prime }\left( t\right) Bv\left( t\right) -v\left( t\right) Bv^{\prime }\left( t\right) =2v\left( t\right) Bv\left( t\right) Bv\left( t\right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that </p><div id="Equne" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u^{\prime \prime }\left( t\right) =\text{Tr}\left( Cv^{\prime \prime }\left( t\right) \right) =2\text{Tr}\left( Cv\left( t\right) Bv\left( t\right) Bv\left( t\right) \right)\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div><p> By the preceding lemma we obtain </p><div id="Equnf" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u\left( t\right) \ u^{\prime \prime }\left( t\right) -2u\left( t\right) \ u^{\prime }\left( t\right) =&amp;2\text{Tr}\left( Cv\left( t\right) \right) \text{ Tr}\left( Cv\left( t\right) Bv\left( t\right) Bv\left( t\right) \right) \\ &amp;-2 \left[ \text{Tr}\left( Cv\left( t\right) Bv\left( t\right) \right) \right] ^{2}\geq 0\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div><p> By Lemma <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar71">6.9</a> it follows that <i>f</i> is concave. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">The Bergstrom Inequality [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 6" title="H. Bergstrom, A triangle inequality for matrices, in Den Ilte Skandinauiske Matematikerkongress (1949), pp. 264–267" href="#ref-CR6" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e102043">6</a>]</h3>
              <p>For every <i>n</i> × <i>n</i> matrix <i>A</i> and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(k\in \left \{ 1,2,\ldots ,n\right \} \)</span> denote by <i>A</i>
<sub><i>k</i></sub> the matrix obtained from <i>A</i> by deleting the <i>k</i>-th row and the <i>k</i>-th column of <i>A</i>. In the paper of Bergstrom [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 6" title="H. Bergstrom, A triangle inequality for matrices, in Den Ilte Skandinauiske Matematikerkongress (1949), pp. 264–267" href="#ref-CR6" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e102120">6</a>] the following inequality was proved: </p><div id="Equng" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \frac{\det \left( A+B\right) }{\det \left( A_{k}+B_{k}\right) }\geq \frac{ \det \left( A\right) }{\det \left( A_{k}\right) }+\frac{\det \left( B\right) }{\det \left( B_{k}\right) },\ \ \ \ A,B\in P_{n}\left( \mathbb{R} \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> One can easily see that Bergstrom inequality is equivalent with the concavity of the function </p><div id="Equnh" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f\left( A\right) =\frac{\det \left( A\right) }{\det \left( A_{k}\right) },\ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left( \mathbb{R} \right) \ \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>A generalization of the Bergstrom inequality can be obtained from Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar73">6.11</a>.</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar74">Theorem 6.12</h3>
              <p><i>Let c</i>
<sub>1</sub>, <i>c</i>
<sub>2</sub>, …, <i>c</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> <i>be nonnegative numbers such that</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\overset {n}{\underset {i=1}{\sum }}c_{i}&gt;0.\)</span> <i>Then the function</i> </p><div id="Equni" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f\left( A\right) =\frac{\det \left( A\right) }{\overset{n}{\underset{i=1}{ \sum }}c_{i}\det \left( A_{i}\right) },\ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left( \mathbb{R} \right) \ \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>is concave.</i>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>It suffices to prove the concavity of <i>f</i> in the case all <i>c</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> &gt; 0. Note that if <i>C</i> = diag<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( c_{1},c_{2},\ldots ,c_{n}\right ) \)</span> then </p><div id="Equnj" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \text{Tr}\left( CA^{-1}\right) =\overset{n}{\underset{i=1}{\sum }}c_{i} \frac{\det \left( A_{i}\right) }{\det \left( A\right) } \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> hence by Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar73">6.11</a>. </p><div id="Equnk" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f\left( A\right) =\frac{1}{\text{Tr}\left( CA^{-1}\right) }=\frac{\det \left( A\right) }{\overset{n}{\underset{i=1}{\sum }}c_{i}\det \left( A_{i}\right) },\ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left( \mathbb{R} \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> is concave. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar75">Theorem 6.13</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2<i>,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(1\leq k\leq \tilde {n},\ a\in \mathbb {R} \)</span><i>. Then the function</i> </p><div id="Equnl" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f\left( A\right) =\left[ \widetilde{\sigma }_{k}\left( A\right) \right] ^{a},\ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left( \mathbb{R} \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p><i>is concave if and only if</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(ap\in \left [ 0,\frac {1}{k}\right ] \)</span> <i>and p </i>∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 1].</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(g\left ( x\right ) =\left [ \sigma _{k}\left ( x^{p}\right ) \right ] ^{a},\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}.\)</span> Note that by Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar62">5.27</a>. <i>g</i> is concave if and only if <span class="mathjax-tex">\(ap\in \left [ 0,\frac {1}{k}\right ] \)</span> and <i>p</i> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 1]. By the Davis theorem <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f=\widetilde {g}\)</span> is concave if and only if <span class="mathjax-tex">\(ap\in \left [ 0,\frac {1}{k}\right ] \)</span> and <i>p</i> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 1]. □</p>
            <p>In the paper of Sra [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 76" title="S. Sra, New concavity and convexity results for symmetric polynomials and their ratios. Linear Multilinear Algebra 1–9 (2018). &#xA;http://dx.doi.org/10.1080/03081087.2018.1527891&#xA;&#xA;&#xA;" href="#ref-CR76" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e103585">76</a>], Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar9">3.4</a>., the following result is stated:</p>
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar76">Theorem 6.14</h3>
              <p><i>If</i> 1 ≤<i> k </i>≤<i> n and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \left ( -1,0\right ) \)</span> <i>then the function</i> </p><div id="Equnm" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{k,p}\left( A\right) =\frac{1}{\widetilde{\sigma }_{k}\left( A\right) },\ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left( \mathbb{R} \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>is concave.</i>
              </p>
            <p>Taking into account Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar75">6.13</a>. one can easily see that <i>f</i>
<sub><i>k</i>,<i>p</i></sub> is concave if and only if <i>k</i> = 1. Consequently Sra’s result holds only in the presence of supplementary hypotheses. One complete statement is that the condition 1 ≤ <i>k</i> ≤ <i>n</i> and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \left [ -\frac {1}{k},0\right ] \)</span> implies that <i>f</i>
<sub><i>k</i>,<i>p</i></sub> is concave.</p>
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar77">Theorem 6.15</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2, 1 ≤<i> k </i>≤<i> n</i>.<i>Consider the functions</i> </p><div id="Equnn" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{1,k}\left( A\right) =\frac{\widetilde{\sigma }_{k+1}\left( A\right) }{ \widetilde{\sigma }_{k}\left( A\right) },\ \ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left( \mathbb{R} \right) ,\ \ k\leq n-1 \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              
                <div id="Equno" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{2,k}\left( A\right) =\left[ \widetilde{\sigma }_{k}\left( A\right) \right] ^{1/k},\ \ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left( \mathbb{R} \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equnp" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{3,k}\left( A\right) =\ln \left[ \widetilde{\sigma }_{k}\left( A\right) \right] ,\ \ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left( \mathbb{R} \right) ,\ \ \ k\leq n-1 \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equnq" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{4,k}\left( A\right) =\ln \left[ \widetilde{\sigma }_{k}\left( A^{-1}\right) \right] ,\ \ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left( \mathbb{R} \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Then f</i>
                <sub>1,<i>k</i></sub>
                <i>, f</i>
                <sub>2,<i>k</i></sub>
                <i>, f</i>
                <sub>3,<i>k</i></sub>
                <i>are concave and f</i>
                <sub>4,<i>k</i></sub>
                <i>is convex.</i>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Concavity of <i>f</i>
<sub>1,<i>k</i></sub> and <i>f</i>
<sub>2,<i>k</i></sub> follows from Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar36">5.1</a>. and from Davis theorem. Concavity of <i>f</i>
<sub>3,<i>k</i></sub> and convexity of <i>f</i>
<sub>4,<i>k</i></sub> follow from Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar46">5.11</a>. and from Davis theorem. Convexity of <i>f</i>
<sub>4,<i>k</i></sub> was proved with variational techniques in the paper of Muir [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 55" title="W.W. Muir, Inequalities concerning the inverses of positive definite matrices. Proc. Edinb. Math. Soc 19,109–113 (1974)" href="#ref-CR55" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e104555">55</a>]. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar78">Corollary 6.16</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 3 <i>and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \mathbb {R} .\)</span> <i>Consider the functions:</i> </p><div id="Equnr" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{1}\left( A\right) =\left[ \det \left( A\right) \right] ^{p},\ \ \ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left( \mathbb{R} \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              
                <div id="Equns" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{2}\left( A\right) =\ln \left[ \det \left( A\right) \right] ,\ \ \ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left( \mathbb{R} \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equnt" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{3}\left( A\right) =\sqrt[3]{\left[ \mathit{\text{Tr}}\left( A\right) \right] ^{3}+2 \mathit{\text{Tr}}\left( A^{3}\right) -3\mathit{\text{Tr}}\left( A^{2}\right) \mathit{\text{Tr}}\left( A\right) },\ \ \ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left( \mathbb{R} \right). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">10.</span>
                    
                      <p>
                        <i>If</i>
                        <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \left [ 0,\frac {1}{n}\right ] \)</span>
                        <i>, then f</i>
                        <sub>1</sub>
                        <i>is concave.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">20.</span>
                    
                      <p>
                        <i>f</i>
                        <sub>2</sub>
                        <i>and f</i>
                        <sub>3</sub>
                        <i>are concave.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">30.</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If p </i>∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0]<i>, then f</i>
<sub>1</sub> <i>is convex.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Note that <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f_{1}\left ( A\right ) =\left \{ \left [ \widetilde {\sigma } _{n}\left ( A\right ) \right ] ^{1/n}\right \} ^{pn},\ A\in P_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ) .\)</span>If <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \left [ 0,\frac {1}{n}\right ] \)</span>, then <span class="mathjax-tex">\(pn\in \left [ 0,1 \right ] \)</span> hence <i>f</i>
<sub>1</sub> is concave. Note also that<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\ \ f_{2}\left ( A\right ) =\ln \left [ \widetilde {\sigma }_{n}\left ( A\right ) \right ] \)</span> and<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\ \ \ f_{3}\left ( A\right ) =\sqrt [3]{6}\sqrt [3]{\widetilde {\sigma }_{3}\left ( A\right ) }\ \ \ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ) .\)</span> By the preceding theorem the functions <i>f</i>
<sub>2</sub> and <i>f</i>
<sub>3</sub> are concave. Suppose now that <i>p</i> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0]. Then the function <i>g</i> defined below is convex. </p><div id="Equnu" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g\left( A\right) =\ln \left( f_{1}\left( A\right) \right) =p\ln \left[ \det \left( A\right) \right] =pf_{2}\left( A\right) ,\ \ \ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left( \mathbb{R} \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Since <i>f</i>
<sub>1</sub> = <i>e</i>
<sup><i>g</i></sup> it follows that <i>f</i>
<sub>1</sub> is convex. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar79">Theorem 6.17</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2 <i>and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \mathbb {R} ^{\ast }= \mathbb {R} -\left \{ 0\right \}\)</span><i>. Consider the functions</i> </p><div id="Equnv" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{1,p}\left( A\right) =\left[ \mathit{\text{Tr}}\left( A^{p}\right) \right] ^{1/p},\ \ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left( \mathbb{R} \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              
                <div id="Equnw" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{2,p}\left( A\right) =\ln \left[ \mathit{\text{Tr}}\left( A^{p}\right) \right] ,\ \ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left( \mathbb{R} \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equnx" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{3,p}\left( A\right) =\left\{ \left[ \mathit{\text{Tr}}\left( A\right) \right] ^{p}- \mathit{\text{Tr}}\left( A^{p}\right) \right\} ^{1/p},\ \ \ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left( \mathbb{R} \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">10.</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If p </i>∈ [1, <i>∞</i>)<i>, then f</i>
<sub>1,<i>p</i></sub> <i>is convex.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">20.</span>
                    
                      <p><i>p </i>∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0) ∪ (0, 1] <i>then f</i>
<sub>1,<i>p</i></sub> <i>is concave.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">30.</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If p </i>∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0)<i>, then f</i>
<sub>2,<i>p</i></sub> <i>is convex.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">40.</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If p </i>∈ (0, 1]<i>, then f</i>
<sub>2,<i>p</i></sub> <i>is concave.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">50.</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If p </i>∈ [1, <i>∞</i>)<i>, then f</i>
<sub>3,<i>p</i></sub> <i>is concave.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(g_{p}\left ( x\right ) =\left [ \sigma _{1}\left ( x^{p}\right ) \right ] ^{1/p}\)</span>, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}\)</span>. By Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar7">3.2</a>. <i>g</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is convex for <i>p</i> ∈ [1, <i>∞</i>) and <i>g</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is concave for <i>p</i> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0) ∪ (0, 1] The validity of assertions 1<sup>0</sup>. and 2<sup>0</sup>. follow from the equality <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f_{1,p}=\tilde {g}_{p}\)</span> and from the Davis theorem. Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(h_{k,p}\left ( x\right ) =\ln \left [ \sigma _{k}\left ( x^{p}\right ) \right ] \)</span>, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n},k\in \left \{ 1,2,\ldots ,n\right \} .\)</span> By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar46">5.11</a> it follows that <i>h</i>
<sub><i>k</i>,<i>p</i></sub> is convex for <i>p</i> ∈ (−<i>∞</i>, 0) and <i>h</i>
<sub><i>k</i>,<i>p</i></sub> is concave for <i>k</i> = <i>n</i> and <i>p</i> ∈ [0, <i>∞</i>) or for <i>k</i> ≤ <i>n</i> − 1 and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \left [ 0,1\right ] .\)</span> The validity of assertions 3<sup>0</sup>. and 4<sup>0</sup>. follows from the equality <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f_{2,p}= \tilde {h}_{k,p}\)</span> and from the Davis theorem.</p>
              <p>Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(u_{p}\left ( x\right ) =\left [ \sigma _{1}\left ( x\right ) \right ] ^{p}-\sigma _{1}\left ( x^{p}\right ) \)</span>, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}.\)</span> By Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar23">4.8</a> it follows that <i>u</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is concave for <i>p</i> ∈ [1, <i>∞</i>). By the Davis theorem <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f_{3,p}=\tilde {u}_{p}\)</span> is concave. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar80">Theorem 6.18</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2 <i>and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p,q\in \mathbb {R} ^{\ast }= \mathbb {R} -\left \{ 0\right \} .\)</span> <i>Consider the functions:</i> </p><div id="Equny" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{1,p}\left( A\right) =\frac{\mathit{\text{Tr}}\left( A^{p}\right) }{\det \left( A\right) },\ \ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left( \mathbb{R} \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              
                <div id="Equnz" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{2,p,q}\left( A\right) =\frac{\mathit{\text{Tr}}\left( A^{p}\right) }{\left[ \mathit{\text{ Tr}}\left( A\right) \right] ^{q}},\ \ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left( \mathbb{R} \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equoa" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{3,p,q}\left( A\right) =\frac{\mathit{\text{Tr}}\left( A^{p}\right) }{\mathit{\text{Tr}} \left( A^{q}\right) },\ \ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left( \mathbb{R} \right). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">10.</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If p </i>∈ [<i>n </i>+ 1, <i>∞</i>)<i>, then f</i>
<sub>1,<i>p</i></sub> <i>is convex.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">20.</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If p </i>≥<i> q </i>+ 1 <i>and q </i>≥ 0<i>, then f</i>
<sub>2,<i>p</i>,<i>q</i></sub> <i>is convex.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">30.</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If p </i>≥<i> q </i>+ 1 <i>and q </i>∈ (0, 1]<i>, then f</i>
<sub>3,<i>p</i>,<i>q</i></sub> <i>is convex.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(u\left ( A\right ) =\left [ \text{Tr}\left ( A^{p}\right ) \right ] ^{1/p},\ \ v\left ( A\right ) =\left [ \det \left ( A\right ) \right ] ^{1/n},\ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ) .\)</span> If <i>p</i> ∈ [<i>n</i> + 1, <i>∞</i>), then the function <span class="mathjax-tex">\(h\left ( x,y\right ) =x^{p}y^{-n},\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( x,y\right ) \in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{2}\)</span>, is convex. By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar79">6.17</a>. <i>u</i> is convex. By Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar78">6.16</a>. <i>v</i> is concave. Hence <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f_{1,p}\left ( A\right ) =h\left ( u\left ( A\right ) ,v\left ( A\right ) \right ) ,\ \ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ) \)</span> is convex. If <i>p</i> ≥ <i>q</i> + 1 and <i>q</i> ≥ 0, then <span class="mathjax-tex">\(g\left ( x,y\right ) =x^{p}y^{-q}\)</span> , <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( x,y\right ) \in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{2}\)</span> is convex. Since <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f_{2,p,q}\left ( A\right ) =g\left ( u\left ( A\right ) ,\text{Tr} \left ( A\right ) \right ) ,\ \ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ) \)</span> it follows that <i>f</i>
<sub>2,<i>p</i>,<i>q</i></sub> is convex. If <i>p</i> ≥ <i>q</i> + 1 and <i>q</i> ∈ (0, 1], then <span class="mathjax-tex">\(w\left ( A\right ) =\left [ \text{Tr}\left ( A^{q}\right ) \right ] ^{1/q},\ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ) \)</span> is concave hence <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f_{3,p,q}\left ( A\right ) =g\left ( u\left ( A\right ) ,w\left ( A\right ) \right ) ,\ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ) \)</span> is convex. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar81">Theorem 6.19</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2 <i>and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p,q\in \mathbb {R} \)</span><i>. Consider the function</i> </p><div id="Equob" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f\left( A\right) =\left[ \mathit{\text{Tr}}\left( A\right) \right] ^{p}\left[ \det \left( A\right) \right] ^{q},\ \ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left( \mathbb{R} \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">10.</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If p </i>+<i> q </i>≤ 0 <i>and q </i>≤ 0<i>, then f is convex.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">20.</span>
                    
                      <p>
                        <i>If</i>
                        <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p+q\in \left [ 0,1\right ] ,\)</span>
                        <span class="mathjax-tex">\(q\in \left [ 0,1\right ] ,\)</span>
                        <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p+nq\in \left [ 0,1\right ] \)</span>
                        <i>, then f is concave.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>
<span class="mathjax-tex">\(g\left ( x\right ) =\left [ \sigma _{1}\left ( x\right ) \right ] ^{p} \left [ \sigma _{n}\left ( x\right ) \right ] ^{q},\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}.\)</span>If <i>p</i> + <i>q</i> ≤ 0 and <i>q</i> ≤ 0, then by Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar41">5.6</a>. it follows that <i>g</i> is convex. By Davis theorem <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f=\tilde {g}\)</span> is convex. If <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p+q\in \left [ 0,1\right ] ,\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(q\in \left [ 0,1\right ] ,\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p+nq\in \left [ 0,1\right ] \)</span>, then by Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar42">5.7</a> it follows that <i>g</i> is concave. By Davis theorem <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f= \tilde {g}\)</span> is concave. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar82">Theorem 6.20</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2<i>,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(D=\left \{ A\in H_{n}\left ( \mathbb {C} \right ) :\exp \left ( \mathit{\text{Tr}}\left ( A\right ) \right ) &gt;\mathit{\text{Tr}}\left ( \exp \left ( A\right ) \right ) \right \} ,\)</span> </p><div id="Equoc" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{1}\left( A\right) =\ln \left[ \mathit{\text{Tr}}\left( \exp \left( A\right) \right) \right] ,\ \ \ \ A\in H_{n}\left( \mathbb{C} \right)\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>

              
                <div id="Equod" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{2}\left( A\right) =\ln \left[ \exp \left( \mathit{\text{Tr}}\left( A\right) \right) -\mathit{\text{Tr}}\left( \exp \left( A\right) \right) \right] ,\ \ \ \ A\in D.\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">10.</span>
                    
                      <p>
                        <i>f</i>
                        <sub>1</sub>
                        <i>is convex.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">20.</span>
                    
                      <p>
                        <i>D is convex.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">30.</span>
                    
                      <p>
                        <i>f</i>
                        <sub>2</sub>
                        <i>is concave.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(u\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots x_{n}\right ) =\ln \left ( \underset {i=1}{ \overset {n}{\sum }}\text{e}^{x_{i}}\right ) ,\ \left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots x_{n}\right ) \in \mathbb {R} ^{n}.\)</span> By Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar7">3.2</a> it follows that <i>u</i> is convex. By Davis theorem <span class="mathjax-tex">\( f_{1}=\tilde {u}\)</span> is convex. Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(v\left ( A\right ) =f_{1}\left ( A\right ) -\)</span>Tr<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( A\right ) ,\ \ A\in H_{n}\left ( \mathbb {C} \right ) .\)</span> Note that <i>v</i> is convex and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(D=\left \{ A\in H_{n}\left ( \mathbb {C} \right ) :v\left ( A\right ) &lt;0\right \} .\)</span> Hence <i>D</i> is convex. Let </p><div id="Equoe" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} U=\left\{ \left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots x_{n}\right) \in \mathbb{R} ^{n}:\exp \left( \overset{n}{\underset{i=1}{\sum }}x_{i}\right) -\underset{ i=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }}e^{x_{i}}&gt;0\right\}\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equof" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} w\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots x_{n}\right) =\ln \left[ \exp \left( \overset{n}{ \underset{i=1}{\sum }}x_{i}\right) -\underset{i=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }} e^{x_{i}}\right] ,\ \ \ \left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots x_{n}\right) \in U\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div><p> By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar27">4.12</a> the function <i>w</i> is concave. By the Davis theorem <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f_{2}=\tilde {w}\)</span> is concave on <i>D</i>. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar83">Corollary 6.21</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2<i>. Then the following inequalities hold:</i> </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">10.</span>

<p>
<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left [ \det \left ( A+B\right ) \right ] ^{1/n}\geq \left [ \det \left ( A\right ) \right ] ^{1/n}+\left [ \det \left ( B\right ) \right ] ^{1/n},\ \ \ \ A,B\in P_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ); \)</span>
</p>

</li>
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">20.</span>

<p>
<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\det \left ( A+B\right ) \geq \det \left ( A\right ) +\det \left ( B\right ) ,\ \ \ \ A,B\in P_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ); \)</span>
</p>

</li>
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">30.</span>

<p><i>Tr</i>
<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( e^{A+B}\right ) \geq \)</span><i>Tr</i>
<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( e^{A}\right ) \cdot \)</span><i>Tr</i>
<span class="mathjax-tex">\( \left ( e^{B}\right ) ,\ \ \ \ A,B\in S_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ); \)</span>
</p>

</li>
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">40.</span>


<div id="Equ19" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \frac{k^{n+1}}{\det \left( A_{1}+A_{2}+\ldots+A_{k}\right) }\leq \frac{1}{\det \left( A_{1}\right) }+\frac{1}{\det \left( A_{2}\right) }+\ldots+\frac{1}{\det \left( A_{k}\right) }, \end{aligned} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (6.19)
                </div></div>

<p><i>for every</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(A_{1},A_{2},\ldots ,A_{k}\in P_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right )\)</span>
</p>

</li>
</ol>

            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>The first inequality follows from the concavity of the function <span class="mathjax-tex">\( f\left ( A\right ) =\left [ \det \left ( A\right ) \right ] ^{1/n},\ \ A\in P_{n}\left ( \mathbb {R} \right ) .\)</span> The second inequality follows at once from the first inequality.</p>
              <p>From Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar82">6.20</a> it follows that the function <span class="mathjax-tex">\(g\left ( A\right ) =\ln \left [ \text{Tr}\left ( \exp \left ( A\right ) \right ) \right ]\)</span>, where <span class="mathjax-tex">\(A\in H_{n}\left ( \mathbb {C} \right ) \)</span>, is convex. Hence </p><div id="Equog" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \ln \left[ \text{Tr}\left( \exp \left( \frac{A+B}{2}\right) \right) \right] =g\left( \frac{A+B}{2}\right) \leq \frac{g\left( A\right) +g\left( B\right) }{2}=\ln \left( \sqrt{g\left( A\right) g\left( B\right) }\right). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>Thus </p><div id="Equoh" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \text{Tr}\left( \exp \left( A+B\right) \right) \leq \left[ \text{Tr}\left( \exp \left( \frac{A+B}{2}\right) \right) \right] ^{2}\leq \text{Tr}\left( e^{A}\right) \cdot \text{Tr}\left( e^{B}\right) ,\ \ A,B\in S_{n}\left( \mathbb{R} \right). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>By Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar78">6.16</a>. the function </p><div id="Equoi" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} h\left( A\right) =\frac{1}{\det \left( A\right) },\ \ \ \ A\in P_{n}\left( \mathbb{R} \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> is convex. By Jensen inequality applied to function <i>h</i> we obtain inequality (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ19">6.19</a>). In the case <i>k</i> = <i>n</i> = 2 the inequality (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ19">6.19</a>) becomes: </p><div id="Equoj" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \frac{8}{\det \left( A_{1}+A_{2}\right) }\leq \frac{1}{\det \left( A_{1}\right) }+\frac{1}{\det \left( A_{2}\right) },\ \ \ \ A_{1},A_{2}\in P_{2}\left( \mathbb{R} \right). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> If </p><div id="Equok" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} A_{j}=\left( \begin{array}{cc} x_{j} &amp; y_{j} \\ y_{j} &amp; z_{j} \end{array} \right) ,\ \ \ \ j=1,2, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> then the above inequality becomes </p><div id="Equol" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \frac{8}{\left( x_{1}+x_{2}\right) \left( z_{1}+z_{2}\right) -\left( y_{1}+y_{2}\right) ^{2}}=\frac{8}{\det \left( A_{1}+A_{2}\right) }\leq \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equom" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \leq \frac{1}{\det \left( A_{1}\right) }+\frac{1}{\det \left( A_{2}\right) }= \frac{1}{x_{1}z_{1}-y_{1}^{2}}+\frac{1}{x_{2}z_{2}-y_{2}^{2}}, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> where <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x_{j}z_{j}-y_{j}^{2}&gt;0, x_{j}&gt;0, z_{j}&gt;0\)</span>, <i>j</i> = 1, 2. The above inequality is the inequality from the statement of Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar34">4.19</a>. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar84">Proposition 6.22</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2<i>,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f:H_{n}\left ( \mathbb {C} \right ) \rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span><i>,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f\left ( A\right ) =\left [ \mathit{\text{Tr}}\left ( A\right ) \right ] ^{2}-4\det \left ( A\right ) ,\ \ \ A\in H_{n}\left ( \mathbb {C} \right ) .\)</span> <i>Then f is convex if and only if n </i>= 2.</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(B\left ( t\right ) =\)</span>diag<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( t,t,\ldots ,t\right ) ,\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(t\in \mathbb {R} .\)</span> If <i>f</i> is convex, then <span class="mathjax-tex">\(g\left ( t\right ) =f\left ( B\left ( t\right ) \right ) =n^{2}t^{2}-4t^{n},\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(t\in \mathbb {R}, \)</span> is convex. Note that </p><div id="Equon" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g^{\prime \prime }\left( t\right) =2n^{2}-4n\left( n-1\right) t^{n-2}=4n\left( n-1\right) \left[ \frac{n}{2\left( n-1\right) }-t^{n-2} \right] ,\  t\in \mathbb{R}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> If <i>n</i> ≥ 3, then <i>g</i>
<sup><i>′′</i></sup> change its sign on <span class="mathjax-tex">\( \mathbb {R} .\)</span>This shows that <i>n</i> = 2. Note that <span class="mathjax-tex">\(h\left ( x,y\right ) =\left ( x+y\right ) ^{2}-4xy=\left ( x-y\right ) ^{2},\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x,y\in \mathbb {R} \)</span> is convex and if <i>n</i> = 2 we have <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f=\tilde {h}.\)</span> By the Davis theorem it follows that <i>f</i> is convex. □</p>
            </div></div></section><section data-title="A Generalization of Muirhead Theorem and Applications"><div class="c-article-section" id="Sec7-section"><h2 id="Sec7" class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item"><span class="c-article-section__title-number">7 </span>A Generalization of Muirhead Theorem and Applications</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Sec7-content"><p>The Muirhead’s inequality, also known as the “bunching” method, generalizes the inequality between arithmetic and geometric means. It was named after its author Robert Franklin Muirhead, a Scottish mathematician who lived between 1860 and 1941. The inequality is between two homogeneous symmetric polynomials of several variables. It is often useful in proofs involving inequalities between homogeneous symmetric polynomials. For example, Newton’s inequality for elementary symmetric functions can be proved with Muirhead’s inequality.</p>
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar85">Theorem 7.1 (Muirhead)</h3>
              <p>
                <i>Let</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {x}=\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n},\)</span>
                <b>a</b>
                <b><i>=</i></b>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\ \left ( a_{1},a_{2},\ldots ,a_{n}\right ) \)</span>
                <i>,</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {b}=\left ( b_{1},b_{2},\ldots ,b_{n}\right ) \mathbf {\in \mathbb {R} }^{n}\)</span>
                <i>and</i>
                <b>a</b>
                <b><i>≤</i></b>
                <sub>
                  <i>HLP</i>
                </sub>
                <b>b</b>
                <b><i>.</i></b>
                <i>Then the following inequality holds</i>
                </p><div id="Equ20" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \underset{\sigma \in S_{n}}{\sum }x_{\sigma \left( 1\right) }^{a_{1}}x_{\sigma \left( 2\right) }^{a_{2}}\ldots x_{\sigma \left( n\right) }^{a_{n}}\leq \underset{\sigma \in S_{n}}{\sum }x_{\sigma \left( 1\right) }^{b_{1}}x_{\sigma \left( 2\right) }^{b_{2}}\ldots x_{\sigma \left( n\right) }^{b_{n}} \end{aligned} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (7.20)
                </div></div>
              
            <p>In his original paper Muirhead [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 56" title="R.F. Muirhead, Some methods applicable to identities and inequalities of symmetric algebraic functions of n letters. Proc. Edinb. Math. Soc. 21, 144–157 (1903)" href="#ref-CR56" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e114277">56</a>] has considered the case where the exponents <i>a</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> and <i>b</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> are positive integers. The extension of Muirhead result to real exponents was done by Hardy, Littlewood, and Polya in the book [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 27" title="G.H. Hardy, J.E. Littlewood, G. Polya, Inequalities, 2nd edn. (Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, 1952)" href="#ref-CR27" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e114295">27</a>]. Proofs of Muirhead’s inequality can be found in the monographs of Niculescu and Persson [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 58" title="C.P. Niculescu, L.-E. Persson, Convex Functions and Their Applications: A Contemporary Approach (Springer, Berlin, 2018)" href="#ref-CR58" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e114298">58</a>], Marshall Olkin and Arnold [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 49" title="A.W. Marshal, I. Olkin, B.C. Arnold, Inequalities : Theory of Majorization and Its Applications 2nd edn. (Springer, Berlin, 2011)" href="#ref-CR49" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e114301">49</a>], Garling [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 24" title="D.J.H. Garling, Inequalities: A Journey into Linear Analysis (Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, 2007)" href="#ref-CR24" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e114304">24</a>], and Manfrino et al. [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 47" title="R.B. Manfrino, J.A.G. Ortega, R.V. Delgado, Inequalities: A Mathematical Olympiad Approach (Springer, Berlin, 2010)" href="#ref-CR47" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e114307">47</a>].</p><p>A more complete statement of Muirhead theorem is the following (see the references [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 27" title="G.H. Hardy, J.E. Littlewood, G. Polya, Inequalities, 2nd edn. (Cambridge University Press, Cambridge, 1952)" href="#ref-CR27" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e114313">27</a>] and [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 47" title="R.B. Manfrino, J.A.G. Ortega, R.V. Delgado, Inequalities: A Mathematical Olympiad Approach (Springer, Berlin, 2010)" href="#ref-CR47" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e114316">47</a>]):</p>
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar86">Theorem 7.2 (Schulman [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 70" title="L.J. Schulman, Muirhead-Rado inequality for compact groups. Positivity 13, 559–574 (2009)" href="#ref-CR70" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e114325">70</a>])</h3>
              <p><i>Let</i> <b>a</b> <b><i>=</i></b> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\ \left ( a_{1},a_{2},\ldots ,a_{n}\right ) \)</span><i>,</i>
<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {,b}=\left ( b_{1},b_{2},\ldots ,b_{n}\right )\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {\in \mathbb {R} }^{n}\)</span><i>. Then inequality</i> (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ20">7.20</a>) <i>holds for every</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {x}=\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}\)</span> <i>if and only if</i> <b>a</b> <b><i>≤</i></b>
<sub><i>HLP</i></sub><b>b</b><b><i>.</i></b> <i>Equality takes place only when</i> <b>a</b> <b><i>=</i></b> <b>b</b> <i>or when all the x</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub><i>-s are equal.</i>
</p>
            <p>There are several generalizations of the Muirhead’s inequality. Marshall and Proschan proved in [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 50" title="A.W. Marshall, F. Proschan, An inequality for convex functions involving majorization. J. Math. Anal. Appl. 12, 87–90 (1965)" href="#ref-CR50" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e114682">50</a>] an inequality for the expectation of permutation-invariant and convex functions of permutation-invariant random variables. Muirhead’s theorem can be obtained as a special case of their result. A different generalization of Muirhead’s theorem was given by Proschan and Sethuraman [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 63" title="F. Proschan, J. Sethuraman, Two generalizations of Muirhead’s theorem. Bull. Calcutta Math. Soc. 69, 341–344 (1977)" href="#ref-CR63" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e114685">63</a>]. In this generalization the multivariate monomials were replaced with the product of log-convex functions. Their result states:</p>
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar87">Theorem 7.3</h3>
              <p>
                <i>For every</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(i\in \left \{ 1,2,\ldots ,n\right \} \)</span>
                <i>let</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\psi _{i}: \mathbb {R} \rightarrow \left ( 0,\infty \right ) \)</span>
                <i>be log-convex functions. If</i>
                <b>a</b>
                <b><i>=</i></b>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\ \left ( a_{1},a_{2},\ldots ,a_{n}\right ) \)</span>
                <i>,</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {,b}=\left ( b_{1},b_{2},\ldots ,b_{n}\right ) \mathbf {\in \mathbb {R} }^{n}\)</span>
                <i>and</i>
                <b>a</b>
                <b><i>≤</i></b>
                <sub>
                  <i>HLP</i>
                </sub>
                <b>b</b>
                <i>, then</i>
                </p><div id="Equoo" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \underset{\sigma \in S_{n}}{\sum }\left( \underset{i=1}{\overset{n}{\prod }} \psi _{\sigma \left( i\right) }\left( a_{i}\right) \right) \leq \underset{ \sigma \in S_{n}}{\sum }\left( \underset{i=1}{\overset{n}{\prod }}\psi _{\sigma \left( i\right) }\left( b_{i}\right) \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
            <p>In case that in the above theorem we take <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\psi _{i}\left ( z\right ) =x_{i}^{z}\)</span>, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(z\in \mathbb {R} \)</span> we obtain Muirhead’s inequality.</p><p>A continuous analog of Muirhead’s inequality was proved by Ryff [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 66" title="J.V. Ryff, On Muirhead’s theorem. Pacific J. Math. 21(3), 567–576 (1967)" href="#ref-CR66" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e115328">66</a>]. Muirhead’s majorization inequality was extended by Rado to the case of arbitrary permutation groups (that is subgroups of the permutations group <i>S</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub>). A generalization of Muirhead’s inequality considering such subgroups of permutations was given by Daykin [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 16" title="D.E. Daykin, Generalisation of the Muirhead-Rado inequality. Proc. Am. Math. Soc. 30(1), 84–86 (1971)" href="#ref-CR16" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e115338">16</a>]. In Kimelfeld [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 34" title="B. Kimelfeld, A generalization of Muirhead’s theorem. Linear Algebra Appl. 216, 205–209 (1995)" href="#ref-CR34" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e115341">34</a>] was defined the function </p><div id="Equop" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned}F(z)=\underset{g\in G}{\sum }\exp \left( \xi \left( gz\right) \right),\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div><p> where <i>G</i> is a finite group of linear transformations acting on a real linear space <i>E</i> and <i>ξ</i> is a linear functional on <i>E</i>. With the help of this function was formulated a generalization of Muirhead’s theorem. In the paper of Schulman [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 70" title="L.J. Schulman, Muirhead-Rado inequality for compact groups. Positivity 13, 559–574 (2009)" href="#ref-CR70" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e115433">70</a>] Muirhead’s inequality was further generalized to compact groups and their linear representations over the reals. Several discussions about Muirhead’s inequality can be found in the paper of Pecaric, Proschan, and Tong [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 61" title="J.E. Pečarić, F. Proschan, Y.L. Tong, Convex Functions, Partial Orderings, and Statistical Applications. Mathematics in Science and Engineering, vol. 187 (Academic Press, London, 1992)" href="#ref-CR61" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e115437">61</a>] and in the monograph of Marshall, Olkin, Arnold [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 49" title="A.W. Marshal, I. Olkin, B.C. Arnold, Inequalities : Theory of Majorization and Its Applications 2nd edn. (Springer, Berlin, 2011)" href="#ref-CR49" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e115440">49</a>].</p><p>In this section we shall give a different generalization of Muirhead theorem and we shall present various applications of the main result. Let <i>n</i> ≥ 2. Let <i>E</i> be a real linear space and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(A=\left ( a_{ij}\right ) \)</span> be an <i>n</i> × <i>n</i> matrix with real entries. We shall define the linear application <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\tilde {A} :E^{n}\rightarrow E^{n}\)</span>, </p><div id="Equoq" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \tilde{A}\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) =\left( \overset{n}{\underset{ i=1}{\sum }}a_{1j}x_{j},\overset{n}{\underset{i=1}{\sum }}a_{2j}x_{j},\ldots, \overset{n}{\underset{i=1}{\sum }}a_{nj}x_{j}\right) ,\ \ \ \ \ \ \ \  \left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) \in E^{n}\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>
<p>In the following we shall introduce a majorization preorder on the vectors of <i>E</i>
<sup><i>n</i></sup>. Let <i>E</i> be a real linear space and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {x}=\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in E^{n}\)</span>, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {y}=\left ( y_{1},y_{2},\ldots ,y_{n}\right ) \in E^{n}.\)</span> We shall say that <b>x</b> <i>majorize</i> <b>y</b> and we shall write <b>x</b> <b>≥</b>
<sub>HLP</sub><b>y</b> or <b>y</b> <b>≤</b>
<sub>HLP</sub><b>x</b> if there exists a double stochastic matrix <span class="mathjax-tex">\(A=\left ( a_{ij}\right ) \)</span> of order <i>n</i> such that <b>y</b>=<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\tilde {A}\mathbf {x.}\)</span>
</p>
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar88">Theorem 7.4</h3>
              <p><i>Let E be a real linear space, D be a convex subset of E</i>
<sup><i>n</i></sup> <i>and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f:D\rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span> <i>be a quasi-convex function. Suppose that D is permutation invariant that is if</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in D\)</span> <i>then</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( x_{\sigma \left ( 1\right ) },x_{\sigma \left ( 2\right ) },\ldots ,x_{\sigma \left ( n\right ) }\right ) \in D\)</span> <i>for every permutation σ </i>∈<i> S</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> <i>and f is symmetric. Then for every</i> <b>x</b><b><i>,</i></b> <b>y</b> ∈<i> D</i>, <b>x</b> <b><i>≥</i></b>
<sub><i>HLP</i></sub><b>y</b> <i>we have</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f\left ( \mathbf {x}\right ) \geq f\left ( \mathbf {y} \right ) .\)</span></p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <b>x,</b> <b>y</b> ∈ <i>D</i> be such that <b>x</b> <b>≥</b>
<sub>HLP</sub><b>y</b>. Then there exists a double stochastic matrix <span class="mathjax-tex">\(A=\left ( a_{ij}\right ) \)</span> of order <i>n</i> such that <b>y</b>=<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\tilde {A}\mathbf {x.}\)</span> By Birkhoff theorem there exist <i>m</i> permutation matrices <i>P</i>
<sub><i>j</i></sub>, <i>j</i> = 1, 2, …, <i>m</i> such that <span class="mathjax-tex">\(A=\underset {j=1}{\overset {m}{\sum }}\lambda _{j}P_{j}\)</span> where <i>λ</i>
<sub><i>j</i></sub> ≥ 0, <i>j</i> = 1, 2, …, <i>m</i> and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\underset {j=1}{ \overset {m}{\sum }}\lambda _{j}=1.\)</span> Note that </p><div id="Equor" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f\left( \mathbf{y}\right) =f\left( \tilde{A}\mathbf{x}\right) =f\left( \underset{j=1}{\overset{m}{\sum }}\lambda _{j}\tilde{P}_{j}\mathbf{x} \right) \leq \underset{1\leq j\leq m}{\max }f\left( \tilde{P}_{j}\mathbf{x} \right) =f\left( \mathbf{x}\right)\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div><p> □</p>
            <p>The following two theorems will be called Generalized Muirhead theorems.</p>
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar89">Theorem 7.5</h3>
              <p>
                <i>Let E be a real linear space, D be a convex subset of E and</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f:D\rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span>
                <i>be a convex function. Denote</i>
                </p><div id="Equos" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \varDelta =\left\{ \left( a_{1},a_{2},\ldots,a_{n}\right) \in \mathbb{R} ^{n}:\underset{i=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }}a_{i}=1,\ a_{i}\geq 0,\ i=1,2,\ldots,n\right\} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">10.</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {a=}\left ( a_{1},a_{2},\ldots ,a_{n}\right ) \mathbf {,b=} \left ( b_{1},b_{2},\ldots ,b_{n}\right )\in \varDelta \)</span> <i>with</i> <b>a</b> ≥<sub><i>HLP</i></sub><b>b</b><i>, then for every</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in D^{n}\)</span> <i>the following inequality holds:</i> </p><div id="Equ21" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \underset{\sigma \in S_{n}}{\sum }f\left( \underset{i=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }} a_{i}x_{\sigma \left( i\right) }\right) \geq \underset{\sigma \in S_{n}}{ \sum }f\left( \underset{i=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }}b_{i}x_{\sigma \left( i\right) }\right) \end{aligned} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (7.21)
                </div></div>

                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">20.</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {a=}\left ( a_{1},a_{2},\ldots ,a_{n}\right ) \mathbf {,b=} \left ( b_{1},b_{2},\ldots ,b_{n}\right ) \mathbf {\in \lbrack }0,\infty )^{n},\ D\)</span> <i>is a convex cone and</i> <b>a</b> <b><i>≥</i></b>
<sub><i>HLP</i></sub><b>b</b><i>, then for every</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in D^{n}\)</span> <i>inequality</i> (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ21">7.21</a>) <i>holds.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">30</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {a=}\left ( a_{1},a_{2},\ldots ,a_{n}\right ) \mathbf {,b=} \left ( b_{1},b_{2},\ldots ,b_{n}\right ) \mathbf {\in \mathbb {R} }^{n},\ D=E\)</span> <i>and</i> <b>a</b> <b><i>≥</i></b>
<sub><i>HLP</i></sub><b>b</b><i>, then for every</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\( \left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in D^{n}\)</span> <i>inequality</i> (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ21">7.21</a>) <i>holds.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>For every <b>x</b>=<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in D^{n}\)</span> consider the function </p><div id="Equot" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} F_{\mathbf{x}}\left( a_{1},a_{2},\ldots,a_{n}\right) =\underset{\sigma \in S_{n} }{\sum }f\left( \underset{i=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }}a_{i}x_{\sigma \left( i\right) }\right) ,\ \ \ \ \ \left( a_{1},a_{2},\ldots,a_{n}\right) \in \mathbb{R} ^{n} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that for every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in D^{n}\)</span> the function <i>F</i>
<sub><b>x</b></sub> is convex and symmetric on <i>Δ</i>. By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar88">7.4</a>., if <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {a=}\left ( a_{1},a_{2},\ldots ,a_{n}\right ) \mathbf {,b=}\left ( b_{1},b_{2},\ldots ,b_{n}\right ) \in \varDelta , \mathbf {a}\geq _{\text{HLP}} \mathbf {b}\)</span>, then for every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in D^{n}\)</span> the inequality (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ21">7.21</a>) holds.</p>
              <p>If <i>D</i> is a convex cone, then for every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in D^{n}\)</span> the function <i>F</i>
<sub><b>x</b></sub> is convex and symmetric on <b>[</b>0, <i>∞</i>)<sup><i>n</i></sup>. Applying Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar88">7.4</a>., if <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {a=}\left ( a_{1},a_{2},\ldots ,a_{n}\right ) \mathbf {,}\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {b=}\left ( b_{1},b_{2},\ldots ,b_{n}\right ) \mathbf {\in }[0,\infty )^{n}\mathbf {,}\)</span> with <b>a</b> <b>≥</b>
<sub>HLP</sub><b>b</b>, then for every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in D^{n}\)</span> the inequality (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ21">7.21</a>) holds.</p>
              <p>If <i>D</i> = <i>E</i>, then for every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in D^{n}\)</span> the function <i>F</i>
<sub><b>x</b></sub> is convex and symmetric on <span class="mathjax-tex">\( \mathbb {R} ^{n}.\)</span> By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar88">7.4</a>. if <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {a=}\left ( a_{1},a_{2},\ldots ,a_{n}\right ) \mathbf {,}\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {b=}\left ( b_{1},b_{2},\ldots ,b_{n}\right ) \mathbf {\in } \mathbb {R} ^{n}\mathbf {,}\)</span> and <b>a</b> <b>≥</b>
<sub>HLP</sub><b>b</b>, then for every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in D^{n}\)</span> the inequality (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ21">7.21</a>) holds. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar90">Theorem 7.6</h3>
              <p>
                <i>Let D</i>
                <sub>1</sub>
                <i>be a subset of</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{m}\)</span>
                <i>,</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f:D_{1}\rightarrow \mathbb {R}. \)</span>
                <i>Consider the set</i>
                </p><div id="Equou" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned}D_{2}=\left\{ \left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{m}\right) \in \mathbf{ \mathbb{R} }^{m}:\left( e^{x_{1}},e^{x_{2}},\ldots,e^{x_{m}}\right) \in D_{1}\right\} .\end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Suppose that U is a convex subset of D</i>
                <sub>2</sub>
                <i>, and</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(g:U\rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span>
                <i>,</i>
                </p><div id="Equov" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{m}\right) =f\left( e^{x_{1}},e^{x_{2}},\ldots,e^{x_{m}}\right) ,\ \ \ \left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{m}\right) \in U\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p><i>is a convex function. If</i> <b>a</b> <b><i>=</i></b> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\ \left ( a_{1},a_{2},\ldots ,a_{n}\right ) \)</span><i>,</i>
<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {,b}=\left ( b_{1},b_{2},\ldots ,b_{n}\right ) \mathbf {\in \mathbb {R} }^{n}\)</span><i>and</i> <b>a</b> <b><i>≤</i></b>
<sub><i>HLP</i></sub><b>b</b> <i>then for every</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {x} _{i}=\left ( x_{i,1},x_{i,2},\ldots ,x_{i,n}\right ) \in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}\)</span><i>, i </i>= 1, 2, …, <i>m, the following inequality holds</i> </p><div id="Equow" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \underset{\sigma \in S_{n}}{\sum }f&amp;\left( x_{\sigma \left( 1\right) ,1}^{a_{1}}x_{\sigma \left( 2\right) ,1}^{a_{2}}\ldots x_{\sigma \left( n\right) ,1}^{a_{n}},x_{\sigma \left( 1\right) ,2}^{a_{1}}x_{\sigma \left( 2\right) ,2}^{a_{2}}\ldots x_{\sigma \left( n\right) ,2}^{a_{n}},\ldots,x_{\sigma \left( 1\right) ,m}^{a_{1}}x_{\sigma \left( 2\right) ,m}^{a_{2}}\ldots \right. \\ &amp;\left. x_{\sigma \left( n\right) ,m}^{a_{n}},\right) \leq \end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>

              
                <div id="Equox" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \leq \underset{\sigma \in S_{n}}{\sum }f&amp;\left( x_{\sigma \left( 1\right) ,1}^{b_{1}}x_{\sigma \left( 2\right) ,1}^{b_{2}}\ldots x_{\sigma \left( n\right) ,1}^{b_{n}},x_{\sigma \left( 1\right) ,2}^{b_{1}}x_{\sigma \left( 2\right) ,2}^{b_{2}}\ldots x_{\sigma \left( n\right) ,2}^{b_{n}},\ldots,x_{\sigma \left( 1\right) ,m}^{b_{1}}x_{\sigma \left( 2\right) ,m}^{b_{2}}\ldots\right. \\ &amp; \left.x_{\sigma \left( n\right) ,m}^{b_{n}}\right).\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Since the function <i>g</i> is convex, by the Generalized Muirhead theorem (Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar89">7.5</a>), we have </p><div id="Equoy" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><img src="//media.springernature.com/lw557/springer-static/image/chp%3A10.1007%2F978-3-030-27407-8_4/MediaObjects/450928_1_En_4_Equoy_HTML.png" class="u-display-block" alt=""></div></div><p> for <span class="mathjax-tex">\({\mathbf {y}}_{i}=\left ( y_{i,1},y_{i,2},\ldots ,y_{i,n}\right ) \in \mathbb {R} ^{n}\)</span>, <i>i</i> = 1, 2, …, <i>m</i>. This is equivalent to </p><div id="Equoz" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \underset{\sigma \in S_{n}}{\sum }g\left( \underset{j=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }} a_{j}y_{\sigma \left( j\right) ,1},\underset{j=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }} a_{j}y_{\sigma \left( j\right) ,2},\ldots,\underset{j=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }} a_{j}y_{\sigma \left( j\right) ,m}\right) \leq \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equpa" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned}\leq \underset{\sigma \in S_{n}}{\sum }g\left( \underset{j=1}{\overset{n}{ \sum }}b_{j}y_{\sigma \left( j\right) ,1},\underset{j=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }} b_{j}y_{\sigma \left( j\right) ,2},\ldots,\underset{j=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }} b_{j}y_{\sigma \left( j\right) ,m}\right), \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> hence </p><div id="Equpb" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \underset{\sigma \in S_{n}}{\sum }f\left( \exp \left( \underset{j=1}{\overset {n}{\sum }}a_{j}y_{\sigma \left( j\right) ,1}\right) ,\exp \left( \underset{ j=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }}a_{j}y_{\sigma \left( j\right) ,2}\right) ,\ldots,\exp \left( \underset{j=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }}a_{j}y_{\sigma \left( j\right) ,m}\right) \right) \leq \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equpc" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \leq \underset{\sigma \in S_{n}}{\sum }f\left( \exp \left( \underset{j=1}{ \overset{n}{\sum }}b_{j}y_{\sigma \left( j\right) ,1}\right) ,\exp \left( \underset{j=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }}b_{j}y_{\sigma \left( j\right) ,2}\right) ,\ldots,\exp \left( \underset{j=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }}b_{j}y_{\sigma \left( j\right) ,m}\right) \right). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Denote <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x_{i,j}=\exp \left ( y_{i,j}\right ) \)</span>. Taking into account that </p><div id="Equpd" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \exp \left( \underset{j=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }}a_{j}y_{\sigma \left( j\right) ,i}\right) =\overset{n}{\underset{j=1}{{\LARGE \varPi }}}\left[ \exp \left( y_{\sigma \left( j\right) ,i}\right) \right] ^{a_{j}}=\overset{n}{\underset{ j=1}{{\LARGE \varPi }}}x_{\sigma \left( j\right) ,i}^{a_{j}}\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div><p> we obtain the inequality from the statement. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar91">Theorem 7.7</h3>
              <p><i>Let m </i>≥ 2, <i>n </i>≥ 2<i>,</i> <b>a</b> <b><i>=</i></b> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\ \left ( a_{1},a_{2},\ldots ,a_{n}\right ) \)</span><i>,</i>
<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {,b}=\left ( b_{1},b_{2},\ldots ,b_{n}\right ) \mathbf {\in \mathbb {R} }^{n}\)</span><i>,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x_{ij}\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) \)</span><i>, i </i>= 1, 2, …, <i>n, j </i>= 1, 2, …, <i>m</i>. <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i> </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">10.</span>

<p><i>If</i> <b>a</b> <b><i>≤</i></b>
<sub><i>HLP</i></sub><b>b</b><i>, then</i> </p><div id="Equpe" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \underset{\sigma \in S_{n}}{\prod }\left( \underset{j=1}{\overset{m}{\sum } }x_{\sigma \left( 1\right) ,j}^{a_{1}}x_{\sigma \left( 2\right) ,j}^{a_{2}}\ldots x_{\sigma \left( n\right) ,j}^{a_{n}}\right) \leq \underset{ \sigma \in S_{n}}{\prod }\left( \underset{j=1}{\overset{m}{\sum }} x_{\sigma \left( 1\right) ,j}^{b_{1}}x_{\sigma \left( 2\right) ,j}^{b_{2}}\ldots x_{\sigma \left( n\right) ,j}^{b_{n}}\right)\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>


</li>
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">20.</span>

<p><i>If</i> <b>a</b> <b><i>≤</i></b>
<sub><i>HLP</i></sub><b>b</b> <i>and for every σ </i>∈<i> S</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> <i>we have</i> </p><div id="Equpf" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} x_{\sigma \left( 1\right) ,1}^{a_{1}}x_{\sigma \left( 2\right) ,1}^{a_{2}}\ldots x_{\sigma \left( n\right) ,1}^{a_{n}}&gt;\underset{j=2}{\overset{m }{\sum }}x_{\sigma \left( 1\right) ,j}^{a_{1}}x_{\sigma \left( 2\right) ,j}^{a_{2}}\ldots x_{\sigma \left( n\right) ,j}^{a_{n}}\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>

<p><i>and</i> </p><div id="Equpg" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} x_{\sigma \left( 1\right) ,1}^{b_{1}}x_{\sigma \left( 2\right) ,1}^{b_{2}}\ldots x_{\sigma \left( n\right) ,1}^{b_{n}}&gt;\left( \underset{j=2}{ \overset{m}{\sum }}x_{\sigma \left( 1\right) ,j}^{b_{1}}x_{\sigma \left( 2\right) ,j}^{b_{2}}\ldots x_{\sigma \left( n\right) ,j}^{b_{n}}\right)\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>

<p><i>then the following inequality holds</i> </p><div id="Equph" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \underset{\sigma \in S_{n}}{\prod }\left( x_{\sigma \left( 1\right) ,1}^{a_{1}}x_{\sigma \left( 2\right) ,1}^{a_{2}}\ldots x_{\sigma \left( n\right) ,1}^{a_{n}}-\underset{j=2}{\overset{m}{\sum }}x_{\sigma \left( 1\right) ,j}^{a_{1}}x_{\sigma \left( 2\right) ,j}^{a_{2}}\ldots x_{\sigma \left( n\right) ,j}^{a_{n}}\right) \geq \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>


<div id="Equpi" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \geq \underset{\sigma \in S_{n}}{\prod }\left( x_{\sigma \left( 1\right) ,1}^{b_{1}}x_{\sigma \left( 2\right) ,1}^{b_{2}}\ldots x_{\sigma \left( n\right) ,1}^{b_{n}}-\left( \underset{j=2}{\overset{m}{\sum }}x_{\sigma \left( 1\right) ,j}^{b_{1}}x_{\sigma \left( 2\right) ,j}^{b_{2}}\ldots x_{\sigma \left( n\right) ,j}^{b_{n}}\right) \right).\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>


</li>
</ol>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f_{1}\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{m}\right ) =\ln \left ( x_{1}+x_{2}+\ldots +x_{m}\right ) ,\ \left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{m}\right ) \in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{m}.\)</span> Note that </p><div id="Equpj" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{1}\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{m}\right) =f_{1}\left( e^{x_{1}},e^{x_{2}},\ldots,e^{x_{m}}\right) = \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equpk" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =\ln \left( e^{x_{1}}+e^{x_{2}}+\ldots+e^{x_{m}}\right) ,\ \ \ \ \ \ \left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{m}\right) \in \mathbb{R} ^{m}\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div><p> is a convex function. By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar90">7.6</a>. it follows the first assertion holds.</p>
              <p>Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(D_{1}=\left \{ \left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{m}\right ) \in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{m}:x_{1}&gt;x_{2}+\ldots +x_{m}\right \} ,\)</span> and </p><div id="Equpl" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned}f_{2}\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{m}\right) =\ln \left( x_{1}-x_{2}+\ldots+x_{m}\right) ,\ \left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{m}\right) \in D_{1}.\end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Let </p><div id="Equpm" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} D_{2}=\left\{ \left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{m}\right) \in \mathbb{R} ^{m}:e^{x_{1}}&gt;e^{x_{2}}+e^{x_{3}}+\ldots+e^{x_{m}}\right\} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equpn" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{2}\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{m}\right) =f_{2}\left( e^{x_{1}},e^{x_{2}},\ldots,e^{x_{m}}\right) = \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equpo" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =\ln \left( e^{x_{1}}-e^{x_{2}}-\ldots-e^{x_{m}}\right) ,\ \ \ \ \ \ \left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{m}\right) \in D_{2} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that <i>g</i>
<sub>2</sub> is concave. By Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar90">7.6</a>. it follows the second assertion holds. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar92">Theorem 7.8</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2<i>,</i> <b>a</b> <b><i>=</i></b> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\ \left ( a_{1},a_{2},\ldots ,a_{n}\right ) \)</span><i>,</i>
<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {,b}=\left ( b_{1},b_{2},\ldots ,b_{n}\right ) \mathbf {\in \mathbb {R} }^{n}\)</span><i>,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x_{i},y_{i}\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) \)</span><i>, i </i>= 1, 2, …, <i>n</i>. <i>If</i> <b>a</b> <b><i>≤</i></b>
<sub><i>HLP</i></sub><b>b</b> <i>then the following inequalities hold:</i> </p><div id="Equpp" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \underset{\sigma \in S_{n}}{\prod }\left( x_{\sigma \left( 1\right) }^{a_{1}}x_{\sigma \left( 2\right) }^{a_{2}}\ldots x_{\sigma \left( n\right) }^{a_{n}}+y_{\sigma \left( 1\right) }^{a_{1}}y_{\sigma \left( 2\right) }^{a_{2}}\ldots y_{\sigma \left( n\right) }^{a_{n}}\right) \leq \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              
                <div id="Equpq" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \leq \underset{\sigma \in S_{n}}{\prod }\left( x_{\sigma \left( 1\right) }^{a_{1}}x_{\sigma \left( 2\right) }^{a_{2}}\ldots x_{\sigma \left( n\right) }^{a_{n}}+y_{\sigma \left( 1\right) }^{a_{1}}y_{\sigma \left( 2\right) }^{a_{2}}\ldots y_{\sigma \left( n\right) }^{a_{n}}\right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equpr" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \sqrt[n]{\underset{i=1}{\overset{n}{\prod }}\left( x_{i}+y_{i}\right) }\geq \sqrt[n]{x_{1}x_{2}\ldots x_{n}}+\sqrt[n]{y_{1}y_{2}\ldots y_{n}}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>The first inequality is a special case of the first inequality from the preceding theorem. In the case we replace <i>y</i>
<sub>1</sub> = <i>y</i>
<sub>2</sub> = … = <i>y</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> = 1 in the first inequality we obtain an inequality from [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 12" title="K.M. Chong, Spectral order preserving matrices and Muirhead’s theorem. Trans. Am. Math. Soc. 200, 437–444 (1974)" href="#ref-CR12" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e128251">12</a>]. The second inequality (known as the Huygens inequality) follows from the first inequality in the special case <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {a=}\left ( \frac {1}{n},\frac {1}{n}, \frac {1}{n},\ldots \frac {1}{n}\right ) \)</span>, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {b=}\left ( 1,0,0,\ldots ,0\right ) .\)</span> □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar93">Corollary 7.9</h3>
              <p><i>Let</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x_{i},y_{i}\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) \)</span><i>, i </i>= 1, 2, 3<i>. Then the following inequality holds</i> </p><div id="Equps" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left[ \left( x_{1}^{3}+y_{1}^{3}\right) \left( x_{2}^{3}+y_{2}^{3}\right) \left( x_{3}^{3}+y_{3}^{3}\right) \right] ^{2}\geq\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>

              
                <div id="Equpt" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} &amp;\left( x_{1}^{2}x_{2}+y_{1}^{2}y_{2}\right) \left( x_{1}^{2}x_{3}+y_{1}^{2}y_{3}\right) \left( x_{2}^{2}x_{1}+y_{2}^{2}y_{1}\right) \left( x_{2}^{2}x_{3}+y_{2}^{2}y_{3}\right) \\ &amp;\left( x_{3}^{2}x_{1}+y_{3}^{2}y_{1}\right) \left( x_{3}^{2}x_{2}+y_{3}^{2}y_{2}\right) \geq \end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equpu" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \geq \left( x_{1}x_{2}x_{3}+y_{1}y_{2}y_{3}\right) ^{6} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {a=}\left ( 3,0,0\right ) ,\mathbf {b=}\left ( 2,1,0\right ) , \mathbf {c=}\left ( 1,1,1\right ) .\)</span> Note that <b>a</b> <b>≥</b>
<sub>HLP</sub><b>b</b> <b>≥</b>
<sub>HLP</sub><b>c</b> and apply the first inequality from the statement of the preceding theorem. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar94">Theorem 7.10</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2<i>,</i> <b>a</b> <b><i>=</i></b> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\ \left ( a_{1},a_{2},\ldots ,a_{n}\right ) \)</span><i>,</i>
<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {b}=\left ( b_{1},b_{2},\ldots ,b_{n}\right ) \mathbf {\in \mathbb {R} }^{n}\)</span><i>,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(a_{i},b_{i},x_{i},y_{i}\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) \)</span><i>, i </i>= 1, 2, …, <i>n</i>. <i>If</i> <b>a</b> <b><i>≤</i></b>
<sub><i>HLP</i></sub><b>b</b> <i>and x</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> ≥<i> y</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub><i>, i </i>= 1, 2, …, <i>n, then the following inequalities hold</i> </p><div id="Equpv" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \underset{\sigma \in S_{n}}{\prod }\left( x_{\sigma \left( 1\right) }^{a_{1}}x_{\sigma \left( 2\right) }^{a_{2}}\ldots x_{\sigma \left( n\right) }^{a_{n}}-y_{\sigma \left( 1\right) }^{a_{1}}y_{\sigma \left( 2\right) }^{a_{2}}\ldots y_{\sigma \left( n\right) }^{a_{n}}\right) \geq \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              
                <div id="Equpw" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \geq \underset{\sigma \in S_{n}}{\prod }\left( x_{\sigma \left( 1\right) }^{a_{1}}x_{\sigma \left( 2\right) }^{a_{2}}\ldots x_{\sigma \left( n\right) }^{a_{n}}-y_{\sigma \left( 1\right) }^{a_{1}}y_{\sigma \left( 2\right) }^{a_{2}}\ldots y_{\sigma \left( n\right) }^{a_{n}}\right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equpx" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \sqrt[n]{\underset{i=1}{\overset{n}{\prod }}\left( x_{i}-y_{i}\right) }\geq \sqrt[n]{x_{1}x_{2}\ldots x_{n}}-\sqrt[n]{y_{1}y_{2}\ldots y_{n}} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>The proof is similar with the proof of the preceding theorem. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar95">Corollary 7.11</h3>
              <p><i>Let x</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> &gt;<i> y</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> &gt; 0, <i>i </i>= 1, 2, 3<i>. Then the following inequality holds</i> </p><div id="Equpy" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left[ \left( x_{1}^{3}-y_{1}^{3}\right) \left( x_{2}^{3}-y_{2}^{3}\right) \left( x_{3}^{3}-y_{3}^{3}\right) \right] ^{2}\geq\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>

              
                <div id="Equpz" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} &amp; \left( x_{1}^{2}x_{2}-y_{1}^{2}y_{2}\right) \left( x_{1}^{2}x_{3}-y_{1}^{2}y_{3}\right) \left( x_{2}^{2}x_{1}-y_{2}^{2}y_{1}\right) \left( x_{2}^{2}x_{3}-y_{2}^{2}y_{3}\right) \\ &amp;\left( x_{3}^{2}x_{1}-y_{3}^{2}y_{1}\right) \left( x_{3}^{2}x_{2}-y_{3}^{2}y_{2}\right) \geq\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equqa" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left( x_{1}x_{2}x_{3}-y_{1}y_{2}y_{3}\right) ^{6} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
            <p>Let <i>E</i> be a real linear space<i>, D</i> be a convex set of <i>E</i>, <i>n</i> ∈ <i>N</i>, <i>n</i> ≥ 1, <i>k</i> ∈{1, 2, …, <i>n</i>}, <i>f</i> : <i>D</i> → <i>R</i>, <i>x</i> = (<i>x</i>
<sub>1</sub>, …, <i>x</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub>) ∈ <i>D</i>
<sup><i>n</i></sup>. We define the <i>mixed mean</i> of order <i>k</i>, <i>n</i>: </p><div id="Equqb" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \overline{f}_{k,n}(x)=\frac{1}{{n\choose k}}\sum_{1\leq i_{1}&lt;\dots &lt;i_{k}\leq \ n}f\left( \frac{x_{i_{1}}+\dots +x_{i_{k}}}{k}\right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
<p>The following theorem is a refinement of Jensen’s inequality without weights. For a proof we refer to the papers of Mitrinović and Pečarić [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 52" title="D.S. Mitrinović, J. Pečarić, Unified treatment of some inequalities for mixed means. sterreich. Akad. Wiss. Math. Nat. Kl. Sitzungsber. II, 197(8–10), 391–397 (1988)" href="#ref-CR52" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e131574">52</a>], Pečaric and Volenec [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 62" title="J.E. Pečarić, V. Volenec, Interpolation of the Jensen inequality with some applications. Sitzungsber. Oesterr. Akad. Wiss. Abt. II 197, 463–467 (1988)" href="#ref-CR62" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e131577">62</a>] (see also Pečarić [<a data-track="click" data-track-action="reference anchor" data-track-label="link" data-test="citation-ref" aria-label="Reference 60" title="J.E. Pečarić, Remark on an inequality of S. Gabler. J. Math. Anal. Appl. 184(1), 19–21 (1994)" href="#ref-CR60" id="ref-link-section-d4834617e131580">60</a>]).</p>
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar96">Theorem 7.12</h3>
              <p><i>Let E be a real linear space, D be a convex set of E,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f:D\rightarrow \mathbb {R}\)</span> <i>be a convex function. Then for every</i> <b>x</b> = (<i>x</i>
<sub>1</sub>, …, <i>x</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub>) ∈<i> D</i>
<sup><i>n</i></sup> <i>the following inequalities hold:</i> </p><div id="Equqc" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><img src="//media.springernature.com/lw496/springer-static/image/chp%3A10.1007%2F978-3-030-27407-8_4/MediaObjects/450928_1_En_4_Equqc_HTML.png" class="u-display-block" alt=""></div></div>

            <p>In the following we shall give a proof of Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar96">7.12</a>. by using the Generalized Muirhead’s inequality (Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar89">7.5</a>).</p>
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof of Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar96">7.12</a>.</h3>
              <p>Consider the <i>n</i>-dimensional vectors </p><div id="Equqd" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} {\mathbf{a}}_{1}=\left( a_{1,1},a_{1,2},\ldots,a_{1,n}\right) =\left( 1,0,0,\ldots,0\right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equqe" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} {\mathbf{a}}_{2}=\left( a_{2,1},a_{2,2},\ldots,a_{2,n}\right) =\left( \frac{1}{2}, \frac{1}{2},0,\ldots,0\right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equqf" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \ldots\ldots\ldots. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equqg" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} {\mathbf{a}}_{n}=\left( a_{n,1},a_{n,2},\ldots,a_{n,n}\right) =\left( \frac{1}{n}, \frac{1}{n},\frac{1}{n},\ldots.,\frac{1}{n}\right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that for every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(k\in \left \{ 1,2,\ldots ,n\right \} \)</span> we have </p><div id="Equqh" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \overline{f}_{k,n}(\mathbf{x})=\frac{1}{n!}\underset{\sigma \in S_{n}}{\sum }f\left( a_{k,1}x_{\sigma \left( 1\right) }+a_{k,2}x_{\sigma \left( 2\right) }+\ldots+a_{k,n}x_{\sigma \left( n\right) }\right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> and </p><div id="Equqi" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} {\mathbf{a}}_{1}\geq _{\text{HLP}}{\mathbf{a}}_{2}\geq _{\text{HLP}}\ldots.\geq _{ \text{HLP}}{\mathbf{a}}_{n} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By the Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar89">7.5</a> (the generalized Muirhead’s theorem) the sequence of inequalities from the statement of Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar96">7.12</a>. holds. □</p>
            <p>The following result is a corollary of the preceding theorem.</p>
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar97">Theorem 7.13</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2<i>. For every</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(k\in \left \{ 1,2,\ldots ,n\right \} \)</span> <i>denote by σ</i>
<sub><i>k</i></sub> <i>the k </i>−<i>th elementary symmetric polynomial in n variables. If</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {x}=(x_{1},\dots ,x_{n})\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}\)</span> <i>and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \mathbb {R} \)</span><i>, we shall write</i> </p><div id="Equqj" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \sigma _{k}\left( {\mathbf{x}}^{p}\right) =\sigma _{k}\left( x_{1}^{p},x_{2}^{p},\ldots,x_{n}^{p}\right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>Then for every</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {x}=(x_{1},\dots ,x_{n})\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}\)</span>
                <i>the following inequalities hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">10.</span>
                    
                      
                        <div id="Equqk" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \frac{\sigma _{1}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) }{{n\choose 1}}\geq \frac{\sigma _{2}\left( {\mathbf{x}}^{1/2}\right) }{{n\choose 2}}\geq \ldots\geq \frac{\sigma _{k}\left( {\mathbf{x}}^{1/k}\right) }{{n\choose k}}\geq \ldots\geq \frac{\sigma _{n}\left( {\mathbf{x}}^{1/n}\right) }{{n\choose n}} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
                      
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">20</span>
                    
                      
                        <div id="Equql" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \sigma _{k}\left( {\mathbf{x}}^{k+1}\right) {n\choose k+1}\geq \sigma _{k+1}\left( {\mathbf{x}}^{k}\right) {n\choose k}\mathit{\ \ \ \ \ \ \  } k=1,2,\ldots,n-1 \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
                      
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f\left ( t\right ) =e^{t},t\in \mathbb {R} .\)</span> If <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {y}=\left ( y_{1},y_{2},\ldots y_{n}\right ) \in \)</span>
<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}\)</span> , then </p><div id="Equqm" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \overline{f}_{k,n}(\mathbf{y})=\frac{1}{{n\choose k}}\sum_{1\leq i_{1}&lt;\dots &lt;i_{k}\leq n}f\left( \frac{y_{i_{1}}+\dots +y_{i_{k}}}{k}\right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Denote <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f\left ( y_{i}\right ) =x_{i}\)</span>, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(i\in \left \{ 1,2,\ldots ,n\right \} .\)</span> Then </p><div id="Equqn" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \overline{f}_{k,n}(\mathbf{y})=\frac{1}{{n\choose k}}\sum_{1\leq i_{1}&lt;\dots &lt;i_{k}\leq n}\left[ f\left( y_{i_{1}}\right) \right] ^{1/k}\left[ f\left( y_{i_{2}}\right) \right] ^{1/k}\ldots\left[ f\left( y_{i_{k}}\right) \right] ^{1/k}= \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equqo" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =\frac{1}{{n\choose k}}\sum_{1\leq i_{1}&lt;\dots &lt;i_{k}\leq n}x_{i_{1}}^{1/k}x_{i_{2}}^{1/k}\ldots x_{i_{k}}^{1/k}=\frac{\sigma _{k}\left( {\mathbf{x}}^{1/k}\right) }{{n\choose k}} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Since <i>f</i> is convex, by Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar96">7.12</a>. it follows that </p><div id="Equqp" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \overline{f}_{1,n}(\mathbf{y})\geq \overline{f}_{2,n}(\mathbf{y})\geq \ldots\geq \overline{f}_{n,n}(\mathbf{y}) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Thus the first inequality was proved. The second inequality follows from the first inequality performing a substitution, □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar98">Corollary 7.14</h3>
              <p><i>Let E be a linear normed space,</i> <b>x</b>
<sub>1</sub>, <b>x</b>
<sub>2</sub>, …, <b>x</b>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> ∈<i> E and p </i>≥ 1. <i>Then the following sequence of inequalities holds:</i> </p><div id="Equqq" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \frac{\overset{n}{\underset{i=1}{\sum }}\left\Vert \mathbf{x} _{i}\right\Vert ^{p}}{{n\choose 1}}\geq \frac{\overset{n}{\underset{i&lt;j}{\sum } }\left\Vert {\mathbf{x}}_{i}+{\mathbf{x}}_{j}\right\Vert ^{p}}{2^{p}{n\choose 2}} \geq \frac{\overset{n}{\underset{i&lt;j&lt;k}{\sum }}\left\Vert {\mathbf{x}}_{i}+ {\mathbf{x}}_{j}+{\mathbf{x}}_{k}\right\Vert ^{p}}{3^{p}{n\choose 3}}\geq \ldots \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              
                <div id="Equqr" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \geq \frac{\left\Vert {\mathbf{x}}_{1}+{\mathbf{x}}_{2}+\ldots+\mathbf{x} _{n}\right\Vert ^{p}}{n^{p}{n\choose n}} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Note that <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f\left ( \mathbf {x}\right ) =\left \Vert \mathbf {x} \right \Vert ^{p},\mathbf {x}\in E\)</span> is a convex function. The inequality from the statement follows by applying Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar96">7.12</a>. to function <i>f</i>. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar99">Corollary 7.15</h3>
              <p><i>Let x</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub>, <i>y</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> ∈ [0, <i>∞</i>), <i>i </i>= 1, 2, …, <i>n. Then the following inequalities hold:</i> </p><div id="Equqs" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \frac{\overset{n}{\underset{i=1}{\sum }}\sqrt{x_{i}y_{i}}}{{n\choose 1}}\leq \frac{\overset{n}{\underset{i&lt;j}{\sum }}\sqrt{\left( x_{i}+x_{j}\right) \left( y_{i}+y_{j}\right) }}{{n\choose 2}}\leq \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              
                <div id="Equqt" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \leq \frac{\overset{n}{\underset{i&lt;j&lt;k}{\sum }}\sqrt{\left( x_{i}+x_{j}+x_{k}\right) \left( y_{i}+y_{j}+y_{k}\right) }}{3{n\choose 3}}\leq \ldots.\leq \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Eququ" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \leq \frac{\sqrt{\left( x_{1}+x_{2}+\ldots+x_{n}\right) \left( y_{1}+y_{2}+\ldots+y_{n}\right) }}{n{n\choose n}} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>and</i>
                </p><div id="Equqv" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left[ \underset{i=1}{\overset{n}{\prod }}\left( x_{i}+y_{i}\right) \right] ^{1/{n\choose 1}}\geq \left[ \underset{i&lt;j}{\prod }\left( \sqrt{x_{i}x_{j}}+ \sqrt{y_{i}y_{j}}\right) \right] ^{{}^{1/{n\choose 2}}}\geq \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equqw" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \geq \left[ \underset{i&lt;j&lt;k}{\prod }\left( \sqrt[3]{x_{i}x_{j}x_{k}}+\sqrt{ y_{i}y_{j}y_{k}}\right) \right] ^{{}^{1/{n\choose 3}}}\geq \ldots\geq \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equqx" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \geq \left( \sqrt[n]{x_{1}x_{2}\ldots x_{n}}+\sqrt[n]{y_{1}y_{2}\ldots y_{n}}\right) ^{1/{n\choose n}}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>In order to prove the first sequence of inequalities we apply Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar96">7.12</a>. to the concave function <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f\left ( x,y\right ) =\sqrt {xy},\ x,y\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{2}.\)</span> Consider the function <span class="mathjax-tex">\(g\left ( x,y\right ) =\ln \left ( e^{x}+e^{y}\right ) ,\ \left ( x,y\right ) \in \mathbb {R} ^{2}.\)</span> By Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar8">3.3</a> <i>g</i> is convex. If we apply Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar96">7.12</a>. to function <i>g</i> we obtain: </p><div id="Equqy" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \frac{\overset{n}{\underset{i=1}{\sum }}\ln \left( e^{a_{i}}+e^{b_{i}}\right) }{{n\choose 1}}\geq \frac{\overset{n}{\underset{i=1}{ \sum }}\ln \left( \exp \left( \frac{a_{i}+a_{i}}{2}\right) +\exp \left( \frac{b_{i}+b_{j}}{2}\right) \right) }{{n\choose 2}}\geq \ldots\geq \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equqz" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \geq \frac{\ln \left[ \exp \left( \frac{a_{1}+a_{2}+\ldots+a_{n}}{n}\right) +\exp \left( \frac{b_{1}+b_{2}+\ldots+b_{n}}{n}\right) \right] }{{n\choose n}} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> for <span class="mathjax-tex">\(a_{i},b_{i}\in \mathbb {R} ,\ i=1,2,\ldots ,n.\)</span> If we put <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x_{i}=\exp \left ( a_{i}\right ) ,\ y_{i}=\exp \left ( b_{i}\right ) ,\ i=1,2,\ldots ,n\)</span> in the last sequence of inequalities we obtain the second sequence of inequalities from the statement. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar100">Corollary 7.16</h3>
              <p><i>Let x</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub>, <i>y</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> ∈ (0, <i>∞</i>), <i>i </i>= 1, 2, …, <i>n. Then the following assertions hold:</i> </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">10.</span>

<p><i>If</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \left [ 0,1\right ] \)</span><i>, then</i> </p><div id="Equra" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left[ \underset{i=1}{\overset{n}{\prod }}\left( x_{i}^{p}+y_{i}^{p}\right) \right] ^{1/{n\choose 1}}\leq \frac{1}{2^{p}}\left\{ \underset{i&lt;j}{\prod } \left[ \left( x_{i}+x_{j}\right) ^{p}+\left( y_{i}+y_{j}\right) ^{p}\right] \right\} ^{{}^{1/{n\choose 2}}}\leq \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>


<div id="Equrb" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \leq \frac{1}{3^{p}}\left\{ \underset{i&lt;j&lt;k}{\prod }\left[ \left( x_{i}+x_{j}+x_{k}\right) ^{p}+\left( y_{i}+y_{j}+y_{k}\right) ^{p}\right] \right\} ^{{}^{1/{n\choose 3}}}\leq \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>


<div id="Equrc" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \leq \ldots.\leq \frac{1}{n^{p}}\left[ \left( \overset{n}{\underset{i=1}{\sum } }x_{i}\right) ^{p}+\left( \overset{n}{\underset{i=1}{\sum }}y_{i}\right) ^{p}\right] ^{1/{n\choose n}}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>


</li>
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">20.</span>

<p><i>If</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \left ( -\infty ,0\right ) \)</span><i>, then the above sequence of inequalities holds with the reversed sign of inequalities.</i>
</p>

</li>
</ol>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>For every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \mathbb {R} \)</span> consider the function </p><div id="Equrd" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{p}\left( x,y\right) =\ln \left( x^{p}+y^{p}\right) ,\ \ \ \ \left( x,y\right) \in \left( 0,\infty \right) ^{2}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that <i>f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is concave if <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \left [ 0,1\right ] \)</span> and <i>f</i>
<sub><i>p</i></sub> is convex if <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \left ( -\infty ,0\right ) \)</span>. Inequalities from the statement follow at once from Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar96">7.12</a>. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar101">Lemma 7.17</h3>
              <p><i>Let</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(a_{i},\alpha _{i}\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) ,\)</span> <i>i </i>= 1, 2, …, <i>n</i>, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x_{0}\in \mathbb {R} \)</span><i>, and consider the function</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f: \mathbb {R} \rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span><i>,</i> </p><div id="Equre" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f\left( x\right) =\left( \frac{\overset{n}{\underset{i=1}{\sum }}\alpha _{i}a_{i}^{x}}{\overset{n}{\underset{i=1}{\sum }}\alpha _{i}a_{i}^{x_{0}}} \right) ^{1/\left( x-x_{0}\right) }\ \ \ \ \ \mathit{\text{if }}x\in \mathbb{R} -\left\{ x_{0}\right\} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>with</i>
                </p><div id="Equrf" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f\left( x_{0}\right) =\frac{\overset{n}{\underset{i=1}{\sum }}\alpha _{i}a_{i}^{x_{0}}\ln a_{i}}{\overset{n}{\underset{i=1}{\sum }}\alpha _{i}a_{i}^{x_{0}}}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Then f is increasing on</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\( \mathbb {R} .\)</span>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(g\left ( x\right ) =\ln \left ( \overset {n}{\underset {i=1}{\sum }} \alpha _{i}a_{i}^{x}\right ) ,\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x\in \mathbb {R} .\)</span> Note that </p><div id="Equrg" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g^{\prime }\left( x\right) =\frac{\overset{n}{\underset{i=1}{\sum }}\alpha _{i}a_{i}^{x}\ln a_{i}}{\overset{n}{\underset{i=1}{\sum }}\alpha _{i}a_{i}^{x}},\ \ \ x\in \mathbb{R} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> and </p><div id="Equrh" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g^{\prime \prime }\left( x\right) =\frac{\left( \overset{n}{\underset{i=1}{ \sum }}\alpha _{i}a_{i}^{x}\ln ^{2}a_{i}\right) \left( \overset{n}{\underset {i=1}{\sum }}\alpha _{i}a_{i}^{x}\right) -\left( \overset{n}{\underset{i=1}{ \sum }}\alpha _{i}a_{i}^{x}\ln a_{i}\right) ^{2}}{\left( \overset{n}{ \underset{i=1}{\sum }}\alpha _{i}a_{i}^{x}\right) ^{2}},\ \ \ x\in \mathbb{R}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality it follows that <i>g</i>
<sup><i>′′</i></sup>≥ 0. Let </p><div id="Equri" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} h\left( x\right) =\frac{g\left( x\right) -\left( x_{0}\right) }{x-x_{0}},\ \ \ \ x\in \mathbb{R} -\left\{ x_{0}\right\} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> and </p><div id="Equrj" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} h\left( x_{0}\right) =g^{\prime }\left( x_{0}\right). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Since <i>g</i> is convex it follows that <i>h</i> is increasing, hence <i>f</i> = <i>e</i>
<sup><i>h</i></sup> is increasing. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar102">Theorem 7.18</h3>
              <p><i>Let a</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> ∈ [1, <i>∞</i>), <i>i </i>= 1, 2, …, <i>n</i>, <i>a </i>=<i> a</i>
<sub>1</sub> + 2<i>a</i>
<sub>2</sub> + 3<i>a</i>
<sub>3</sub> + … +<i> na</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub><i>. Then for every</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf { x\in }\left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}\)</span> <i>the following inequalities hold:</i> </p><div id="Equrk" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left[ \sigma _{n}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) \right] ^{a/n}\leq \left( \frac{ \sigma _{1}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) }{{n\choose 1}}\right) ^{a_{1}}\left( \frac{ \sigma _{2}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) }{{n\choose 2}}\right) ^{a_{2}}\ldots\left( \frac{\sigma _{n}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) }{{n\choose n}}\right) ^{a_{n}}\leq \frac{\sigma _{1}\left( {\mathbf{x}}^{a}\right) }{n}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>From the first sequence of inequalities in Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar96">7.12</a>. we obtain </p><div id="Equrl" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \frac{\sigma _{1}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) }{{n\choose 1}}\geq \frac{\sigma _{k}\left( {\mathbf{x}}^{1/k}\right) }{{n\choose k}}\geq \frac{\sigma _{n}\left( {\mathbf{x}}^{1/n}\right) }{{n\choose n}}, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> hence by a substitution we get </p><div id="Equrm" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \frac{\sigma _{1}\left( {\mathbf{x}}^{k}\right) }{{n\choose 1}}\geq \frac{\sigma _{k}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) }{{n\choose k}}\geq \frac{\sigma _{n}\left( {\mathbf{x}}^{k/n}\right) }{{n\choose n}}=\left[ \sigma _{n}\left( \mathbf{x} \right) \right] ^{k/n}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>By the preceding lemma we have </p><div id="Equrn" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left[ \frac{\sigma _{1}\left( {\mathbf{x}}^{ka_{k}}\right) }{n}\right] ^{1/ka_{k}}\leq \left[ \frac{\sigma _{1}\left( {\mathbf{x}}^{a}\right) }{n} \right] ^{1/a}, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> hence </p><div id="Equro" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \frac{\sigma _{1}\left( {\mathbf{x}}^{ka_{k}}\right) }{n}\leq \left[ \frac{ \sigma _{1}\left( {\mathbf{x}}^{a}\right) }{n}\right] ^{ka_{k}/a} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> From the preceding inequalities we obtain </p><div id="Equrp" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left[ \frac{\sigma _{1}\left( {\mathbf{x}}^{a}\right) }{n}\right] ^{ka_{k}/a}\geq \frac{\sigma _{1}\left( {\mathbf{x}}^{ka_{k}}\right) }{n}\geq \left[ \frac{\sigma _{1}\left( {\mathbf{x}}^{k}\right) }{n}\right] ^{a_{k}}\geq \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equrq" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \geq \left[ \frac{\sigma _{k}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) }{{n\choose k}}\right] ^{a_{k}}\geq \left[ \sigma _{n}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) \right] ^{ka_{k}/n}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> If we multiply the preceding inequalities for <i>k</i> = 1, 2, …, <i>n</i>, then we get the inequality in the statement. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar103">Theorem 7.19</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 3, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(m\geq 2,\ J=\left \{ 1,2,\ldots ,n\right \} .\)</span> <i>For every k </i>∈<i> J and every</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {x\in }\left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}\)</span> <i>let</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\left ( k,\mathbf {x}\right ) =\frac {\sigma _{k}\left ( \mathbf {x}\right ) }{{n\choose k}}.\)</span> <i>If</i> <b>a</b> <b><i>=</i></b> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\ \left ( a_{1},a_{2},\ldots ,a_{m}\right ) \)</span><i>,</i>
<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\ \mathbf {b}=\left ( b_{1},b_{2},\ldots ,b_{m}\right ) \mathbf {\in }J^{m}\)</span><i>,</i> <b>a</b> <b><i>≥</i></b>
<sub><i>HLP</i></sub><b>b</b><i>, then for every</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {x\in }\left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}\)</span> <i>the following inequality holds</i> </p><div id="Equrr" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} p\left( a_{1},\mathbf{x}\right) p\left( a_{2},\mathbf{x}\right) \ldots p\left( a_{m},\mathbf{x}\right) \leq p\left( b_{1},\mathbf{x}\right) p\left( b_{2}, \mathbf{x}\right) \ldots p\left( b_{m},\mathbf{x}\right). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>For <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {x\in }\left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}\)</span> let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f_{\mathbf {x} }\left ( k\right ) =\ln \left [ p\left ( k,\mathbf {x}\right ) \right ] ,\)</span> <i>k</i> ∈ <i>J</i>. By Newton inequalities we have that <i>f</i>
<sub><b>x</b></sub> is concave, that is <span class="mathjax-tex">\( 2f_{\mathbf {x}}\left ( k\right ) \geq f_{\mathbf {x}}\left ( k-1\right ) +f_{ \mathbf {x}}\left ( k+1\right ) ,\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(k\in \left \{ 2,3,\ldots ,n-1\right \} .\)</span> Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\( \tilde {f}_{\mathbf {x}}\)</span> :<span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left [ 1,n\right ] \rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span> be the piecewise affine continuation of <i>f</i>
<sub><b>x</b></sub>, that is </p><div id="Equrs" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \tilde{f}_{\mathbf{x}}\left( t\right) {=}\left( t{-}k\right) f_{\mathbf{x} }\left( k{+}1\right) {+}\left( k{+}1{-}t\right) f_{\mathbf{x}}\left( k\right) ,\ \ \ t\in \left[ k,k+1\right] ,k\in \left\{ 1,2,\ldots,n-1\right\} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\tilde {f}\)</span> is concave. If </p><div id="Equrt" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} F_{\mathbf{x}}\left( c_{1},c_{2},\ldots,c_{m}\right) =\tilde{f}_{\mathbf{x} }\left( c_{1}\right) +\tilde{f}_{\mathbf{x}}\left( c_{2}\right) +\ldots+\tilde{f }_{\mathbf{x}}\left( c_{m}\right) ,\left( c_{1},c_{2},\ldots,c_{m}\right) \in \left[ 1,n\right] ^{m}, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> then <i>F</i>
<sub><b>x</b></sub> is symmetric and concave, hence <i>F</i>
<sub><b>x</b></sub> is Schur concave. Therefore, the inequality in the statement holds. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar104">Theorem 7.20</h3>
              <p>
                <i>Let</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(m,n\geq 2,k\in \left \{ 1,2,\ldots ,n\right \} .\)</span>
                <i>For every</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {x}{=}\left ( x_{1},\dots ,x_{n}\right ) \mathbf {\in }\)</span>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left (0,\infty \right ) ^{n}\)</span>
                <i>, let</i>
                </p><div id="Equru" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} q_{k,\mathbf{x}}\left( t\right) =\sigma _{k}\left( {\mathbf{x}}^{t}\right) = \underset{\left\vert K\right\vert =k}{\sum }\underset{i\in K}{\prod } x_{i}^{t},\ \ t\in \mathbb{R} .\end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>If</i>
                <b>a</b>
                <b><i>=</i></b>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\ \left ( a_{1},a_{2},\ldots ,a_{m}\right ) \)</span>
                <i>,</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {,b} =\left ( b_{1},b_{2},\ldots ,b_{m}\right ) \mathbf {\in \mathbb {R} }^{m}\)</span>
                <i>and</i>
                <b>a</b>
                <b><i>≤</i></b>
                <sub>
                  <i>HLP</i>
                </sub>
                <b>b</b>
                <i>, then for every</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf { x\in }\left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}\)</span>
                <i>the following inequality holds:</i>
                </p><div id="Equrv" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} q_{k,\mathbf{x}}\left( a_{1}\right) q_{k,\mathbf{x}}\left( a_{2}\right) \ldots q_{l,\mathbf{x}}\left( a_{m}\right) \leq q_{k,\mathbf{x}}\left( b_{1}\right) q_{k,\mathbf{x}}\left( b_{2}\right) \ldots q_{k,\mathbf{x}}\left( b_{m}\right). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>For every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(K\subset \left \{ 1,2,\ldots ,n\right \} \)</span> and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {x} =\left ( x_{1},\dots ,x_{n}\right ) \mathbf {\in }\left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}\)</span> let </p><div id="Equrw" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u_{K}\left( x_{1},\dots ,x_{n}\right) =\underset{i\in K}{\sum }\ln \left( x_{i}\right). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that </p><div id="Equrx" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v_{k,\mathbf{x}}\left( t\right) =\ln \left[ q_{k,\mathbf{x}}\left( t\right) \right] =\ln \left[ \underset{\left\vert K\right\vert =k}{\sum }\exp \left( tu_{K}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) \right) \right] ,\ \ \ t\in \mathbb{R} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> is convex. For every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(k\in \left \{ 1,2,\ldots ,n\right \} \)</span> and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {x\in } \left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}\)</span>, let </p><div id="Equry" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{k,\mathbf{x}}\left( c_{1},c_{2},\ldots,c_{m}\right) =\ln \left[ q_{k,\mathbf{ x}}\left( c_{1}\right) \right] +\ln \left[ q_{k,\mathbf{x}}\left( c_{2}\right) \right] +\ldots+\ln \left[ q_{k,\mathbf{x}}\left( c_{m}\right) \right] , \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> where <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( c_{1},c_{2},\ldots ,c_{m}\right ) \in \mathbb {R}^{m}\)</span>. Note that for every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(k\in \left \{ 1,2,\ldots ,n\right \} \)</span> and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\mathbf {x\in } \left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}\)</span> the function <i>f</i>
<sub><i>k</i>,<b>x</b></sub> is convex and symmetric, hence <i>f</i>
<sub><i>k</i>,<b>x</b></sub> is Schur convex. Thus <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f_{k,\mathbf {x} }\left ( \mathbf {a}\right ) \leq f_{k,\mathbf {x}}\left ( \mathbf {b}\right ) .\)</span> This is equivalent with the inequality from the statement. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar105">Corollary 7.21</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2. <i>Then for every</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( x_{1},\dots ,x_{n}\right ) \mathbf {\in }\left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}\)</span> <i>the following inequalities hold:</i> </p><div id="Equrz" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left( \underset{i=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }}x_{i}\right) \left( \underset{i=1}{ \overset{n}{\sum }}x_{i}^{2}\right) \left( \underset{i=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }}x_{i}^{9}\right) \geq \left( \underset{i=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }} x_{i}^{3}\right) \left( \underset{i=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }}x_{i}^{4}\right) \left( \underset{i=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }}x_{i}^{5}\right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              
                <div id="Equsa" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left( \underset{i&lt;j}{\overset{n}{\sum }}x_{i}x_{j}\right) \left( \underset{ i&lt;j}{\overset{n}{\sum }}x_{i}^{2}x_{j}^{2}\right) \left( \underset{i&lt;j}{ \overset{n}{\sum }}x_{i}^{9}x_{j}^{9}\right) \geq \left( \underset{i&lt;j}{ \overset{n}{\sum }}x_{i}^{3}x_{j}^{3}\right) \left( \underset{i&lt;j}{\overset{ n}{\sum }}x_{i}^{4}x_{j}^{4}\right) \left( \underset{i&lt;j}{\overset{n}{\sum }}x_{i}^{5}x_{j}^{5}\right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Note that <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( 9,2,1\right ) \geq _{\text{HLP}}\left ( 5,4,3\right ) \)</span> and then apply the result in preceding the theorem. □</p>
            </div></div></section><section data-title="Construction of Convex Functions Based on Taylor Remainder"><div class="c-article-section" id="Sec8-section"><h2 id="Sec8" class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item"><span class="c-article-section__title-number">8 </span>Construction of Convex Functions Based on Taylor Remainder</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Sec8-content"><p>In this section we shall prove the convexity or the concavity of some functions with the help of Taylor remainder of power function and exponential function.</p><p>If <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f:I\rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span> is a <i>k</i> times differentiable function, then it can be written as </p><div id="Equsb" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f\left( x\right) =T_{k}f\left( x\right) +R_{k}f\left( x\right) ,\ \ \ x\in I. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Here <span class="mathjax-tex">\(T_{k}f\left ( x\right ) \)</span> is the Taylor polynomial of <i>f</i> and <span class="mathjax-tex">\( R_{k}f\left ( x\right ) \)</span> is the corresponding Taylor remainder. If <i>f</i> is the power function or the exponential function we shall prove that the function <span class="mathjax-tex">\( g\left ( x\right ) =f^{-1}\left ( R_{k}f\left ( x\right ) \right ) \)</span> is concave. Two conjectures related with concavity of functions built with the help of Taylor remaiders are formulated.</p><p>Let <i>I</i> be an interval of the real axis <i>x</i>
<sub>0</sub>, <i>x</i>
<sub>1</sub>, …, <i>x</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> ∈ <i>I</i> and let <span class="mathjax-tex">\( f:I\rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span> be a function. Denote by <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f\left [ x_{0},x_{1},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ] \)</span> the divided difference of <i>f</i> at the distinct points <i>x</i>
<sub>0</sub>, <i>x</i>
<sub>1</sub>, …, <i>x</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub>. In case <i>f</i> is <i>n</i>-times differentiable then <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f\left [ x_{0},x_{1},\ldots ,x_{n} \right ] \)</span> is defined for all <i>x</i>
<sub>0</sub>, <i>x</i>
<sub>1</sub>, …, <i>x</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> ∈ <i>I</i> not necessarily distinct.</p><p>The Hermite–Genocchi formula is presented in the following theorem.</p>
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar106">Theorem 8.1</h3>
              <p><i>Let I be an interval of the real axis x</i>
<sub>0</sub>, <i>x</i>
<sub>1</sub>, …, <i>x</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> ∈<i> I and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f:I\rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span> <i>be a function of class C</i>
<sup><i>n</i></sup><i>. Denote by Δ</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> <i>the n -dimensional standard simplex, that is</i> </p><div id="Equsc" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \varDelta _{n}=\left\{ \left( t_{0},t_{1},\ldots,t_{n}\right) \in \mathbb{R} ^{n+1}:t_{i}\geq 0\mathit{\text{ for }}i=0,1,2,\ldots,n\mathit{\text{ and }}\underset{i=0}{ \overset{n}{\sum }}t_{i}=1\right\}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>Then</i>
                </p><div id="Equsd" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f\left[ x_{0},x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}\right] =\int_{\varDelta _{n}}f^{\left( n\right) }\left( t_{0}x_{0}+t_{1}x_{1}+\ldots+t_{n}x_{n}\right) dt_{0}dt_{1}\ldots dt_{n}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar107">Theorem 8.2</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 1 <i>be a natural number, I be an open interval of the real axis, a </i>∈<i> I and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f:I\rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span> <i>be a function of class C</i>
<sup><i>n</i></sup><i>. Consider the function</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(g:I\rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span><i>:</i> </p><div id="Equ22" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g\left( x\right) =\frac{f\left( x\right) -f\left( a\right) -f\ ^{\prime }\left( a\right) \frac{x-a}{1!}-\ldots-f^{\left( n-1\right) }\left( a\right) \frac{\left( x-a\right) ^{n-1}}{\left( n-1\right) !}}{\left( x-a\right) ^{n}} ,\ \ \ x\in I-\left\{ a\right\} \end{aligned} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (8.22)
                </div></div>

              <p>
                <i>and</i>
                </p><div id="Equse" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g\left( a\right) =\frac{f^{\left( n\right) }\left( a\right) }{n!}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>If f</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(^{\left ( n\right ) }\)</span>
                <i>is a convex function, then g is a convex function.</i>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>If <i>x</i>
<sub>0</sub>, <i>x</i>
<sub>1</sub>, …, <i>x</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> ∈ <i>I</i> let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f\left [ x_{0},x_{1},\ldots ,x_{n} \right ] \)</span> be the divided difference of <i>f</i> at the points <i>x</i>
<sub>0</sub>, <i>x</i>
<sub>1</sub>, …, <i>x</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub>. Let </p><div id="Equ23" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} h\left( x_{0},x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}\right) =f\left[ x_{0},x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}\right] ,\ \ \ \left( x_{0},x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}\right) \in I^{n+1} \end{aligned} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (8.23)
                </div></div><p> Note that </p><div id="Equsf" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g\left( x\right) =h\left( x,a,a,\ldots,a\right) ,\ \ \ x\in I-\left\{ a\right\} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> The number of <i>a</i> in the argument of <i>h</i> is equal to <i>n</i>. By the Hermite–Genocchi formula we obtain: </p><div id="Equ24" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g\left( x\right) =\int_{\varDelta _{n}}f^{\left( n\right) }\left( t_{0}x+\left( t_{1}+t_{2}+\ldots+t_{n}\right) a\right) dt_{0}dt_{1}\ldots dt_{n} \end{aligned} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (8.24)
                </div></div><p> Since <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f^{\left ( n\right ) }\)</span> is convex it follows that <i>g</i> is convex. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar108">Corollary 8.3</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 1 <i>be a natural number,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(c\in \left ( 0,1\right ) \)</span> <i>,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(I=\left ( c,\infty \right ) \)</span> <i>and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f:I\rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span> <i>be a function of class C</i>
<sup><i>n</i></sup>. <i>If f</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(^{\left ( n\right ) }\)</span><i>is convex, then the function</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(u:I\rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span> </p><div id="Equsg" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u\left( x\right) {=}\frac{f\left( x{+}1\right) {-}f\left( 1\right) {-}f\ ^{\prime }\left( 1\right) \frac{x}{1!}{-}f\ ^{\prime \prime }\left( 1\right) \frac{x^{2} }{2!}--\ldots-f^{\left( n-1\right) }\left( 1\right) \frac{x^{n-1}}{\left( n-1\right) !}}{x^{n}},\ x\in I \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>is convex.</i>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Consider the function <i>g</i> defined by (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ22">8.22</a>) and substitute <i>x</i> → <i>x</i> + 1 and <i>a</i> = 1. Note that </p><div id="Eqush" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u\left( x\right) =g\left( x+1\right) \ \ \ x\in I \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Since <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f^{\left ( n\right ) }\)</span> is convex, by Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar107">8.2</a>., it follows that the function <i>g</i> is convex, hence <i>u</i> is convex. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar109">Corollary 8.4</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 1 <i>be a natural number and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \mathbb {R} .\)</span> <i>For every positive integer k define the generalized binomial coefficient as follows:</i> </p><div id="Equsi" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} {n\choose k}=\frac{p\left( p-1\right) \left( p-2\right) \ldots\left( p-k+1\right) }{k!}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p><i>If k </i>= 0 <i>we shall put</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\({n\choose k}=1\)</span><i>. Consider the functions</i> </p><div id="Equ25" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g\left( x\right) =\frac{\left( 1+x\right) ^{p}-{p\choose 0}-{p\choose 1}x-{p\choose 2}x^{2}-\ldots-{p\choose n-1 }x^{n-1}}{x^{n}},\ \ x\in \left( 0,\infty \right) \end{aligned} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (8.25)
                </div></div>

              
                <div id="Equsj" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} h\left( x\right) =&amp;\frac{\left( 1+x\right) ^{-p}-{p-1\choose 0}+{p\choose 1}x-{p+1\choose 2}x^{2}+\ldots+\left( -1\right) ^{n-1}{p+n-2\choose n-1}x^{n-1}}{x^{n}}, \\ &amp;x\in \left( 0,\infty \right). \end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">10.</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If p </i>≥<i> n </i>+ 1<i>, then g is convex.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">20.</span>
                    
                      <p>
                        <i>If</i>
                        <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) \)</span>
                        <i>, then</i>
                        <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( -1\right ) ^{n}h\)</span>
                        <i>is convex.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Suppose that <i>p</i> ≥ <i>n</i> + 1 and let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f_{1}\left ( x\right ) =x^{p},\ x\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) .\)</span>Note that <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f_{1}^{\left ( n+2\right ) }\left ( x\right ) =p\left ( p-1\right ) \ldots \left ( p-n-1\right ) x^{p-n-2}\geq 0\)</span>, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) .\)</span> By Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar108">8.3</a>., <i>g</i> is a convex function.</p>
              <p>Suppose that <i>p</i> ∈ [0, <i>∞</i>) and let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f_{2}\left ( x\right ) =x^{-p},\ x\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) \)</span>. Note that <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( -1\right ) ^{n}f_{2}^{\left ( n+2\right ) }\left ( x\right ) =p\left ( p+1\right ) \ldots \left ( p+n+1\right ) x^{-p-n-2}\geq 0\)</span>, <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) \)</span>. By Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar108">8.3</a>., <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( -1\right ) ^{n}h\)</span> is a convex function. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar110">Corollary 8.5</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 1 <i>be a natural number. Consider the functions</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(g,h: \mathbb {R} \rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span> </p><div id="Equsk" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g\left( x\right) =\frac{e^{x}-1-\frac{x}{1!}-\frac{x^{2}}{2!}-\ldots-\frac{ x^{n-1}}{\left( n-1\right) !}}{x^{n}},\ \ \ \ x\in \mathbb{R} -\left\{ 0\right\}\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>with</i>
                </p><div id="Equsl" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g\left( 0\right) =\frac{1}{n!} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>and</i>
                </p><div id="Equsm" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} h\left( x\right) =\frac{1-e^{-x}-\frac{x}{1!}+\frac{x^{2}}{2!}-\ldots+\left( -1\right) ^{n-1}\frac{x^{n-1}}{\left( n-1\right) !}}{x^{n}},\ \ \ \ x\in \mathbb{R} -\left\{ 0\right\}\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>with</i>
                </p><div id="Equsn" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} h\left( 0\right) =\frac{1}{n!}.\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Then g and</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( -1\right ) ^{n+1}h\)</span>
                <i>are convex.</i>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>If we apply Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar107">8.2</a> for <i>a</i> = 0 and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f\left ( x\right ) =e^{x}\)</span>, <span class="mathjax-tex">\( x\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) \)</span>, then we obtain that <i>g</i> is convex. In order to prove that <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( -1\right ) ^{n+1}h\)</span> is convex consider the function <span class="mathjax-tex">\( f\left ( x\right ) =\left ( -1\right ) ^{n+1}\left ( 1-e^{-x}\right ) ,\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x\in \mathbb {R}, \)</span> and then apply Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar107">8.2</a>. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar111">Theorem 8.6</h3>
              <p><i>Let</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( X,\varSigma ,\mu \right ) \)</span> <i>be a space with measure, E be a real linear space, D be a convex subset of E, and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\( u:D\times X\rightarrow \mathbb {R} .\)</span> <i>Suppose that</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(u\left ( \cdot ,y\right ) \)</span> <i>is convex for every y </i>∈<i> X and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\( \exp \left ( u\left ( x,\cdot \right ) \right ) \)</span> <i>is integrable for every x </i>∈<i> D. Then the function</i> </p><div id="Equso" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f\left( x\right) =\ln \left[ \int_{X}\exp \left( u\left( x,y\right) \right) d\mu \left( y\right) \right] ,\ \ \ x\in D\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>is convex.</i>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <i>a</i>, <i>b</i> ≥ 0, <i>a</i> + <i>b</i> = 1. Then for every <i>x</i>
<sub>1</sub>, <i>x</i>
<sub>2</sub> ∈ <i>D</i>, we have: </p><div id="Equsp" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f\left( ax_{1}+bx_{2}\right) =\ln \left[ \int_{X}\exp \left( u\left( ax_{1}+bx_{2},y\right) \right) d\mu \left( y\right) \right] \leq \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equsq" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \leq \ln \left[ \int_{X}\exp \left( au\left( x_{1},y\right) +bu\left( x_{2},y\right) \right) d\mu \left( y\right) \right] = \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equsr" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =\ln \left[ \int_{X}\exp \left( au\left( x_{1},y\right) \right) \exp \left( bu\left( x_{2},y\right) \right) d\mu \left( y\right) \right] \leq \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equss" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \leq \ln \left[ \int_{X}\left[ \exp \left( au\left( x_{1},y\right) \right) \right] ^{1/a}d\mu \left( y\right) \right] ^{a}\ln \left[ \int_{X}\left[ \exp \left( bu\left( x_{2},y\right) \right) \right] ^{1/b}d\mu \left( y\right) \right] ^{b}= \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equst" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =a\ln \left[ \int_{X}\exp \left( u\left( x_{1},y\right) \right) d\mu \left( x\right) \right] +b\ln \left[ \int_{X}\exp \left( u\left( x_{2},y\right) \right) d\mu \left( x\right) \right] = \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equsu" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =af\left( x_{1}\right) +bf\left( x_{2}\right). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Consequently, <i>f</i> is convex. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar112">Corollary 8.7</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 1 <i>be a natural number, I be an interval of the real axis,</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f:I\rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span> <i>be an n times differentiable function such that</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f^{\left ( n\right ) }&gt;0\)</span> <i>on I</i>. <i>If</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(u=\ln \left ( f^{\left ( n\right ) }\right ) \)</span> <i>is convex, then the function</i> </p><div id="Equsv" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g\left( x_{0},x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}\right) =\ln \left( f\left[ x_{0},x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}\right] \right) ,\ \ \ \left( x_{0},x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}\right) \in I^{n+1} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>is convex.</i>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>By the Genocchi–Hadamard theorem we have: </p><div id="Equsw" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g\left( x_{0},x_{1},\ldots,x_{n}\right) =\ln \left( \int_{\varDelta _{n}}f^{\left( n\right) }\left( t_{0}x_{0}+t_{1}x_{1}+\ldots+t_{n}x_{n}\right) dt_{0}dt_{1}\ldots dt_{n}\right) =\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equsx" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =\ln \left( \int_{\varDelta _{n}}\exp \left( u\left( t_{0}x_{0}+t_{1}x_{1}+\ldots+t_{n}x_{n}\right) \right) dt_{0}dt_{1}\ldots dt_{n}\right)\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div><p> By the preceding theorem it follows that <i>g</i> is convex on <i>I</i>
<sup><i>n</i>+1</sup>. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar113">Corollary 8.8</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 1 <i>be a natural number. Then the function</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(g:\left ( 0,\infty \right ) \rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span> <i>defined by</i> </p><div id="Equsy" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g\left( x\right) =\ln \left( \frac{e^{x}-1-\frac{x}{1!}-\frac{x^{2}}{2!}-\ldots- \frac{x^{n-1}}{\left( n-1\right) !}}{x^{n}}\right) ,\ \ \ \ x\in \left( 0,\infty \right)\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>is convex.</i>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f\left ( x\right ) =e^{x},x\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) \)</span>. Note that <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\ln \left ( f^{\left ( n\right ) }\left ( x\right ) \right ) =x,\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) \)</span> is convex. Since </p><div id="Equsz" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g\left( x\right) =\ln \left( f\left[ x,0,0,\ldots,0\right] \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> by the preceding Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar112">8.7</a> it follows that <i>g</i> is convex. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar114">Corollary 8.9</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 1 <i>be a natural number and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in \mathbb {R} \)</span><i>. Consider the function:</i> </p><div id="Equta" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g\left( x\right) =\ln \left( \frac{\left( 1+x\right) ^{p}-{p\choose 0}-{p\choose 1}x-{p\choose 2}x^{2}-\ldots-{p\choose n-1}x^{n-1}}{x^{n}} \right) ,\ \ x\in \left( 0,\infty \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">10.</span>
                    
                      <p><i>If</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\left ( p-1\right ) \left ( p-2\right ) \ldots \left ( p-n+1\right ) &gt;0\)</span> <i>and p </i>&lt;<i> n, then the function g is convex.</i>
</p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">20.</span>
                    
                      <p>
                        <i>If</i>
                        <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\left ( p-1\right ) \left ( p-2\right ) \ldots \left ( p-n-1\right ) \leq 0\)</span>
                        <i>, then g is concave.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>In order to prove 1<sup>0</sup> suppose that <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\left ( p-1\right ) \left ( p-2\right ) \ldots \left ( p-n+1\right ) &gt;0\)</span> and <i>p</i> &lt; <i>n</i> Consider the function <span class="mathjax-tex">\( f\left ( x\right ) =x^{p},x\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) .\)</span> Note that </p><div id="Equtb" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f^{\left( n\right) }\left( x\right) =p\left( p-1\right) \left( p-2\right) \ldots\left( p-n+1\right) x^{p-n},x\in \left( 0,\infty \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f^{\left ( n\right ) }&gt;0\)</span> and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\ln \left ( f^{\left ( n\right ) }\right ) \)</span> is convex on <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( 0,\infty \right ) \)</span>. By Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar112">8.7</a>. it follows that <i>g</i> is convex.</p>
              <p>In order to prove 2<sup>0</sup> suppose that <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\left ( p-1\right ) \left ( p-2\right ) \ldots \left ( p-n-1\right ) \leq 0.\)</span> Note that </p><div id="Equtc" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f^{\left( n+2\right) }\left( x\right) =p\left( p-1\right) \left( p-2\right) \ldots\left( p-n-1\right) x^{p-n-2},x\in \left( 0,\infty \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> and <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f^{\left ( n\right ) }\)</span> is concave on <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( 0,\infty \right ) .\)</span> By Corollary <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar108">8.3</a> the function defined by (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ25">8.25</a>) is concave. Hence the function <i>g</i> is concave. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar115">Lemma 8.10</h3>
              <p><i>For every natural number k </i>≥ 0<i>, consider the functions:</i> </p><div id="Equ26" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v_{k}\left( x\right) =e^{x}-1-\frac{x}{1!}-\frac{x^{2}}{2!}-\ldots-\frac{x^{k}}{ k!},\ \ \ \ x\in \left( 0,\infty \right) \end{aligned} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (8.1)
                </div></div>

              
                <div id="Equ27" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{k}\left( x\right) =k!\left( k-x\right) v_{k}\left( x\right) -x^{k+1},x\in \left( 0,\infty \right) \end{aligned} $$
</span></div><div class="c-article-equation__number">
                    (8.2)
                </div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Then</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f_{k}\left ( x\right ) \leq 0\)</span>
                <i>for every</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) .\)</span>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Note that for every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) \)</span> we have successively </p><div id="Equtd" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{k}\left( x\right) =k!\left( k-x\right) \left( \frac{x^{k+1}}{\left( k+1\right) !}+\frac{x^{k+2}}{\left( k+2\right) !}+\ldots.\right) -x^{k+1}= \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equte" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =\left( k-x\right) \left[ \frac{x^{k+1}}{k+1}+\frac{x^{k+2}}{\left( k+1\right) \left( k+2\right) }+\ldots.\right] -x^{k+1}= \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equtf" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =\left( \frac{k}{k+1}-1\right) x^{k+1}+\left( \frac{k}{\left( k+1\right) \left( k+2\right) }-\frac{1}{k+1}\right) x^{k+2}+ \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equtg" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} +\left( \frac{k}{\left( k+1\right) \left( k+2\right) \left( k+3\right) }- \frac{1}{\left( k+1\right) \left( k+2\right) }\right) x^{k+3}+\ldots.= \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equth" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =-\frac{x^{k+1}}{k+1}-\frac{2x^{k+2}}{\left( k+1\right) \left( k+2\right) }- \frac{3x^{k+2}}{\left( k+1\right) \left( k+2\right) \left( k+3\right) } -\ldots\leq 0 \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar116">Theorem 8.11</h3>
              <p><i>For every k </i>≥ 0 <i>let v</i>
<sub><i>k</i></sub> <i>be the function defined in</i> (<a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="equation anchor" href="#Equ26">8.1</a>)<i>. Then the function</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(g_{k}\left ( x\right ) =\ln \left [ v_{k}\left ( x\right ) \right ] ,\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) \)</span> <i>is concave.</i>
</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>A direct computation of the second derivative shows that <i>g</i>
<sub>1</sub> and <i>g</i>
<sub>2</sub> are concave. If <i>k</i> ≥ 2, then </p><div id="Equti" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v_{k}^{\prime }=v_{k-1},v_{k}^{\prime \prime }=v_{k-2},g_{k}^{\prime }=\frac{ v_{k}^{\prime }}{v_{k}} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equtj" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{k}^{\prime \prime }=\frac{v_{k}v_{k}^{\prime \prime }-\left( v_{k}^{\prime }\right) ^{2}}{v_{k}^{2}}=\frac{v_{k}v_{k-2}-v_{k-1}^{2}}{ v_{k}^{2}} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Let </p><div id="Equtk" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} w_{k}\left( x\right) =v_{k}\left( x\right) v_{k}^{\prime \prime }\left( x\right) -\left[ v_{k}^{\prime }\left( x\right) \right] ^{2},\ \ \ \ x\in \left( 0,\infty \right). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that <i>g</i>
<sub><i>k</i></sub> is concave if and only if <i>w</i>
<sub><i>k</i></sub> ≤ 0. From the above lemma we obtain: </p><div id="Equtl" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} w_{k}\left( x\right) =v_{k}\left( x\right) v_{k-2}\left( x\right) -\left[ v_{k-1}\left( x\right) \right] ^{2}= \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equtm" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =v_{k}\left( x\right) \left[ v_{k}\left( x\right) +\frac{x^{k-1}}{\left( k-1\right) !}+\frac{x^{k}}{k!}\right] -\left( v_{k}\left( x\right) +\frac{ x^{k}}{k!}\right) ^{2}= \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equtn" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =v_{k}\left( x\right) \left( \frac{x^{k-1}}{\left( k-1\right) !}-\frac{x^{k} }{k!}\right) -\frac{x^{2k}}{\left( k!\right) ^{2}}= \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equto" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =\frac{x^{k-1}}{\left( k!\right) ^{2}}\left[ k!\left( k-x\right) v_{k}\left( x\right) -x^{k+1}\right] \leq 0\  \text{if }x\in \left( 0,\infty \right). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> This proves that <i>g</i>
<sub><i>k</i></sub> is concave. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar117">Lemma 8.12</h3>
              <p>
                <i>For</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(a,b\in \left [ 0,1\right ] \)</span>
                <i>consider the functions</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(u\left ( x\right ) =e^{x}-a-bx\)</span>
                <i>and</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f\left ( x\right ) =\ln \left [ u\left ( x\right ) \right ]\)</span>
                <i>, where</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) .\)</span>
                <i>If</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(b\leq 1- \sqrt {1-a}\)</span>
                <i>, then f is concave.</i>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Note that <i>f</i> is concave if and only if <span class="mathjax-tex">\(v=uu^{\prime \prime }-\left ( u^{\prime }\right ) ^{2}\leq 0\)</span> By <span class="mathjax-tex">\(b\leq 1-\sqrt {1-a}\)</span> it follows that − <i>a</i> + 2<i>b</i> ≤ <i>b</i>
<sup>2</sup>, hence </p><div id="Equtp" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><img src="//media.springernature.com/lw571/springer-static/image/chp%3A10.1007%2F978-3-030-27407-8_4/MediaObjects/450928_1_En_4_Equtp_HTML.png" class="u-display-block" alt=""></div></div><p> Since <i>e</i>
<sup>−<i>x</i></sup> ≥ 1 − <i>x</i> it follows that </p><div id="Equtq" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v\left( x\right) \leq be^{x}\left[ b-x-b\left( 1-x\right) \right] =b\left( b-1\right) xe^{x}\leq 0. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Consequently, <i>f</i> is concave. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar118">Theorem 8.13</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2 <i>be a natural number,</i> </p><div id="Equtr" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \sigma _{2}\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) =\underset{1\leq i&lt;j\leq n}{ \sum }x_{i}x_{j}\ ,\ \ \ \ \left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) \in \left( 0,\infty \right) ^{n}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>Then the function</i>
                </p><div id="Equts" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) {=}\ln \left[ \exp \left( \underset{i{=}1}{ \overset{n}{\sum }}x_{i}\right) {-}\left( \underset{i{=}1}{\overset{n}{\sum }} e^{x_{i}}\right) {+}n{-}1{-}\sigma _{2}\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) \right], \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>where</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}\)</span>
                <i>is separately concave.</i>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Since <i>f</i> is symmetric it suffices to prove the concavity of it in the first variable. Fix <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( x_{2},x_{3,}\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n-1}\)</span> and let </p><div id="Equtt" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} s=x_{2}+x_{3}+\ldots+x_{n}, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equtu" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} q_{1}=\underset{2\leq i&lt;j\leq n}{\sum }x_{i}x_{j}, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equtv" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} q_{2}=\overset{n}{\underset{i=2}{\sum }}\left( e^{x_{i}}-1\right), \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equtw" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} a=\frac{q_{1}+q_{2}}{e^{s}-1},\ \ b=\frac{s}{e^{s}-1}, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equtx" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \psi \left( t\right) =\ln \left( e^{t}-a-bt\right) ,\ \ t\in \left( 0,\infty \right), \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> and </p><div id="Equty" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \alpha =\overset{n}{\underset{i=2}{\sum }}\phi \left( x_{i}\right). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that we have the relations </p><div id="Equtz" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} q_{1}+q_{2}=q_{1}+\overset{n}{\underset{i=2}{\sum }}\left( x_{i}+\frac{ x_{i}^{2}}{2}+\phi \left( x_{i}\right) \right) =s+\frac{s^{2}}{2}+\alpha, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> hence <span class="mathjax-tex">\(a,b\in \left [ 0,1\right ] \)</span>. One can easily see that </p><div id="Equua" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) =\ln \left[ e^{x_{1}}\left( e^{s}-1\right) -q_{2}-sx_{1}-q_{1}\right] = \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equub" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \ln \left( e^{s}-1\right) +\ln \left[ e^{x_{1}}-\frac{q_{1}+q_{2}}{e^{s}-1}- \frac{s}{e^{s}-1}x_{1}\right] &amp;=\ln \left( e^{s}-1\right) +\ln \left[ e^{x_{1}}-a-bx_{1}\right] \\ &amp;=\psi \left( x_{1}\right). \end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div><p> We shall prove that − <i>a</i> + 2<i>b</i> ≤ <i>b</i>
<sup>2</sup>, hence <span class="mathjax-tex">\(b\leq 1-\sqrt {1-a}\)</span>. By Lemma <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar117">8.12</a>. we obtain that <i>ψ</i> is concave, hence <i>f</i> will be separately concave. Note that </p><div id="Equuc" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} -a+2b-b^{2}=-\frac{s+\frac{s^{2}}{2}+\alpha }{e^{s}-1}+\frac{2s}{e^{s}-1} -\left( \frac{s}{e^{s}-1}\right) ^{2}= \frac{\left( s-\frac{s^{2}}{2}-\alpha \right) \left( e^{s}-1\right) -s^{2}}{ \left( e^{s}-1\right) ^{2}} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equud" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =\frac{\left( s-\frac{s^{2}}{2}\right) \left( e^{s}-1\right) -s^{2}-\alpha \left( e^{s}-1\right) }{\left( e^{s}-1\right) ^{2}}= \frac{\frac{s}{2}\left[ \left( 2-s\right) e^{s}-2-s\right] -\alpha \left( e^{s}-1\right) }{\left( e^{s}-1\right) ^{2}}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(v\left ( t\right ) =\left ( 2-t\right ) e^{t}-t-2,\ t\in \lbrack 0,\infty ). \)</span> Note that <span class="mathjax-tex">\(v\left ( 0\right ) =0,\)</span> and <i>v</i> is decreasing. Hence <span class="mathjax-tex">\(v\left ( t\right ) \leq 0\)</span> if <i>t</i> ∈ [0, <i>∞</i>). Thus </p><div id="Equue" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} -a+2b-b^{2}=\frac{\frac{s}{2}v\left( s\right) -\alpha \left( e^{s}-1\right) }{\left( e^{s}-1\right) ^{2}}\leq 0 \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> □</p>
            <p>For every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(a\in \mathbb {R} \)</span> denote by <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left [ a\right ] \)</span> the greatest integer smaller or equal than <i>a</i>.</p>
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar119">Theorem 8.14</h3>
              <p>
                <i>For every</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p\in (1,\infty ),\ k\in \left \{ 0,1,2,\ldots , \left [ p\right ] +1\right \} \)</span>
                <i>, consider the function</i>
                </p><div id="Equuf" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{k,p}\left( x\right) =\left( \underset{i=0}{\overset{k}{\sum }} {p\choose i}x^{i}\right) ^{1/p},\ x\in (0,\infty ). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p>
                <i>Then the following assertions hold:</i>
                </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">10.</span>
                    
                      <p>
                        <i>If</i>
                        <span class="mathjax-tex">\(k\in \left \{ 0,1,2,\ldots ,\left [ p\right ] \right \} \)</span>
                        <i>, then f</i>
                        <sub><i>k</i>,<i>p</i></sub>
                        <i>is concave.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                  <li>
                    <span class="u-custom-list-number">20.</span>
                    
                      <p>
                        <i>If</i>
                        <span class="mathjax-tex">\(k=\left [ p\right ] +1\)</span>
                        <i>, then f</i>
                        <sub><i>k</i>,<i>p</i></sub>
                        <i>is convex.</i>
                      </p>
                    
                  </li>
                </ol>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let </p><div id="Equug" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u_{k,p}\left( x\right) =\underset{i=0}{\overset{k}{\sum }}{p\choose i}x^{i},\ x\in \lbrack 0,\infty ). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that </p><div id="Equuh" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{k,p}=\left( u_{k,p}\right) ^{1/p},\ \ \ f_{k,p}^{\ \prime }=\frac{1}{p} \left( u_{k,p}\right) ^{(1/p)-1}u_{k,p}^{\prime } \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> and </p><div id="Equui" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{k,p}^{\ \prime \prime }=\frac{1}{p^{2}}\left( u_{k,p}\right) ^{(1/p)-2} \left[ pu_{k,p}u_{k,p}^{\prime \prime }-\left( p-1\right) \left( u_{k,p}^{\prime }\right) ^{2}\right]. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> One can easily see that <i>f</i>
<sub><i>k</i>,<i>p</i></sub> is concave if and only if </p><div id="Equuj" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v_{k,p}=\left( p-1\right) \left( u_{k,p}^{\prime }\right) ^{2}-pu_{k,p}u_{k,p}^{\prime \prime }\geq 0. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that </p><div id="Equuk" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u_{k,p}^{\prime }=pu_{k-1,p-1},\ \ u_{k,p}^{\prime \prime }=p\left( p-1\right) u_{k-2,p-2}, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> hence </p><div id="Equul" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} v_{k,p}=p^{2}\left( p-1\right) \left[ \left( u_{k-1,p-1}\right) ^{2}-u_{k,p}u_{k-2,p-2}\right]. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Thus </p><div id="Equum" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left( u_{k-1,p-1}\left( x\right) \right) ^{2}-u_{k,p}\left( x\right) u_{k-2,p-2}\left( x\right) = \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equun" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =\left( {p-1\choose 0}+{p-1\choose 1}x+\ldots+{p-1\choose k-1}x^{k-1}\right) ^{2}- \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equuo" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} &amp;-\left( {p\choose 0}+{p\choose 1}x+\ldots+{p\choose k-1}x^{k}\right) \left( {p-2\choose 0}+{p-2\choose 1}x+\ldots \right. \\ &amp; \left. +{p-2\choose k-2}x^{k-2}\right) =\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equup" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =c_{0}+c_{1}x+\ldots+c_{2k-2}x^{2k-2}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> One notes that </p><div id="Equuq" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} c_{2k-r-1}=\frac{r}{p-1}{p-1\choose k}{p-1\choose k-r},\ \ \ \ r\in \left\{ 1,2,\ldots,k\right\} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equur" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} c_{r}=0,\ \  r\in \left\{ 0,1,2\ldots.,k-2\right\}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> If <span class="mathjax-tex">\(k\in \left \{ 0,1,2,\ldots ,\left [ p\right ] \right \} \)</span>, then we have <i>v</i>
<sub><i>k</i>,<i>p</i></sub> ≥ 0, hence <i>f</i>
<sub><i>k</i>,<i>p</i></sub> is concave. If <span class="mathjax-tex">\(k=\left [ p\right ] +1\)</span>, then <i>v</i>
<sub><i>k</i>,<i>p</i></sub> ≤ 0, hence <i>f</i>
<sub><i>k</i>,<i>p</i></sub> is convex. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar120">Conjecture A</h3>
              <p><i>For every natural number p </i>≥ 2 <i>and</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(k\in \left \{ 0,1,2,\ldots ,\left [ \frac {p}{2}\right ] -1\right \} \)</span> <i>consider the function</i> </p><div id="Equus" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{k,p}\left( x\right) =\left[ \left( 1+x\right) ^{p}-\overset{k}{\underset{ i=0}{\sum }}{p\choose i}\left( x^{i}+x^{p-i}\right) \right] ^{1/p},\ \ \ x\in (0,\infty ) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>Then g</i>
                <sub><i>k</i>,<i>p</i></sub>
                <i>is concave.</i>
              </p>
            <p>Note that </p><div id="Equut" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} g_{k,p}\left( x\right) =\left( \overset{p-k-1}{\underset{i=k+1}{\sum }} {p\choose i}x^{i}\right) ^{1/p},\ \ \ x\in (0,\infty ) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> The following lemma contains a classical result from matrix theory.</p>
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar121">Lemma 8.15</h3>
              <p><i>Let A be an n </i>×<i> n matrix with complex entries. Denote by A</i>
<sup>∗</sup> <i>the adjugate of A. If</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x,y\in \mathbb {R} ^{n}\)</span> <i>and A </i>=<i> I</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> +<i> xy</i>
<sup><i>T</i></sup><i>, then the following equalities hold:</i> </p><div id="Equuu" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \det \left( A\right) =1+x^{T}y \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              
                <div id="Equuv" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} A^{\ast }=\left( 1+x^{T}y\right) I_{n}-xy^{T} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              
                <div id="Equuw" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} A^{-1}=I_{n}-\frac{1}{1+x^{T}y}xy^{T}\ \ \ \mathit{\text{if }}1+x^{T}y\neq 0 \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar122">Lemma 8.16</h3>
              <p>
                <i>Let</i>
                <span class="mathjax-tex">\(x,y,u,v\in \mathbb {R} ^{n}\)</span>
                <i>. Then the following equality holds:</i>
                </p><div id="Equux" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \det \left( I_{n}+xy^{T}+uv^{T}\right) =\left( 1+x^{T}y\right) \left( 1+u^{T}v\right) -\left( x^{T}v\right) \left( y^{T}u\right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <i>A</i> = <i>I</i>
<sub><i>n</i></sub> + <i>xy</i>
<sup><i>T</i></sup>. Then </p><div id="Equuy" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \det \left( I_{n}+xy^{T}+uv^{T}\right) =\det \left( A+uv^{T}\right) =\det \left( A\right) +v^{T}A^{\ast }u=\end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equuz" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} &amp;=\det \left( A\right) +v^{T}\left[ \left( 1+x^{T}y\right) I_{n}-xy^{T}\right] u=\\ &amp;=\left( 1+x^{T}y\right) \left( 1+u^{T}v\right) -\left( x^{T}v\right) \left( y^{T}u\right). \end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div><p> □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar123">Theorem 8.17</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2 <i>be a natural number. Then the function</i> </p><div id="Equva" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) =&amp;\ln \left[ \exp \left( \underset{i=1}{ \overset{n}{\sum }}x_{i}\right) -\left( \underset{i=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }} e^{x_{i}}\right) +n-1\right] ,\\ &amp;\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) \in \left( 0,\infty \right) ^{n} \end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div>

              <p>
                <i>is concave.</i>
              </p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\phi :\left ( 0,\infty \right ) \rightarrow \mathbb {R} \)</span> be a two times differentiable function and </p><div id="Equvb" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) =\phi \left( \underset{i=1}{\overset{n}{ \sum }}x_{i}\right) -\left( \underset{i=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }}\phi \left( x_{i}\right) \right) ,\ \left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) \in \left( 0,\infty \right) ^{n} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Suppose that <i>u</i> &gt; 0 on <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}\)</span> and <i>ϕ</i>
<sup><i>′′</i></sup> &gt; 0 on <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( 0,\infty \right ) .\)</span> Note that </p><div id="Equvc" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \frac{\partial u}{\partial x_{i}}\left( x\right) =\phi ^{\prime }\left( \underset{r=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }}x_{r}\right) -\phi ^{\prime }\left( x_{i}\right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equvd" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \frac{\partial ^{2}u}{\partial x_{i}^{2}}\left( x\right) =\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( \underset{r=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }}x_{r}\right) -\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( x_{i}\right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equve" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \frac{\partial ^{2}u}{\partial x_{i}\partial x_{j}}\left( x\right) =\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( \underset{r=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }}x_{r}\right). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(A\left ( x\right ) =\left ( a_{ij}\left ( x\right ) \right ) _{1\leq i,j\leq n}\)</span> be the Jacobian matrix of <span class="mathjax-tex">\(g=\ln \left ( u\right ) .\)</span> Denote <span class="mathjax-tex">\(s=\underset { r=1}{\overset {n}{\sum }}x_{r}\)</span>. Then </p><div id="Equvf" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} a_{ij}\left( x\right) =\left( \phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( s\right) -\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( x_{i}\right) \right) \delta _{ij}u\left( x\right) -\left( \phi ^{\prime }\left( s\right) -\phi ^{\prime }\left( x_{i}\right) \right) \left( \phi ^{\prime }\left( s\right) -\phi ^{\prime }\left( x_{j}\right) \right), \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> where we have denoted by <i>δ</i>
<sub><i>ij</i></sub> the Kronecker symbol. Note that <i>g</i> is concave if and only if the matrix </p><div id="Equvg" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left( \frac{a_{ij}\left( x\right) }{u^{2}\left( x\right) }\right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> is negative semidefinite. Let </p><div id="Equvh" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} b_{ij}\left( x\right) =\delta _{ij}-\frac{\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( s\right) }{\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( x_{i}\right) }+\frac{\left( \phi ^{\prime }\left( s\right) -\phi ^{\prime }\left( x_{i}\right) \right) \left( \phi ^{\prime }\left( s\right) -\phi ^{\prime }\left( x_{j}\right) \right) }{ u\left( x\right) \phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( x_{i}\right) }. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that <i>g</i> is concave if and only if the matrix <span class="mathjax-tex">\(B\left ( x\right ) =\left ( b_{ij}\left ( x\right ) \right ) _{1\leq i,j\leq n}\)</span> is positive semidefinite. For every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(m\in \left \{ 1,2,\ldots ,n\right \} \)</span> consider the matrix </p><div id="Equvi" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} B_{m}\left( x\right) =\left( b_{ij}\left( x\right) \right) _{1\leq i,j\leq m} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that <i>g</i> is concave if and only for every <span class="mathjax-tex">\(m\in \left \{ 1,2,\ldots ,n\right \} \)</span> we have <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\det \left ( B_{m}\left ( x\right ) \right ) \geq 0\)</span>
</p>
              <p>Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(m\in \left \{ 1,2,\ldots ,n\right \} .\)</span> Consider the <i>m</i>-dimensional vectors </p><div id="Equvj" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \alpha =\left( \begin{array}{c} \frac{\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( s\right) }{\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( x_{1}\right) } \\ . \\ . \\ . \\ \frac{\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( s\right) }{\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( x_{m}\right) } \end{array} \right) ,\ \ \beta =\left( \begin{array}{c} -1 \\ . \\ . \\ . \\ -1 \end{array} \right) ,\ \ \gamma =\left( \begin{array}{c} \frac{\phi ^{\prime }\left( s\right) -\phi ^{\prime }\left( x_{1}\right) }{ u\left( x\right) \phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( x_{1}\right) } \\ . \\ . \\ . \\ \frac{\phi ^{\prime }\left( s\right) -\phi ^{\prime }\left( x_{m}\right) }{ u\left( x\right) \phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( x_{m}\right) } \end{array} \right), \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equvk" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \delta =\left( \begin{array}{c} \phi ^{\prime }\left( s\right) -\phi ^{\prime }\left( x_{1}\right) \\ . \\ . \\ . \\ \phi ^{\prime }\left( s\right) -\phi ^{\prime }\left( x_{m}\right) \end{array} \right). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> By the preceding lemma we obtain </p><div id="Equvl" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \det \left( B_{m}\left( x\right) \right) {=}\det \left( I_{m}{+}\alpha \beta ^{T}{+}\gamma \delta ^{T}\right) {=} \left( 1+\alpha ^{T}\beta \right) \left( 1+\gamma ^{T}\delta \right) -\left( \alpha ^{T}\delta \right) \left( \beta ^{T}\gamma \right) = \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equvm" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} 1-\underset{i=1}{\overset{m}{\sum }}\frac{\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( s\right) }{\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( x_{i}\right) }+\underset{i=1}{ \overset{m}{\sum }}\frac{\left( \phi ^{\prime }\left( s\right) -\phi ^{\prime }\left( x_{i}\right) \right) ^{2}}{u\left( x\right) \phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( x_{i}\right) }- \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equvn" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left( \underset{i=1}{\overset{m}{\sum }}\frac{\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( s\right) }{\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( x_{i}\right) }\right) \left( \underset{i=1}{\overset{m}{\sum }}\frac{\left( \phi ^{\prime }\left( s\right) -\phi ^{\prime }\left( x_{i}\right) \right) ^{2}}{u\left( x\right) \phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( x_{i}\right) }\right) + \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equvo" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left( \underset{i=1}{\overset{m}{\sum }}\frac{\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( s\right) \left( \phi ^{\prime }\left( s\right) -\phi ^{\prime }\left( x_{i}\right) \right) }{\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( x_{i}\right) }\right) \left( \underset{i=1}{\overset{m}{\sum }}\frac{\phi ^{\prime }\left( s\right) -\phi ^{\prime }\left( x_{i}\right) }{u\left( x\right) \phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( x_{i}\right) }\right). \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that </p><div id="Equvp" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u\left( x\right) \det \left( B_{m}\left( x\right) \right) =u\left( x\right) \left( 1-\underset{i=1}{\overset{m}{\sum }}\frac{\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( s\right) }{\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( x_{i}\right) }\right) + \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equvq" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} +\underset{i=1}{\overset{m}{\sum }}\frac{\left( \phi ^{\prime }\left( s\right) -\phi ^{\prime }\left( x_{i}\right) \right) ^{2}}{\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( x_{i}\right) }-\left( \underset{i=1}{\overset{m}{\sum }}\frac{ \phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( s\right) }{\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( x_{i}\right) }\right) \left( \underset{i=1}{\overset{m}{\sum }}\frac{\left( \phi ^{\prime }\left( s\right) -\phi ^{\prime }\left( x_{i}\right) \right) ^{2}}{\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( x_{i}\right) }\right) + \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equvr" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} +\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( s\right) \left( \underset{i=1}{\overset{m}{ \sum }}\frac{\phi ^{\prime }\left( s\right) -\phi ^{\prime }\left( x_{i}\right) }{\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( x_{i}\right) }\right) ^{2}= \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equvs" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =\left( 1-\underset{i=1}{\overset{m}{\sum }}\frac{\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( s\right) }{\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( x_{i}\right) }\right) \left( u\left( x\right) +\underset{i=1}{\overset{m}{\sum }}\frac{\left( \phi ^{\prime }\left( s\right) -\phi ^{\prime }\left( x_{i}\right) \right) ^{2}}{ \phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( x_{i}\right) }\right) + \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equvt" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} +\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( s\right) \left( \underset{i=1}{\overset{m}{ \sum }}\frac{\phi ^{\prime }\left( s\right) -\phi ^{\prime }\left( x_{i}\right) }{\phi ^{\prime \prime }\left( x_{i}\right) }\right) ^{2}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Let <span class="mathjax-tex">\(p_{m}=\underset {i=1}{\overset {m}{\sum }}e^{-x_{i}},q_{r}=\underset {i=1}{ \overset {r}{\sum }}e^{x_{i}},r\in \left \{ 1,2,\ldots ,m\right \} ,c_{m}=q_{n}-q_{m}.\)</span> If <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\phi \left ( t\right ) =e^{t}-1,\)</span> where <span class="mathjax-tex">\(t\in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) \)</span>, then </p><div id="Equvu" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u\left( x\right) \det \left( B_{m}\left( x\right) \right) =\left( 1-e^{s}p_{m}\right) \left[ e^{s}-q_{n}+n-1+\underset{i=1}{\overset{m}{\sum }} \frac{\left( e^{s}-e^{x_{i}}\right) ^{2}}{e^{x_{i}}}\right] + \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equvv" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} +e^{s}\left( \underset{i=1}{\overset{m}{\sum }}\frac{e^{s}-e^{x_{i}}}{ e^{x_{i}}}\right) ^{2}= \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equvw" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =\left( 1-e^{s}p_{m}\right) \left( e^{s}-q_{n}+n-1+e^{2s}p_{m}-2me^{s}+q_{m}\right) +e^{s}\left( p_{m}e^{s}-m\right) ^{2}= \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equvx" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =e^{s}\left[ \left( m-1\right) ^{2}-p_{m}\left( n-1-c_{m}\right) +e^{-s}\left( n-1-c_{m}\right) \right]. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Note that </p><div id="Equvy" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} c_{m}-n+1\geq n-m-n+1=1-m, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equvz" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} 0\leq p_{m}-e^{-s}\leq m-1, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equwa" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left( p_{m}-e^{-s}\right) \left( c_{m}-n+1\right) \geq \left( p_{m}-e^{-s}\right) \left( 1-m\right), \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> hence </p><div id="Equwb" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} e^{-s}u\left( x\right) \det \left( B_{m}\left( x\right) \right) =\left( m-1\right) ^{2}+\left( p_{m}-e^{-s}\right) \left( c_{m}-n+1\right) \geq \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equwc" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \geq \left( m-1\right) ^{2}+\left( p_{m}-e^{-s}\right) \left( 1-m\right) =\left( m-1\right) \left( m-1-p_{m}+e^{-s}\right) \geq 0. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> We proved that <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\det \left ( B_{m}\left ( x\right ) \right ) \geq 0\)</span> for every <span class="mathjax-tex">\( m\in \left \{ 1,2,\ldots ,n\right \} .\)</span> Thus the function <i>g</i> = <i>f</i> is concave. □</p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar124">Corollary 8.18</h3>
              <p><i>Let a</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub>, <i>b</i>
<sub><i>i</i></sub> ∈ [1, <i>∞</i>), <i>i </i>= 1, 2, …, <i>n</i>. <i>Then the following inequality holds:</i> </p><div id="Equwd" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left[ a_{1}^{2}a_{2}^{2}\ldots a_{n}^{2}-\left( \underset{i=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }}a_{i}^{2}\right) +n-1\right] \cdot \left[ b_{1}^{2}b_{2}^{2}\ldots b_{n}^{2}- \left( \underset{i=1}{\overset{n}{\sum }}b_{i}^{2}\right) +n-1\right] \leq \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              
                <div id="Equwe" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \leq \left( a_{1}a_{2}\ldots a_{n}b_{1}b_{2}\ldots b_{n}-\left( \underset{i=1}{ \overset{n}{\sum }}a_{i}b_{i}\right) +n-1\right) ^{2}. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar125">Theorem 8.19</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2 <i>be a natural number. For i</i>, <i>k </i>≥ 0 <i>natural numbers consider the functions</i> </p><div id="Equwf" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} q_{i,n}\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) =\frac{\left( \overset{n}{ \underset{r=1}{\sum }}x_{r}\right) ^{i}-\left( \overset{n}{\underset{r=1}{ \sum }}x_{r}^{i}\right) }{i!}, \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              
                <div id="Equwg" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} u_{k,n}\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) =\exp \left( \overset{n}{\underset {r=1}{\sum }}x_{r}\right) -\left( \overset{n}{\underset{r=1}{\sum }} e^{x_{r}}\right) -\overset{k}{\underset{i=0}{\sum }}q_{i,n}\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right), \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>
              
              <p><i>where</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}\)</span><i>. Then for every natural number k </i>≥ 0 <i>the function</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(f_{k,n}=\ln \left ( u_{k,n}\right ) \)</span> <i>is Schur concave on</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}.\)</span></p>
            
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading">Proof</h3>
              <p>Note that we have <span class="mathjax-tex">\(q_{0,n}\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) =1-n\)</span>, <span class="mathjax-tex">\( q_{1,n}\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) =0,\)</span> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(q_{2,n}\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) =\underset {1\leq i&lt;j\leq n}{\sum }x_{i}x_{j}.\)</span> If <i>k</i> = 0, then </p><div id="Equwh" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} &amp;f_{0,n}\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) =\ln \left( \exp \left( \overset{n }{\underset{r=1}{\sum }}x_{r}\right) -\left( \overset{n}{\underset{r=1}{\sum }}e^{x_{r}}\right) +n-1\right) ,\ \ \\ &amp;\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right). \in \left( 0,\infty \right) ^{n} \end{aligned} $$
</span></div></div><p> Note that </p><div id="Equwi" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left( x_{1}-x_{2}\right) \left( \frac{\partial f_{0,n}}{\partial x_{1}} \left( \mathbf{x}\right) -\frac{\partial f_{0,n}}{\partial x_{2}}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) \right) = \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equwj" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =\frac{\left( x_{1}-x_{2}\right) }{u_{0,n}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) }\left( \frac{\partial u_{0,n}}{\partial x_{1}}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) -\frac{ \partial u_{0,n}}{\partial x_{2}}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) \right) = \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div> <div id="Equwk" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} =-\frac{\left( x_{1}-x_{2}\right) }{u_{0,n}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) }\left( e^{x_{1}}-e^{x_{2}}\right) \leq 0 \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> hence <i>f</i>
<sub>0,<i>n</i></sub> is Schur concave. If <i>k</i> ≥ 1 is a natural number, then </p><div id="Equwl" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \frac{\partial u_{k,n}}{\partial x_{j}}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) =\exp \left( \overset{n}{\underset{r=1}{\sum }}x_{r}\right) -e^{x_{j}}-\overset{k-1}{ \underset{i=0}{\sum }}\frac{\left( \overset{n}{\underset{r=1}{\sum }} x_{r}\right) ^{i}-x_{j}^{i}}{i!} \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> hence if we denote </p><div id="Equwm" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} w\left( t\right) =e^{t}-\overset{k-1}{\underset{i=0}{\sum }}\frac{t^{i}}{i!} ,\ \ t\in \left( 0,\infty \right) \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> we have </p><div id="Equwn" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} \left( x_{1}-x_{2}\right) \left( \frac{\partial f_{k,n}}{\partial x_{1}} \left( \mathbf{x}\right) -\frac{\partial f_{k,n}}{\partial x_{2}}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) \right) = -\frac{\left( x_{1}-x_{2}\right) }{u_{k,n}\left( \mathbf{x}\right) }\left[ w\left( x_{1}\right) -w\left( x_{2}\right) \right] \leq 0. \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div><p> Consequently, <i>f</i>
<sub><i>k</i>,<i>n</i></sub> is Schur concave on <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}.\)</span> □</p>
            <p>We conclude this section with the following conjecture.</p>
              <h3 class="c-article__sub-heading" id="FPar126">Conjecture B</h3>
              <p><i>Let n </i>≥ 2 <i>be a natural number. Then for every natural number k </i>≥ 0 <i>the function</i> </p><div id="Equwo" class="c-article-equation"><div class="c-article-equation__content"><span class="mathjax-tex">
$$\displaystyle \begin{aligned} f_{k,n}\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) {=}\ln \left( \exp \left( \overset{n }{\underset{r{=}1}{\sum }}x_{r}\right) {-}\left( \overset{n}{\underset{r{=}1}{\sum }}e^{x_{r}}\right) {-}\overset{k}{\underset{i=0}{\sum }}q_{i,n}\left( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\right) \right) , \end{aligned}$$
</span></div></div>

              <p><i>where</i> <span class="mathjax-tex">\(\left ( x_{1},x_{2},\ldots ,x_{n}\right ) \in \left ( 0,\infty \right ) ^{n}\)</span> <i>is concave. Here the functions q</i>
<sub><i>i</i>,<i>n</i></sub> <i>are defined in the statement of Theorem</i> <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar125">8.19</a>.</p>
            <p>Recall that until now we proved the following results: </p><ol class="u-list-style-none">
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">(i)</span>

<p><i>f</i>
<sub>2,<i>n</i></sub> is separately concave (cf. Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar118">8.13</a>);</p>

</li>
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">(ii)</span>

<p><i>f</i>
<sub>0,<i>n</i></sub> is concave (cf. Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar123">8.17</a>);</p>

</li>
<li>
<span class="u-custom-list-number">(iii)</span>

<p><i>f</i>
<sub><i>k</i>,<i>n</i></sub> is Schur concave (cf. Theorem <a data-track="click" data-track-label="link" data-track-action="subsection anchor" href="#FPar125">8.19</a>).</p>

</li>
</ol>
</div></div></section>
                        </div>
                    
                

                <div id="MagazineFulltextChapterBodySuffix"><section aria-labelledby="Bib1" data-title="References"><div class="c-article-section" id="Bib1-section"><h2 id="Bib1" class="c-article-section__title js-section-title js-c-reading-companion-sections-item"><span class="c-article-section__title-number"> </span>References</h2><div class="c-article-section__content" id="Bib1-content"><div data-container-section="references"><ol class="c-article-references" data-track-component="outbound reference" data-track-context="references section"><li class="c-article-references__item js-c-reading-companion-references-item" data-counter="1."><p class="c-article-references__text" id="ref-CR1">M. Aguiar, C. Andre, C. Benedetti, N. Bergeron, Z. Chen, P. Diaconis, A. Hendrickson, S. Hsiao, I.M. Isaacs, A. Jedwab, et al., Supercharacters, symmetric functions in noncommuting variables, and related Hopf algebras. Adv. Math. <b>229</b>(4), 2310–2337 (2012)</p><p class="c-article-references__links u-hide-print" id="ref-CR1-links"><a data-track="click_references" rel="nofollow noopener" data-track-label="10.1016/j.aim.2011.12.024" data-track-item_id="10.1016/j.aim.2011.12.024" data-track-action="Article reference" data-track-value="Article reference" href="https://doi.org/10.1016%2Fj.aim.2011.12.024" aria-label="Article reference 1" data-doi="10.1016/j.aim.2011.12.024">Article</a>&nbsp;
